Ceviri.ekle("en","ekokuma",{
"mimari-sultanahmet":{"baslik": ["The Sultan Ahmed Mosque — the first imperial mosque with six minarets", "89906298"], "metin": ["The Sultan Ahmed Mosque was begun on the order of Ahmed I in 1609 at the At Meydanı (Hippodrome) and completed in 1620; its architect was Sedefkâr Mehmed Agha, who had trained under the architect Sinan. The building follows the arrangement of the classical Ottoman mosque scheme with an arcaded courtyard: the central dome, about 22.4 metres in diameter, rests on four great piers and is supported on four sides by a semi-dome each, and these semi-domes are extended by three exedrae each. In an arrangement that had not been tried until then, six minarets were built — the TDV does not confirm the widespread story that this led to a claim of 'equality' with the Kaaba; it only records that it was 'an arrangement not tried until that time'.\n\nCut küfeki stone (a local limestone) was used as the predominant material of the building; the interior is furnished with more than 21,000 Iznik and Kütahya tiles and with decoration in marble, wood and metal. Dating from the first quarter of the 17th century, the mosque represents the final phase of classical Ottoman architecture, while baroque motifs are also encountered in some of its parts — the first traces of the approaching change of style.\n\nA separate note: the mosque's founder, Ahmed I, had it built directly out of the treasury, without its being based on a victory of conquest; this became a subject of criticism in some circles of the period, because the great imperial mosques were traditionally built to commemorate a victory.", "aa63b1a5"], "kisa": ["The first imperial mosque with six minarets — and the first to be built from the treasury, without a victory of conquest.", "e89c1d2c"]},
"mimari-topkapi-sarayi":{"baslik": ["Topkapı Palace — buildings added over four centuries", "6425580e"], "metin": ["Topkapı Palace was built at the tip of the historic peninsula, on a site of about 700 dönüms that looks at once toward the Sea of Marmara, Üsküdar and the Golden Horn. The work, which began after the conquest with garden layouts and pavilions at Sarayburnu (Seraglio Point), then called Zeytinlik (the Olive Grove), continued with the Sûr-ı Sultânî, the imperial walls that separated the area from the city. The sources give differing dates for its beginning and completion. Because the main gate, the Bâb-ı Hümâyun (Imperial Gate), bears the date 883 (1478), the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam accepts that construction started before then, probably in 1465. Following the terrain, the complex grows horizontally; its interconnected parts received new additions in almost every period until the middle of the nineteenth century.\n\nGATES AND COURTYARDS. The palace wall, which abuts the Byzantine walls, has three great gates (the Bâb-ı Hümâyun, the Demir Kapı or Iron Gate, and the Otluk Kapısı) and five service (side) gates. The Bâb-ı Hümâyun is a two-storey pavilion gate, and its inscription bears the signature of Ali b. Yahya al-Sufi. Its upper storey was demolished after suffering damage from fire and earthquake in the second half of the nineteenth century. The palace takes shape around three courtyards and three monumental gates (the Bâb-ı Hümâyun, the Bâbüsselâm or Gate of Salutation, and the Bâbüssaâde or Gate of Felicity).\n\nIn the first courtyard, the Church of Hagia Irene, surviving from Byzantine times, was used as an armoury. The Deâvî Kasrı, where petitions were handed in, was a small octagonal pavilion with a tower. The council hall (divanhane, the Kubbealtı) in the second courtyard was built in the time of Mehmed the Conqueror. When İbrahim Pasha, grand vizier of Suleiman the Lawgiver, renovated it, he had the building brought somewhat forward. The base of the Tower of Justice, which rises just behind it, dates from the earliest years; its present appearance, however, belongs largely to the reign of Mahmud II. After the fire of 1574, the architect Sinan rebuilt the kitchens on the right side of the courtyard while preserving the main structure. This section, recognisable from afar by its conical domes and chimneys, consists of nine hearths and a confectionery kitchen (helvahane).\n\nThe Audience Chamber (Arz Odası) in the third courtyard was also built in the Conqueror's time and was later renovated many times; its base is thought to survive from the fifteenth century. A portico of marble columns runs around it beneath the eaves. The Enderun Library behind it is an eighteenth-century building: it stands on a high basement storey, is domed in the centre, has three iwans and is decorated with tiles.\n\nTHE HAREM AND THE PAVILIONS. The Harem is a separate section on the Golden Horn side of the second and third courtyards. It reached its present size mainly during the long reign of Suleiman the Lawgiver. From the end of the sixteenth century to the middle of the nineteenth, rooms and pavilions were added as the need arose; this accounts for the section's unplanned, tangled structure. The principal buildings of the 'fourth place', which took shape later behind the Enderun courtyard, are the Revan and Baghdad pavilions built by Murad IV. The Sofa Pavilion, thought to have been built in the eighteenth century, is also here. The last pavilion built in the palace is Abdülmecid's Mecidiye Pavilion.\n\nIn the decoration, alongside the woodwork, tiles of various periods from the fifteenth century onward stand out. The façade of the Circumcision Room and the tiles of the Audience Chamber, the Chamber of the Sacred Mantle (Hırka-i Saâdet) and the Harem turn the palace, in the words of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, into a veritable museum of tiles.\n\nTHE TILED KIOSK: ONE OF THE PALACE'S OLDEST BUILDINGS. The kiosk stands inside the Sûr-ı Sultânî but outside the palace's actual precincts, on the slope descending to the Golden Horn. Its rear façade rests on an artificial terrace formed by arched and vaulted substructures surviving from Byzantine times. According to its inscription, it was completed in the Conqueror's reign in 877 (September 1472); its architect is unknown.\n\nWhite küfeki limestone was used in the two-storey stone building, and red brick infill on the side and rear walls gives it a colourful appearance. The main floor is laid out on a scheme of four iwans opening onto a central space. The interlaced ribs at the transition to the vaults and domes show that the building tradition of Central Asia and the Seljuks lived on. The five-sided domed room projecting toward the Golden Horn commands a view of the Golden Horn and the entrance to the Bosphorus. The tiles on the façade and in the great iwan are executed in the mosaic technique.\n\nSTYLE. Semavi Eyice regards the kiosk as the only example still standing in Istanbul of an old Turkish conception of building whose roots reach back to Central Asia and which passed through Iran to arrive in Anatolia. It translates into stone, and onto a monumental scale, the plan of the traditional Turkish house, with a central hall (sofa) and four rooms around it. Tursun Bey, a historian of the Conqueror's time, likewise records that the kiosk was built in the old style, and that a second pavilion in the Ottoman manner was constructed facing it.\n\nThe repairs made after the fire of 19 November 1737 changed the façade considerably. The column capitals of the present front portico do not conform to the style of the Conqueror's period. Eyice surmises that this repair was carried out in the reign of Mahmud I, when influences foreign to Turkish architecture began to enter it. By an official memorandum of 1873 the kiosk was assigned to the Museum of Antiquities (Âsâr-ı Atîka Müzesi), and around 1875 it was converted into a museum.\n\nNote: For the later additions to the palace, the sources use no stylistic label such as Baroque or Empire; the stylistic assessment above applies only to the Tiled Kiosk.", "58bc1283"], "kisa": ["Not a single building but an ensemble of structures added over four centuries. Its oldest part, the Tiled Kiosk of 1472, carries on the building tradition of Central Asia.", "239b194e"]},
"mimari-uc-serefeli-camii":{"baslik": ["Üç Şerefeli Mosque — the first centrally planned Ottoman mosque", "0fbe5f13"], "metin": ["Construction. This small complex, built by Murad II, consists of a mosque, the Saatli (Clock) Madrasa, a school, a sebil (public water kiosk), a fountain and a burial ground. The mosque is accepted to have been built between 841 and 851 (1437-1447) by the architect Muslihuddin and the master Şehabeddin. In the second half of the fifteenth century the Peykler Madrasa was added to the complex.\n\nPLAN and dimensions. The building occupies a nearly square rectangular area: the prayer hall, which extends in width, is 60.41 × 23.90 m, and the porticoed courtyard 60.70 × 35.50 m. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam considers it the first example of a centrally planned mosque in Ottoman architecture and attaches particular importance to its porticoed courtyard with an ablution fountain. The middle of the prayer hall is covered by a main dome 24.10 m in diameter; on each side are two domes 10.50 m in diameter, and in the triangles left between the main dome and the side domes are four small cupolas with muqarnas. The main dome, which rests on a drum that has twenty-four sides on the exterior, is carried by six broad arches of cut stone: the two arches on the east and west are free-standing, while the four on the north and south come down onto six piers embedded in the walls.\n\nTHE COURTYARD. Along the wall of the prayer hall, in the porticoed courtyard of twenty-two bays, stretches the seven-bay late-comers' portico (son cemaat yeri); one of the bays is covered by a cloister vault, the others by domes with polygonal drums and pendentives. The portico columns are mostly of marble, but in the north wing there are also columns of green Euboean (Eğriboz) stone and of granite. The courtyard walls have two rows of windows. The west gate, facing the market, is of white marble and is the most imposing; the north gate is of küfeki limestone and plainer, and the east gate is the most modest.\n\nTHE MINARETS. The decoration of each of the four minarets is different. The three-balconied minaret that gives the mosque its name stands at the northwest of the prayer hall: it is 6 m in diameter and 67.75 m tall, and with its cap it reaches 76 m. Inside it are three separate staircases — one leads up to the first and second balconies, one to the second and third, and one to the third balcony only. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, it is also important as the tallest minaret of its time. The two-balconied 'lozenge-patterned minaret' (baklavalı minare) at the northeast of the prayer hall was built by Mehmed the Conqueror when the Peykler Madrasa was being constructed; its shaft has diagonal squares of red stone and küfeki limestone. The single-balconied 'spiral minaret' (burmalı minare) at the northwest corner of the courtyard takes its name from the thick red-and-white mouldings that wind around its shaft like a helix; according to the encyclopaedia, it was built during Mustafa III's repairs after an earthquake.\n\nREPAIRS. Damaged in the earthquake of 1762, the mosque was repaired by Mustafa III in 1763-64; the inscription recording this repair is on the spandrels of the arch on the axis of the main portal in the late-comers' portico. The royal tribune in the southeast corner of the prayer hall, estimated to have been added in the seventeenth century, was removed in 1935. The mosque has also undergone a thorough repair in recent times.", "90837b27"], "kisa": ["The Ottomans' first centrally planned mosque: a 24-metre dome, a porticoed courtyard with four minarets unlike one another at its corners, and the tallest minaret of its time — with three balconies reached by three separate staircases.", "a7db4694"]},
"mimari-yeni-cami":{"baslik": ["Yeni Cami (New Mosque) — a mosque completed after 67 years", "16f45fde"], "metin": ["CONSTRUCTION: THREE ARCHITECTS, TWO QUEEN MOTHERS. The patron of the complex, which stands at Eminönü facing the harbour and commercial district, was Safiye Sultan, mother of Mehmed III. The architect Davud Agha began construction on 9 April 1598 (3 Ramadan 1006). The ground on the shore was strengthened by driving piles whose tips were bound with lead bands, and the foundation was laid with stone blocks; stone for the building was brought from Rhodes. Davud Agha died in September 1598, when he had raised the structure about 75 cm (1 zira) above the ground. Dalgıç Ahmed Agha, the Superintendent of Waterways, who was brought in to replace him, carried the walls up to above the first row of windows. When Mehmed III died in 1603, Safiye Sultan was sent to the Old Palace and the construction was left unfinished.\n\nAfter the great Istanbul fire of 1660, the Queen Mother (Valide Sultan) Hatice Turhan decided to complete the half-finished mosque. In 1661, under the responsibility of the chief architect Mustafa Agha, one course of stone was removed from the unfinished walls and construction resumed. The mosque was opened for worship with the Friday prayer held on 30 October 1665 (20 Rabi al-Akhir 1076). The endowment deed and the inscriptions, however, give the date 1074 (1663-64).\n\nTHE COMPLEX. The complex comprises the mosque, the royal pavilion (hünkâr kasrı), an arasta or market street (today's Egyptian Bazaar, also known as the Spice Bazaar), a tomb, a school for Quran reciters (darülkurra), a sebil (public water kiosk), a fountain and a primary school. A madrasa and a soup kitchen are also mentioned in connection with the first phase of construction, but they were never built. The arasta has been known as the Egyptian Bazaar since the mid-eighteenth century. In the L-shaped bazaar two vaulted streets meet at a prayer square; it contains eighty-eight shops in all. From the three-storey royal pavilion a long ramped passage leads to the sultan's tribune.\n\nPLAN AND DIMENSIONS. The mosque is a centrally planned building that continues the classical Ottoman scheme with a porticoed courtyard. The prayer hall, measuring 35.50 × 40.90 metres, is covered by a dome 16.20 metres in diameter. The main dome is extended by half-domes in four directions. Two exedrae are added to the sides of each half-dome except the northern one; the northern half-dome is extended one stage further with three exedrae, lengthening the space along the qibla direction. The four great piers rise on the exterior to the base of the dome as weight towers with octagonal shafts. The building receives light through six rows of windows. It has two minarets with three balconies each.\n\nMATERIALS. Dressed küfeki limestone was used in the main walls and minarets, marble in the gallery and portico façades and in the door and window openings, and brick in the roofing system. On the side façades, galleries with alternating large and small arches, together with the use of two-coloured stone, create a lively appearance.\n\nDECORATION AND STYLE (TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam). Up to above the second row of windows the walls are covered with underglaze tiles, in which blue, turquoise and green predominate. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam notes that the decline in tile technique seen in the seventeenth century is easily noticeable in these tiles. Classical designs were used in the renewed painted decoration. The large petition window (hacet penceresi) in the tomb courtyard, however, was made in the Baroque style in the nineteenth century.\n\nLATER HISTORY. In the second half of the nineteenth century, traffic increased with the building of the Galata Bridge, and the mosque's outer courtyard was demolished along with the shops around it. When the primary school was also pulled down in 1904, the integrity of the complex was lost.", "ea5afbd9"], "kisa": ["A mosque whose foundation was laid in 1598 and which opened for worship 67 years later. The ground on the shore was strengthened with lead-banded piles; the plan carries the classical scheme on into the seventeenth century, and the complex draws its income from the Egyptian Bazaar.", "5ee4cf21"]},
"misir1805-tarih-anlatisi":{"baslik": ["May 1805: how is Cairo's overthrow of a governor told in Egyptian historiography?", "79c8597c"], "metin": ["The event, day by day (from the body text of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam). The encyclopaedia's article `hursid-ahmed-pasa` (Cevdet Küçük) narrates the event from the side of the governor, Hurşid Pasha. The Sublime Porte has appointed Mehmed Ali governor of Jeddah, and, in the article's words, \"Hurşid Pasha, who announced that Mehmed Ali Pasha had been appointed governor of Jeddah, invested him with a robe of honour (10 May 1805).\" But the ceremony backfires: \"However, as he was returning to his residence after the ceremony, he was met with hostile demonstrations by Mehmed Ali Pasha's soldiers. The people and soldiers who poured into the streets also demonstrated in favour of Mehmed Ali Pasha.\" Immediately afterwards the ulema step in: \"The ulema and sheikhs of Egypt asked Hurşid Pasha to give up his post.\" Hurşid's reply is the core of the quarrel between narratives that is the real subject of this card: \"Hurşid Pasha, for his part, declared that he would not abandon, at the demand of a few fellahin, the post to which he had come by order of the sultan, and that he would not leave the citadel until a written order arrived from the sultan.\" The result: \"The soldiers and the people laid siege to the citadel.\" The centre recognises the fait accompli only weeks later — the decision of the Sublime Porte is dated 18 June 1805, the written order was \"read in Cairo in the presence of the ulema and the soldiers (9 July 1805)\", and Hurşid Pasha left Egypt on 6 August 1805.\n\nThe SAME event, in Istanbul's wording. The article `kavalali-mehmed-ali-pasa` in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam summarises the same process in a single sentence and in the language of the centre: \"After eliminating, through various political manoeuvres, Hüsrev, Tahir, Ali and Hurşid Pashas, the last governors of Egypt, he also won the support of the ulema, the notables and the people of Egypt, and was appointed governor by the Sublime Porte (3 July 1805).\" Note: the one doing the appointing is not Cairo but the Sublime Porte; the support of the ulema and the people is mentioned as a supporting factor, not as the appointing authority. It is precisely this sentence that the Egyptian narrative turns on its head — there it is Cairo that appoints, and Istanbul merely approves.\n\nThe framework of EGYPTIAN historiography. Why this event is regarded in Egypt as an opening scene is, before it is a matter of individual authors, a matter of framework. According to Fatma Zehra Beyaz's article examining Egyptian historiography, in the framework constructed in the twentieth century \"the classical Ottoman period will be deemed a dark age of Egypt, in a discourse borrowed from the Orientalists, and erased from history, and academic research will be confined to the period after Mehmed Ali Pasha.\" The same article describes the thesis rejected by Halil İbrahim Erol's book as follows: \"The thesis, dominant in Arab nationalism, that the classical Ottoman period is to be considered a 'dark age' for the Arab lands, and that the French occupation and the ensuing era of Mehmed Ali Pasha were a period of awakening and cultural enlightenment.\" Within such a framework, 1805 is not a change of Ottoman governor but the opening of a new age.\n\nAn EGYPTIAN historian's objection to his country's own narrative. One of those who have most forcefully opened the framework to debate is the Egyptian historian Khaled Fahmy. According to Halil İbrahim Erol's assessment, in his first book Fahmy \"was criticising the construct and claims that the Egyptian nationalist narrative sought to build around Mehmed Ali of Kavala\"; in his second book, too, he treats \"from a critical perspective the portrait of Mehmed Ali created in the nationalist construct, which he himself had also criticised\". The same assessment cites Abd al-Rahman al-Rafi'i (d. 1966) as an example of works supporting the nationalist construct and reports that Fahmy has \"argued\" that \"this situation continued to a great extent until the military coup of 1952, and partly thereafter\". The turning point is the archive: \"He draws attention to the fact that, with the opening of all the archival material to researchers from the 1970s onward, more comprehensive, higher-quality and critical studies began.\"\n\nA PEOPLE'S movement, or a tax revolt? The nature of the event is also debated. In objecting to Fahmy's reading of the two Cairo uprisings during the years of the French occupation (1798-1801) as a national resistance against occupation, Erol states his criterion as follows: \"The people of Cairo rebelled, along with the problems arising from the French manner of government, ultimately against the form of taxation\" — and he bases this on the assessments of the historian Abd al-Rahman al-Jabarti (d. 1240/1825), one of the ulema of al-Azhar, who witnessed the events at first hand. When this criterion is carried over to 1805, the shape of the question changes: was what Cairo did in 1805 under the leadership of the ulema a national/constitutional moment, or a familiar urban reaction against the pressure of taxes and soldiery? This card does not close that question: Erol's sentence concerns the uprisings of 1798-1801, and no source passing the same verdict on 1805 could be read.\n\nAND this site's own entry — corrected. The chronology on this site at first dated the event to 13 May 1805; that day does not occur in either of the two TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam articles whose body text was read as cited above, and two academic theses (Özkoç 2013, p. 66; Ertuğrul 2018, p. 52) likewise say only \"May 1805\". On 20 September 2026 the precision of the entry was reduced to the month (1805-05) and its citation was changed to real sources. The days given by the sources: the days given by the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam are 10 May (the robe of honour), 18 June (the decision of the Sublime Porte), 3 July (`kavalali-mehmed-ali-pasa`) / 9 July (the reading of the written order, `hursid-ahmed-pasa`) and 6 August (Hurşid's departure). The difference between 3 July and 9 July in the encyclopaedia's two articles has not been resolved either. So three separate days can be counted as three separate 'beginnings' — which one is chosen depends on who is telling the event: Cairo's day (May), the centre's decision (June) or the day the decree was read (July).", "b8ed6557"], "kisa": ["The overthrow of a governor in Cairo under pressure from the people and the ulema was long regarded in Egypt as the opening scene of the narrative of the 'birth of modern Egypt'. The critical historiography of the last forty years has opened this narrative to debate as a 'construct' — to use the Egyptian historian Khaled Fahmy's own term.", "9ed55e95"]},
"mora-despot-kardesler-1460":{"oneri": ["What delivered the Morea to the Ottomans was the hatred two brother despots bore each other; one a monk in Edirne…", "f1ee72f9"], "sebep.b": ["The despot brothers Thomas and Demetrios Palaiologos, who shared the Morea after 1449, constantly fighting each other, and Thomas openly refusing the tribute in 1458", "bd9424bc"], "sonuc.b": ["The Conqueror putting an end to the Despotate of the Morea in the campaign of 1460 by taking the whole peninsula except Venice's coastal fortresses", "d825ef35"], "bag": ["According to the TDV article on the Morea, following the refusal of tribute the Conqueror came with a great army in 1458, took the north of the peninsula (Patras, Vostitsa, Kalavryta, Corinth) and established Ottoman administration there. The quarrel between the brothers went on; according to the article on Mehmed II, Thomas, supported by the Westerners, gained control of the peninsula. In the spring of 1460, while Pope Pius II was preparing a Crusade, the sultan marched on the Morea again before an army could be assembled.", "80b8b7ec"], "metin": ["Here the paths of the two brothers parted. Demetrios, who chose to surrender, was left Lemnos, Imbros (Gökçeada) and Samothrace in the Aegean and Enez on the coast of Thrace; he spent the end of his life in retirement in a monastery and died in Edirne in 1470. Thomas, who put his trust in the West, took refuge in Italy, lived on a generous pension granted by the pope and died there in 1465. His son Manuel and other members of the Palaiologos family entered Ottoman service and became Muslims; the family was still in office even in the time of Bayezid II. But the expression \"the whole of the Morea\" is incomplete: Coron, Modon, Navarino, Nauplion (Anabolu) and Monemvasia remained with Venice. These were taken in the war of 1499-1502 and in the later wars that lasted until 1540; only then did the peninsula pass entirely to the Ottomans. According to the TDV, this was followed by a period of peace lasting more than two centuries; the tax registers (tahrir) show that up to the first quarter of the sixteenth century the population more than doubled, and the article describes the claim that the Greeks fled to the mountains from Turkish oppression as \"a historical fallacy\". The TDV dates the conquest only as \"the spring of 1460\".", "49e54601"]},
"murad4-revan-senligi-gecesi":{"soru": ["What happened during the celebrations of the Yerevan victory?", "cfaa47bd"], "baslik": ["The first night of the victory celebrations", "d0dc4d60"], "metin": ["When news of the conquest of Yerevan (Revan) reached Istanbul, celebrations began that were to last four nights. According to the TDV, Murad IV, worried that his mother was plotting against him, took advantage of the atmosphere created by the victory and had his brothers in Istanbul, Prince Bayezid and Prince Süleyman, killed (27 August 1635). The killing of the two princes, each about twenty-five years old, on the night the celebrations began aroused deep grief and anger among the people.", "1692f2de"], "not": ["The murders described belong to the first night of the celebrations that began when news of the conquest reached Istanbul.", "b0ff56bf"]},
"murad4-son-gunleri":{"soru": ["Where and how did Murad IV die?", "1d2117a3"], "baslik": ["His last breath in the room where he had had his brother strangled", "5f14f372"], "metin": ["According to the TDV, after retaking Baghdad and returning to Istanbul, Murad IV one evening, at the suggestion of his intimates, ate and drank as he used to; the next day he fell ill and, despite every treatment, grew worse day by day. The imam in his company, Şâmî Yûsuf Efendi, relates that he lost consciousness from time to time. The sultan died on a Thursday evening in February 1640, after the night prayer, in the room where he had had his brother Prince Kasım strangled before setting out on the Baghdad campaign; he was succeeded by his brother İbrahim.", "d5294ad7"], "not": ["The detail of the loss of consciousness rests on a single witness. The day of death appears in the sources as 8 or 9 February 1640.", "ec3623bc"]},
"murad4-tebdil-machiavelli-rivayeti":{"soru": ["Did Murad IV read Machiavelli?", "eae7f33d"], "baslik": ["The sultan in disguise and the Machiavelli story", "e9a56fac"], "metin": ["During the years of regency spent under the influence of his mother Kösem Sultan, Murad IV took a close interest in affairs of state; according to the TDV, he went about in disguise and tried to see the condition of the country with his own eyes. Some Western sources go further: they relate that the sultan kept spies everywhere and read Machiavelli, whom he had had translated by a convert to Islam. The encyclopaedia reports these last two pieces of information only as hearsay.", "f2184df3"]},
"murad5-kisa-saltanat-kacirma-1876":{"soru": ["Why was the reign of Murad V so short?", "185dd039"], "baslik": ["A sultan who jumped into a pool, rescuers dressed as women", "9d746185"], "metin": ["Murad V, whose ailment had long been known, was so shaken by what he experienced on his accession day while being taken to the Serasker's headquarters that the ceremony of allegiance was cut short. The mysterious death of his uncle Abdülaziz broke him down completely: at Yıldız Palace he threw himself into a pool, and one morning, after returning from a Friday procession (selamlık), he tried to take his own life by smashing window panes. To conceal the situation, the government spread the rumour that boils had broken out on the sultan's face and back; the ceremony of girding on the sword could not be held, and ambassadors could not present their credentials. When the medical board reported that the chance of recovery was very slight, he was deposed on 31 August 1876. During his 28 years under surveillance at Çırağan there were three attempts to abduct him: in one, four people were caught trying to enter the palace dressed as women; the last ended with the killing of Ali Suavi (20 May 1878).", "bcbe27ee"], "not": ["He is remembered as the Ottoman sultan who spent the shortest time on the throne; it is recorded that he joined the Freemasons in 1872, that he was fond of drink, and that he died of diabetes in 1904.", "624770f1"]},
"mustafa1-kizlar-agasi-cikaran-indiren-1618":{"soru": ["Why did the Chief Black Eunuch put Mustafa on the throne and then depose him?", "0f89cc9d"], "baslik": ["Ninety-six days apart: the same agha who enthroned him also deposed him", "ff03466a"], "metin": ["When Ahmed I died young in 1617, it was not his eldest son Osman who succeeded him but his brother Mustafa, whose mental health had long since been badly shaken. According to Peçuylu İbrahim, a historian of the period, the Chief Black Eunuch Mustafa Agha had a hand in this decision: the agha, looking to his own interests, influenced the accession decision by suggesting that the feeble-minded prince might recover in time. The hoped-for recovery never came. Peçuylu and Kâtib Çelebi relate that the new sultan went on sea excursions at all sorts of times, threw the gold coins he carried to the fish as food, distributed money at random, and pulled off the turbans of viziers who came into his presence, baring their heads. According to the historian Hasanbeyzâde, this time the same Mustafa Agha took action: he informed the deputy grand vizier (kaymakam) pasha and the Shaykh al-Islam of the sultan's condition and spread reports that unless it was stopped, he would empty the treasury, have the princes killed and wipe out the line of the dynasty. On 26 February 1618, at the end of a reign of ninety-six days, Mustafa was shut up in the room where he lived, and his nephew Osman II came to the throne. Topçular Kâtibi, who wrote about the same days, makes no mention at all of a deposition and says that the sultan withdrew of his own accord.", "536bc30f"], "not": ["That the agha played a role in both decisions emerges when the accounts of different historians are placed side by side; the interpretations of the agha's intentions belong to those historians.", "fd9478d6"]},
"mustafa1-kubbeyi-delen-asiler-1622":{"soru": ["How did the rebels get Mustafa out of the locked room?", "3c41b59e"], "baslik": ["Rebels who broke through a dome to bring out a sultan", "7e303205"], "metin": ["On 19 May 1622, when one of the rebels who had entered the palace mentioned the name of Mustafa I, the crowd proclaimed him sultan and rushed to look for him. It is stated that from inside the room whose door they could not open, Mustafa, whenever he heard his name, called out, 'If you want me, I want you too.' The rebels climbed onto the dome, cut through the lead sheeting with an axe, and three men who let themselves down by rope through the opening found him sitting on a cushion. According to Hüseyin Tûgī, a witness of the events, the former sultan, too weak to stay on a horse, was made to wait in his undergarments. According to what Peçuylu İbrahim reports from a witness, Mustafa, seated in the mihrab of the mosque of the Janissary companies, kept running to the window whenever a sound came from outside, while his mother loosened his hands from the grille and sat him back in his place. At noon the next day he was placed on the throne for the second time.", "8a72d119"], "not": ["The details of the scene are the account of the eyewitness Hüseyin Tûgī and the contemporary historian Peçuylu İbrahim.", "8f637b3a"]},
"nasil-abdulhamid1":{"baslik": ["What did you make of Abdülhamid I? A good-natured reformer, or the sultan who lost the Crimea?", "f16b3516"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nThe TDV article presents Abdülhamid I as a good-natured, compassionate and diligent sultan. Knowing that he had no experience in affairs of state, on taking the throne he left the grand vizier Muhsinzade Mehmed Pasha in his post and conducted business through him. Despite his political failures, he attached great importance to reform, especially to putting the army in order: he took a close interest in the drilling of the Bombardier (Humbaracı) and Artillery corps, tried to renew the fleet through Cezayirli Gazi Hasan Pasha, attached importance to the training of naval officers at the Imperial Naval Engineering School (Mühendishane-i Bahri) in 1775 and had the marines of the sailing fleet (kalyoncus) settled in barracks. During the grand vizierate of Halil Hamid Pasha (1782-1785) the Müteferrika printing press was put back into operation and history books were printed. Internal revolts were suppressed: Zahir al-Umar in Syria (1775), the Mamluks in Egypt, the disturbances in the Morea (1779) and the levends who had been causing turmoil in Anatolia for years. The soup kitchen and madrasa in Sirkeci and the complex he had built in Beylerbeyi in his mother's name are his most important works.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe disintegration of the army at Kozluca and Shumen (Şumnu) in the first months of his reign forced him to accept the Russian peace offer; with Küçük Kaynarca, signed on 21 July 1774, territory was lost in the north, above all Azov, the independence of the Crimea was recognised and Russia acquired the title of protector of the Orthodox. The Aynalıkavak Convention (Tenkihname) (1779) strengthened Russian influence still further. Against Potemkin's annexation of the Crimea in 1784, he could not decide on war because he knew the army was not ready. The spread of Wahhabism in Arabia could not be prevented. Finally, the war opened in 1787 went badly with the fall of Jassy, Khotyn and Ochakov (Özi); with the recall of the French experts, the innovations in the army also came to a standstill.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nThe TDV text does not separately report what contemporary chronicles or ambassadors said about the sultan's personality; the personal assessment belongs to the author of the article. The only political testimony the text gives is this: in 1785 he was in danger of being deposed through a plot by the Grand Vizier Halil Hamid Pasha. The author states openly whose hands the successes came from: able statesmen such as Karavezir Seyyid Mehmed Pasha, Halil Hamid Pasha, Koca Yusuf Pasha and Gazi Hasan Pasha. In the decision for the war of 1787, too, it is related that the grand vizier Koca Yusuf Pasha was decisive, with the intention of acting in accordance with the wishes of Britain and Prussia.\n\n■ WHAT DID YOU MAKE OF HIM\nThe general verdict of the author of the article, M. Münir Aktepe, is gentle: a sultan who throughout his fifteen-year reign was constantly occupied with internal and external problems, who achieved great success in reform thanks to able statesmen, and who established relations with the Muslim states in Morocco and India. Aktepe writes that he sensed early on the course of events in the Crimea and understood even then that Şahin Giray's khanate was the beginning of an occupation; Russia and Austria, meanwhile, were planning to divide the Ottoman lands between them. Thus Abdülhamid I appears as a sultan of transition who could not prevent the loss but saw it, and who prepared the ground for the reform of Selim III. His end, too, fits this picture: with his health already failing, he died of a stroke that came while he was reading the grand vizier's letter reporting the fall of Ochakov (Özi).", "66ccacbe"], "kisa": ["He took the throne at the age of forty-nine, without experience of state affairs, in the middle of a losing war. He initiated reform in the army and navy and put the printing press back into operation; but it was also he who signed Küçük Kaynarca and watched the Russian annexation of the Crimea.", "36d605fe"]},
"nasil-abdulhamid2":{"baslik": ["What did you make of Abdülhamid II? A master of balance who kept the state standing, or a suspicious ruler who shut himself up in Yıldız?", "6dca4ef1"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nAccording to the TDV article, Abdülhamid was a ruler of extraordinary intelligence and memory, strong-willed, steadfast in the face of danger, who listened much and spoke little and treated everyone courteously. He did not drink, led a simple life far from dissipation and reduced palace expenses to a minimum. Before important decisions he would have statesmen of differing views debate in his presence, and then decide. He did not like the shedding of blood: the death warrants he signed in thirty-three years do not exceed a few, and he even had stipends allotted to his opponents who had fled abroad. He commuted the death sentences passed on Midhat Pasha and his associates to life imprisonment. With the Decree of Muharrem (1881) he lightened the burden of the foreign debt. The schools of Civil Service (Mülkiye), Law, Fine Arts (Sanayi-i Nefise), Commerce and Halkalı Agriculture, and the Darülfünun (university), were opened in his reign. According to the author of the article, his most successful side was his foreign policy; in the palace he set up an information centre that gathered the world's publications and embassy reports.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same article describes his temperament as “cool-headed but apprehensive”; the suspiciousness in his character grew with what he went through. The depositions of the two preceding sultans made the suspicion that he would be removed from the throne a fixed idea with him; after the Çırağan raids he established a powerful secret police organisation and monopolised the government at Yıldız Palace. In 1878, when a deputy accused him in the assembly that was debating responsibility for the War of '93 (1877–1878), he said that he would follow “in the footsteps of Sultan Mahmud”, and on 13 February 1878 he suspended the Chamber of Deputies indefinitely; constitutional government remained on paper for thirty years. The Ottoman Public Debt Administration came to dominate the financial life of the state, great bribery and corruption were seen in the system of concessions, and the country was divided into zones of foreign economic influence.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nTDV reports Abdülhamid's defence, in his own words, of informing by secret reports (jurnals): in his view it was a shameful thing, but it was impossible to give it up, because nowhere had intrigue grown so great. The article writes that conservative statesmen such as Gazi Osman Pasha and Cevdet Pasha supported his concentrating the government in the palace. The harsh nicknames used in Western and opposition circles do not appear in the article. A turning point is also recorded: in the First World War the former sultan, whose opinion Talat and Enver Pashas sought, said that it was a great irresponsibility to join the land powers in declaring war on the sea powers; according to the article, his worth was better understood in this period.\n\n■ WHAT DID YOU MAKE OF HIM\nThe verdict of the author of the article, Cevdet Küçük, is openly favourable: Abdülhamid is a model of chastity, dignity and honour befitting the office of caliph, a pious and charitable man. Küçük attributes the harshness in domestic policy to external pressures and to the incidents that foreign states stirred up within the country; he writes that it was not possible to govern the state in a disintegrating empire without bringing interest groups under control. This is the apologetic reading of the TDV author; the article itself does not conceal the closing of the assembly or the secret police organisation either.", "3bd44241"], "kisa": ["It was he who opened the parliament and he who suspended it indefinitely; he who opened the schools and he who built the network of informers. During his reign many intellectuals wrote against him, and in his last years they began to write in his favour.", "4d6de23e"]},
"nasil-abdulmecid":{"baslik": ["What did you make of Abdülmecid? A reforming sultan, or the ambassadors' sultan?", "62b8a08e"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nAccording to the article in TDV, Abdülmecid was a sultan who had been brought up like a European prince, had learned French well enough to speak it and understand what he read, and followed European publications. He took the throne at sixteen, in the shadow of the rout at Nizip; knowing his own inexperience, he promised to listen to the advice of the senior statesmen. By approving the Gülhane Imperial Rescript (3 November 1839) prepared by Mustafa Reşid Pasha, he opened the Tanzimat; the Reform Edict of 1856 was also proclaimed in his name. In 1848 his refusal, despite the threat of war from Austria and Russia, to hand back the Hungarian refugees had a great resonance in Europe in favour of the Ottomans. He abandoned the tradition of sultans living shut away in the palace: he inspected barracks and the naval arsenal, attended school examinations, opened the Supreme Council (Meclis-i Vâlâ) every year with a speech, and made tours to Bursa and Çanakkale in 1844 and to Rumelia in 1846. In his reign the first secondary school (rüşdiye) for girls (1858), a teachers' training school (1848) and the Encümen-i Dâniş (Academy of Learning) (1851) were founded.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same article describes his character as “emotional and touchy”: he would dismiss influential grand viziers who did not suit his temperament, and would give way to the promptings of his consorts, daughters and sons-in-law. One of the chief reasons for the changes of grand vizier was foreign intervention; depending on the degree of pressure from the British and French ambassadors, he would bring one group or the other to power. The British ambassador Stratford Canning could enter the sultan's presence without an appointment. The finances collapsed: the first paper money was issued, the foreign borrowing that began in 1854 for the Crimean War was repeated four times up to 1860, and important sources of revenue were mortgaged for each loan. TDV writes that part of this borrowed money was used to have palaces and pavilions built (Dolmabahçe, Beykoz, Küçüksu). His inability to stop a policy that met Europe's every demand increased discontent; in 1859 the Kuleli Incident, which aimed to depose him, came to light.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nThe TDV text does not separately report what contemporaries said about the sultan; the only contemporary judgement it reports is the nickname “the uncrowned sultan”, which diplomats gave Stratford Canning because of his influence over the sultan. The judgement of the opposition, for its part, is on record through action: those who prepared the Kuleli affair wanted to change the sultan in order to stop the European states from acting like a guardian. When the Grand Vizier Âli Pasha sharply criticised the borrowing, he was dismissed on 18 October 1859. The bibliography of the article rests on contemporary witnesses such as Cevdet Pasha's Tezâkir and Ma'rûzât, Lutfî's History and the memoirs of the palace doctor Spitzer.\n\n■ WHAT DID YOU MAKE OF HIM\nThe picture drawn by the author of the article, Cevdet Küçük, is twofold: Abdülmecid was a sultan who recognised the sharia as supreme but had come to believe that there was no other remedy than to give the state an order suited to the conditions of the age, and who therefore supported reforming statesmen. Remaining faithful to his edicts, he tried to unite the people around equality and Ottomanism; but the privileges recognised by the Reform Edict and the protection extended by the Western states made this unity impossible. According to Küçük, with the acceptance of the joint note of Europe (1839) the state had in a sense come under the tutelage of the European states; Abdülmecid is the sultan who both made the proclamations of this tutelage and was unable to stop it. The efforts in education did not achieve complete success either; in schooling, as in law, a duality of old and new arose.", "c33b9c22"], "kisa": ["The young sultan who opened the palace gates to the people and proclaimed the Tanzimat and the Reform Edict; but in the same years a ruler who changed his grand viziers according to pressure from ambassadors and had palaces built with borrowed money. The TDV article records both faces on the same page.", "a1322023"]},
"nasil-ahmed1":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Ahmed I? As a pious benefactor, or as a harsh and merciless ruler?", "51c406be"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nWhen he ascended the throne he was only fourteen years old and had not gone out to govern a sanjak and gain experience in administration. Even so, his first act was to send to the Old Palace his grandmother Safiye Sultan, who had meddled in affairs of state during the reigns of two sultans. In order to protect the people from the oppression of the provincial officials in the villages emptied by the Celali revolts, he issued a justice decree (adaletname) in 1609 and worked to resettle the abandoned villages. He attached importance to the navy; in his time the Ottoman fleet enjoyed a successful period. Because he was a pious and charitable sultan who did not give himself up to pleasure and amusement, he won the people's trust. He built the Sultan Ahmed Mosque and its complex, and when the foundations were laid he worked with a golden pickaxe until he broke into a sweat; he had the walls of the Kaaba repaired and sent a marble pulpit to Medina. He wrote poetry under the pen name Bahti and has a small divan (collected poems).\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam characterizes him as harsh by nature: he did not forgive those who betrayed him, and because of his harshness he was at times merciless even toward those who served the state. In the first years of his reign Anatolia was turned upside down by the Celali revolts; most of the people left their villages, and the treasury was deprived of tax revenues. The grand vizier Derviş Pasha, who proved helpless against the Celalis, was executed; Kuyucu Murad Pasha, who restored order, resorted to appearing to pardon the rebels and then eliminating them. The places lost to Shah Abbas in the east and the unsuccessful Yerevan (Revan) campaign cast their shadow over the reign.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nThe TDV article does not separately report any judgment on his personality from contemporary chronicles or foreign observers. The only general judgment of historians that it reports concerns the Treaty of Zsitvatorok of 1606: historians generally agree that the growth of the Ottoman state came to a halt with this treaty. Under the treaty Austria's annual tribute was abolished, and the sultan and the emperor were deemed equals. The TDV stresses that the Celali revolts in Anatolia played a great part in the acceptance of this treaty; that is, the peace was not a weakness but the result of being squeezed on two fronts.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nMücteba İlgürel's article does not offer a lengthy assessment; it sums up his personality in a few sentences: pious, charitable, a man who had won the people's trust, but harsh. The legacy the article chiefly stresses is the change in the succession to the sultanate: from him onward it became the accepted practice for the eldest member of the dynasty to ascend the throne (seniority), and the other princes began to be kept behind the lattice of the 'kafes' (the princes' confinement). That he was succeeded by his brother Mustafa I was the result of observing this rule. He himself died at the age of twenty-eight of a stomach illness that lasted fifty-one days.", "9086fbf0"], "kisa": ["The sultan who ascended the throne at fourteen and won the people's trust through his piety and charity is described in the same TDV article as harsh by nature and at times merciless even toward those who served the state. His reign, for its part, is remembered as the years in which the empire's growth came to a halt.", "f5b158b5"]},
"nasil-ahmed2":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Ahmed II? As a sultan who protected the reaya, or as a sensitive temperament swayed by suggestion?", "b0fcdac8"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nHe came to the throne at the age of forty-nine, in the middle of the war with Austria. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam writes that in affairs of state he always gave priority to the rights of the reaya (tax-paying subjects). As business increased, he raised the meetings of the Divan (imperial council), in accordance with the law code of the Lawgiver, from two days a week to four; he made it his habit to be present in person at the deliberations and did not abandon this habit even during his illness. In order to protect the reaya from the arbitrary conduct of frequently changing tax farmers and to prevent losses to the treasury, the malikâne system (life-term tax farming) was introduced in his reign for the tax farms of state revenue sources (mîrî mukâtaa); it began first in provinces such as Damascus, Aleppo and Diyarbekir and was later to spread to the whole of Anatolia and Rumelia. He was interested in poetry and music and was a calligrapher. He was greatly grieved by the occupation of Chios and immediately ordered preparations for the island's rescue.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nIn the same sentence the TDV also says that he had a sensitive and irascible temperament and was quickly swayed by the suggestions of those around him. Bozoklu Mustafa Pasha, who saved Belgrade from an Austrian siege, was dismissed on his return from the campaign by the sultan, who was under the influence of the chief black eunuch (kızlar ağası). In his short reign grand viziers changed often; Kadı Ali Pasha went because he did not set out on campaign and gave way to greed for wealth, and Hacı Ali Pasha resigned because of the unjust dismissal of the chief treasurer (defterdar). At Slankamen, Fazıl Mustafa Pasha fell in battle, the army broke up and the fortress of Várad was lost; the siege of Petrovaradin ended without result on account of the rain and the enemy's reinforcements. When the garrisons of the islands were reduced for the sake of the campaign, Chios was left defenseless and was occupied. As for the malikâne system, although at first it protected the people, it later prepared the ground for the emergence of the office of ayan (provincial notables), which was to cause the state great troubles.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nThe TDV article is short (1989) and does not report any direct judgment on his personality from contemporary chronicles or foreign observers. The information about the custom of the Divan is based on Raşid's History and a modern study (Özcan). It is recorded that during his reign the fires of Cibali, Ayazma Kapısı and the Bedesten (covered market) burned down a large part of Istanbul.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nMücteba İlgürel's article does not offer a lengthy assessment; it sums up his personality in a few adjectives: a sultan who was sensitive, irascible and open to suggestion, but who looked after the rights of the reaya and took part in person in the business of the Divan. The lasting mark of his reign is a fiscal measure of good intention and debatable result: the malikâne system, introduced to protect the people, opened the door to the problem of the ayan in later periods. His four-year reign ended with his death from dropsy in Edirne.", "0dac1dfc"], "kisa": ["The sultan who convened the Divan four days a week, did not stay away from its deliberations even when ill, and put the rights of the reaya first is the same sultan who changed his grand viziers at the prompting of the chief black eunuch. The malikâne system he introduced to protect the reaya, for its part, prepared the ground for the office of ayan in the following century.", "bcd2c2f6"]},
"nasil-ahmed3":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Ahmed III? As the patron of the printing press and the tulip, or as a sultan who amused himself at pleasure grounds while the people were in distress?", "c27c7986"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nIn time he removed the ringleaders of the Edirne Incident, to whom he had given positions while being raised to the throne, and became complete master of the administration of the state. In his reign a great victory was won against Tsar Peter on the Pruth, Azov was recovered, and the Morea peninsula, which had been ceded to Venice at Karlowitz, was reconquered. The Treaty of Passarowitz prevented further loss of territory in Serbia. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, in his twenty-seven-year reign, particularly during the grand vizierate of his son-in-law İbrahim Pasha, he attached great importance to economic and social questions and to innovations: with the embassy of Yirmisekiz Çelebi Mehmed Efendi to Paris, westernization began in practice, the quarantine system gained importance, the corps of firefighting pumpmen was founded, the manufacture of paper was begun at Yalova and of tiles at the Tekfur Palace, and by his decree the first press to print books in Turkish was opened in 1727. He was a good calligrapher who had trained in the hand of Hafız Osman and a poet with the pen name Necib; he patronized many poets, Nedim foremost among them, and a committee of translators. He had the palace library and the famous fountain in the square of Hagia Sophia built.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nAfter the Morea, the Corfu campaign and the war with Austria went unsuccessfully. As the Iranian campaigns grew more burdensome, hostility toward him and his grand vizier increased. The TDV enumerates the new taxes imposed one after another because of the campaigns, the lack of public order in the provinces, migration to Istanbul, unemployment and the hardship of the tradesmen; in these hard days, the amusements of the sultan and the statesmen at pleasure grounds aroused the anger of the people. The constant appointment to high offices of relatives of the grand vizier, the Shaykh al-Islam and the viziers alienated state and ulema circles. His unwillingness to undertake a new campaign against Nader's advance in the east was the final spark of the revolt. In the end he was forced to have his own son-in-law and grand vizier strangled and handed over to the rebels; a day later he was made to abdicate.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nMünir Aktepe's article does not directly report any personal judgment on the sultan by a contemporary chronicle or ambassador; the bibliography cites the reports of the Venetian baili and the author's own study of the Patrona revolt. It notes that the name 'Tulip Era' was given to the period later, that the tulip became a symbol and that books were written on its cultivation. Details about his marksmanship also appear in the sources.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nAktepe's assessment is predominantly favorable: a sultan of an era who protected art and artists, attached importance to innovations and in whose time westernization began in practice. But the same article also clearly enumerates the economic and social roots of the discontent that brought the reign to an end. He spent the last six years of his life in the apartment set aside for deposed sultans.", "d5cc915b"], "kisa": ["The victory on the Pruth, the recovery of the Morea, the first Turkish printing press and the window opened to the West belong to his reign; so do war taxes, unemployment and revels at the pleasure grounds. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, what gave rise to the Patrona Halil revolt was these two pictures standing side by side.", "01ccbd9a"]},
"nasil-bayezid1":{"baslik": ["How was Bayezid I the Thunderbolt regarded? The founder of the first centralised state, or the one who brought it down?", "560da821"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nHe won the epithet 'Yıldırım' (the Thunderbolt) through the courage and dash he showed at the Battle of Frenk Yazısı during the Karaman campaign of 1386. At Kosovo (1389) he saved the victory by his exertions on the right wing. After coming to the throne he swiftly brought the principalities of western Anatolia under Ottoman rule, routed the Crusader army of the Hungarian King Sigismund at Nicopolis (1396) and held Istanbul under blockade for years. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, he established the first centralised administration, organised the kul system (of the sultan's slave-servants) and introduced new practices of customary (sultanic) law and law codes (kanunnames); with him the Ottoman State became one of the principal centres of international politics. The Great Mosque (Ulucami), hospital, madrasa and soup kitchen in Bursa and Anadoluhisarı are his works.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam adds in the same sentence that he had a harsh temperament. As soon as he came to the throne, on the advice of the high officials of state, he had his only brother Yakub killed so that civil war would not break out. While the danger from Timur was growing, instead of coming to terms with the Mamluk sultan, he took from the Mamluks cities such as Elbistan, Malatya and Divriği. His protection of Timur's enemies Ahmad Jalayir and Qara Yusuf angered Timur; he replied harshly to Timur's letter of threats and admonition and rejected all his terms. The result was defeat at Ankara (1402), captivity and the shrinking of the state in Anatolia back to its borders at the beginning of the reign of Murad I.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nThe harshest words from among his contemporaries come from Timur: according to the article on Osman Ghazi in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, Timur, in the letter he wrote to Bayezid, sought to demean the Ottoman sultan as descended from a family of Turkmen boatmen; letters full of insults passed back and forth between the two rulers. Neşri writes that during the battle some of the forces from the principalities, above all Germiyan, went over to the side of their former lords who had taken refuge with Timur. After his victory Timur sent the kings of France and England victory dispatches informing them that he had defeated the Ottoman ruler whom they had been unable to defeat. The article on the Battle of Ankara in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam records that the defeat delayed the conquest of Istanbul by nearly half a century.\n\n■ HOW WAS HE REGARDED\nThe verdict of Halil İnalcık, the author of the article, is two-sided: Bayezid partly succeeded in his aim of removing the vassal dynasties and establishing a centralised state in the Near Eastern Islamic conception of the state; but this new centralised state was not long-lived. He was an extremely brave, active, capable and just administrator, but harsh in temperament. What saved the state and preserved its unity was its lands in Rumelia.", "b606053f"], "kisa": ["In thirteen years he built a centralised state stretching from the Danube to the Euphrates, then lost his gains in Anatolia in a single battle at Ankara. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam describes him both as 'extremely brave, just' and as 'harsh in temperament'.", "318de386"]},
"nasil-bayezid2":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Bayezid II? As a saint, or as a man who could not keep his throne?", "8af5eed2"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nThe TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam describes him as a man of mild, even melancholic temperament: in his youth he led a free life, but on becoming sultan he turned to worship and charitable works, and for this reason he came to be called Bayezid-i Veli (Bayezid the Saint). He stayed away from war unless forced into it, and strove to ensure peace at home and to put the administrative system on a firm footing. He returned to their former owners the endowments that had been converted into timars in his father's time. He wrote poetry under the pen name Adli (the TDV considers him a poet of middling rank, and indeed a quite gifted calligrapher). He commissioned two Ottoman histories, one in Persian from İdris-i Bitlisi and one in Turkish from İbn Kemal; the names of a great many poets, artists and scholars appear in his register of gifts. He attended the funeral of Molla Gürani and paid his debts from the treasury. Leonardo da Vinci had written him a letter offering to build a bridge over the Golden Horn and the Bosphorus. The mosque complexes in Istanbul, Edirne and Amasya are his legacy.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe TDV also writes that he was not as tolerant and open-minded as his father: Bellini's paintings were removed from the palace and sold, and Molla Lutfi was accused of unbelief and executed. In order to have the Knights of Rhodes keep hold of his brother Cem, he agreed to pay them 40,000 ducats a year, while at the same time seeking ways to have Cem abducted or killed. When he grew old and gout ruined his health, he wished to put on the throne his son Ahmed, who resembled him, and thereby set off an early struggle among his sons. The Şahkulu revolt drenched Anatolia in blood; in the end, under pressure from the Janissaries, he gave up the throne to Selim (1512).\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nThe Venetian ambassador Andrea Gritti, who came to Istanbul in 1503, writes that Korkut considered himself the most worthy of the throne, while Selim had won fame through his war with the Shah of Iran. The Genoese Antonio Menavino, who was in his service, gives a close account of the transfer of the throne to Selim. İbn Kemal records that the number of his children and grandchildren exceeded 300. His death is suspicious: according to the TDV, some local and foreign sources dwell on the possibility that he may have been poisoned.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nIn the judgment of Şerafettin Turan, the author of the article, Bayezid was a sultan who loved tranquility and wished to see the country flourishing and the people prosperous, but he did not achieve this aim. Among the reasons Turan counts the outbreaks of plague, the six-year famine and the earthquake of 1509 known as the 'lesser doomsday'; but he holds the sultan himself responsible too: his growing intolerance and his leaving the administration to unqualified persons also had a share in this failure.", "0361f5d1"], "kisa": ["He returned to their owners the endowments his father had confiscated, generously protected scholars and poets, and was called 'the Saint'. But he stepped down from the throne he had ascended with the support of the Janissaries, again under their pressure.", "9d1e26e4"]},
"nasil-ibrahim":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Sultan İbrahim? As 'the Mad', or as a troubled sultan who nonetheless asked after his work?", "0ce8381a"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nHis reign was a calmer period than that of his elder brother Murad IV. The first four years passed in stability thanks to the grand vizier Kemankeş Kara Mustafa Pasha, who is remembered in the sources as a capable administrator; the harsh regime gave way to a freer administration, and fiscal measures brought relief to Istanbul and the provinces. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam writes plainly that the sultan also had a share in this: he followed the memoranda submitted to him and frequently sent questions and instructions to his grand vizier. In the imperial writs he wrote to the grand vizier he says that he grows uneasy if for a few days no news of state affairs arrives, and that his heart is saddened when no news comes from the frontiers; he asks for information about Yemen, Azov and Ochakov (Özi). According to the TDV, there are not as many spelling mistakes in his writs as has been claimed, and he had received an education that can be considered good. It was he himself who insisted on taking Crete; he wished to revive his ancestors' power of conquest.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nAfter the execution of Kemankeş Mustafa Pasha, intrigues within the palace began; favorites such as Cinci Hüseyin Efendi and Şekerpare Hatun gained influence over the sultan. The grand vizier Ahmed Pasha stoked the sultan's passion for furs and imposed a sable tax. According to Vecihi, the administrators in the provinces were ordered by decree to supply sable for lining the pavilions, and those who failed to bring it were dismissed. In order to furnish the mansion of one favorite, inns and covered markets were opened in the middle of the night and goods were taken by force. The Venetian blockade put Istanbul in difficulty, and in Dalmatia more than thirty fortresses fell. He left no great charitable work in his own name.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nKatip Çelebi, Vecihi, Mehmed Halife and Karaçelebizade, who was himself involved in the events, narrate more cautiously and plainly than Naima. Mehmed Halife blames the grand vizier who steered the sultan toward women and wrestling entertainments, the spread of bribery, and the diverting of water to the palaces. According to Naima, on the day of the deposition Karaçelebizade told the sultan to his face that he had squandered the treasury. The TDV finds Naima's account of the execution somewhat far from convincing and very dramatic; but it also notes that the reasonable replies reported by Naima contradict the idea that these were the words of someone on the verge of madness.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nAccording to Feridun Emecen, İbrahim was not like his uncle Mustafa I; he was a man shaken from time to time by psychological disorders. At the beginning he tended, like Murad IV, to take the administration directly into his own hands; but owing to his increasing crises, the worsening problems and the influence of the Harem, he lost the attentiveness of his first years and in the end let everything take its course. His condition was not constant; it sometimes grew worse and sometimes eased. As almost the only male member of the dynasty, he is also in a sense considered its second founder.", "59484ca7"], "kisa": ["In popular memory he is 'İbrahim the Mad'; but the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam says that this epithet was put forward by some historians in the early 20th century, whereas the hundreds of imperial writs from his own pen show a man who was ill but of sound mind, anxiously following affairs of state. The extravagance with sable and ambergris, however, is a reality of the same reign.", "6e8b49e2"]},
"nasil-mahmud1":{"baslik": ["What did you make of Mahmud I? The sultan of the last brilliant period, or a master of balance who stayed on his feet by changing grand viziers?", "9958df87"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nWhen he took the throne after twenty-seven years of confinement in the kafes (the princes' apartments), the state was practically under the tutelage of Patrona Halil. By means of men he trusted, he drew the leading military figures to his own side, broke Patrona's influence, eliminated him and gained control of the situation; the revolt that followed failed thanks to the support of the townspeople. Belgrade was recovered from Austria, and the peace of 1739 brought the wars to an end; with Iran an agreement was made based on the principles of Qasr-e Shirin. By staying out of the wars of succession in Europe and the turmoil in Iran after the death of Nader Shah, he maintained his peaceful policy. He tried to strengthen the army, kept the Janissaries under control and paid their salaries regularly; the Hendesehâne (school of geometry) founded by Bonneval is the first example of the later engineering schools. In 1740 he issued a justice decree (adaletname). He adorned Istanbul with libraries and brought water to Galata and Beyoğlu; the name Taksim derives from the water distribution reservoir that he had built.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nIn the first weeks of his reign he gave in to all the demands of the rebels: they had their own men appointed to important posts, and he accepted the demolition of the Sa'dabad pavilions. His way of getting rid of Patrona was to summon him to a meeting at the palace. He rejected the agreement signed with Iran because it gave up Tabriz, and dismissed the grand vizier and the Shaykh al-Islam who favoured peace; with the added prompting of Beşir Agha, a new grand vizier was appointed and war was declared. The influence of the chief eunuchs of the Abode of Felicity (darüssaade aghas), the levend (irregular soldiers) banditry in Anatolia and the Istanbul revolt of 1748 were the internal problems of his reign. He tried to establish the power of the centre in the provinces but could not prevent the ayan (provincial notables) from becoming a centre of power.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nThe sources describe him as a pious, intelligent, learned, mild-tempered, public-spirited, peace-loving, just and dignified sultan. Because of the importance he attached to the development of Istanbul, contemporary historians call him muammir-i bilâd, meaning the one who makes cities flourish. According to the History of Destari Salih, his uncle Ahmed III, who was the first to pledge allegiance to him, advised him to take the affairs of state into his own hands and to trust no one. Şem'dânîzâde is one of the principal witnesses of the period. His summoning İbrahim Müteferrika into his presence to ask the reason for the defeats led to the writing of the Usûlü'l-hikem.\n\n■ WHAT DID YOU MAKE OF HIM\nAccording to Abdülkadir Özcan, Mahmud I gave the Ottoman state its last brilliant period and left his successors a long era of peace. Abroad he put his name to successful agreements; at home he followed a policy of balance that sought to forestall unrest by frequently changing grand viziers. It is reported that he attended the meetings of the Divan (imperial council) and listened to the people's troubles. He died at the gate of Topkapı Palace while returning from Friday prayer.", "b79ebe68"], "kisa": ["A reign that began under the tutelage of Patrona Halil ended with the recovery of Belgrade and a long peace. TDV regards him as the sultan who gave the state its last brilliant period; but the same article also writes that he calmed internal unrest by frequently changing grand viziers and could not prevent the rise of the ayan.", "7b21a5f2"]},
"nasil-mahmud2":{"baslik": ["What did you make of Mahmud II? The renewer of the century, or the “infidel sultan”?", "50579881"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nThe TDV article presents Mahmud II as a capable, determined, hard-working and extremely cool-headed ruler: the moment when, having only just escaped death, he gave Alemdar his first orders is an example of this. He followed affairs of state personally down to the smallest detail, studied the translations of diplomatic notes down to their meanings between the lines and wrote important official documents himself; it was thanks to this attentiveness that he discovered the young Mustafa Reşid. He followed the foreign press; he was the first Ottoman ruler to see the press and public opinion as a political force. In the war of 1828-29 he stayed for more than a year at Rami Barracks in a colonel's uniform; his palace was so plain that Prussian officers such as Moltke found it less showy than the house of a rich Hamburg merchant. He eliminated powerful ayan such as Ali Pasha of Tepelena; by abolishing the Janissary Corps in 1826 he opened the way for the reforms of the last thirteen years; he established the ministries. He said that he made no distinction between his Muslim and non-Muslim subjects and permitted the repair of thousands of churches.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same article also sets out the weak points. The Greek revolt of 1821 turned into an international question; the Ottoman-Egyptian fleet was burned at Navarino (1827), and the Russians came as far as Edirne. The forces of Mehmed Ali Pasha defeated the Ottoman army at Konya, took the grand vizier prisoner and advanced as far as Kütahya (1833); lands taken by the conquests of 1516-1517 were left to the administration of Mehmed Ali, and with Hünkâr İskelesi Russia was granted important rights in the Straits. Finance was the weakest side of the age: through ten devaluations the silver content of the kuruş fell by about 80 percent, and the high cost of living crushed those on small salaries. He would forgive personal grievances, but from the first day he kept a list of those who committed offences against the state and made use of it when the time came. His taking to excessive drinking in his last years dragged him into a fatal illness.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nAccording to what TDV reports, Şirvanlı Fâtih Efendi exalted him as the “müceddid” (renewer) who comes once in a century; Câbî Ömer Efendi records that rumours arose among the people that he was a saint. On the other side, because of his European dress, his having his portraits hung in government offices and the opera pieces played in place of the mehter (Ottoman military band), part of the people called him the “infidel sultan”. As for the Englishman Slade's criticism that he weakened the Turkish cavalry, the author of the article regards it as a superficial observation that fails to grasp the technical necessity. Rosen writes that it was announced that the new eşkinci soldiers would drill in European uniform; this announcement all but openly drove the Janissaries to revolt. His funeral, contrary to what had been feared, turned into a procession joined by crowds of Muslims and non-Muslims.\n\n■ WHAT DID YOU MAKE OF HIM\nThe author of the article, Kemal Beydilli, places him in the ranks of the great renewers: just as the Russian people called Peter “mad”, so the Ottoman people's calling him the “infidel sultan” is the common fate of renewers who change the destiny of nations. According to Beydilli, the revolt of 1826 was no accident but a trap set by a sultan who believed that the time had come; in this period the old order was granted no right whatsoever to live. His life, spent in a struggle so wearing that he said “Gāile-i saltanattan usandım” (I am sick of the troubles of the sultanate), was a fight to keep the state standing.", "97f5d883"], "kisa": ["To some, the renewer who comes once in a century to revive the state; to part of the people, the fez-wearing, opera-listening “infidel sultan”. In the same reign the Janissary Corps was abolished; but Greece, Navarino and Kütahya happened too.", "f1e84f9c"]},
"nasil-mehmed1":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Mehmed Çelebi? As a conciliator, or as a man who shed his brothers' blood?", "927811db"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nAfter the eleven years in which, following the disaster of Ankara, four brothers fought one another for Bursa and Edirne, he once again gathered the Ottoman lands into a single hand (1413). Once on the throne, he received the envoys of Byzantium, Serbia, Wallachia, the Morea and Athens and sent them back with assurances of peace and friendship. The Karamanid ruler sued for peace, and the lands of Hamîd-ili and Said-ili were annexed. In his campaign against Wallachia he had the fortress of Giurgiu (Yergöğü) built, and the voivode Mircea was made to pay tribute. In Bursa he built a mosque, a madrasa, a soup kitchen and a tomb; the Eskicami (Old Mosque) in Edirne was completed in his time. He is known as 'Çelebi' or by the epithet 'Kirişçi', which comes from the Greek for 'young lord'.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nUnity did not come without a price. According to the Ottoman account, after defeating his brother İsa Çelebi, he captured him and killed him at Eskişehir. He had Sheikh Bedreddin, whose followers had risen in revolt in Deliorman and western Anatolia, seized at Zagra and executed at Serres. Orhan, the son of his elder brother Süleyman, was blinded and kept in a dungeon. In foreign policy he bowed to the east: in his reply to a warning from Timur's son Shah Rukh he expressed, in the words of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, complete submission. Close to his death, in order to secure the throne for his son Murad, he made an agreement accepting that he would send his two younger sons to Byzantium as hostages.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nThe Byzantine historian Doukas records that Mehmed called the emperor Manuel II 'father'. The Ottoman side's story of the struggle of the Interregnum can be followed in the Menâkıbnâme transmitted by Neşri. Modern historians dispute this narrative on some points: Elizabeth Zachariadou states that İsa was killed in 1403 not by Mehmed but by Süleyman. Byzantine and Serbian sources (Doukas, the biography of Stefan Lazarević) describe the agreements Mehmed made with his neighbors during his illness. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam does not report any detailed contemporary portrait of his personality.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nIn the judgment of Halil İnalcık, the author of the encyclopaedia article, Mehmed's ultimate success rested on his mostly appeasing and conciliatory attitude toward the emperor; his rival Musa, by contrast, had alienated people through his harsh personality and a centralizing policy that offended the frontier lords. According to İnalcık, the fundamental question of his reign was whether the state would re-emerge as the dominant power after the disaster of 1402, and this was achieved: the dynasty held its ground as the sole source of legitimacy in the region, and the system of land surveys and timars was developed and spread in this period.", "8164e838"], "kisa": ["He ended the Interregnum and reunited the disintegrating state; he did so by calling the Byzantine emperor 'father' and bowing to the Timurids. Along the same road, his brothers and a sheikh lost their lives.", "f511e060"]},
"nasil-mehmed2":{"baslik": ["How do you remember the Conqueror? As a world emperor, or as a man who crushed the people with taxes?", "f9c119bf"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nAccording to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, immediately after the conquest of Istanbul the Conqueror was, as a western source records, a ruler extremely bold in every undertaking, eager to win glory like Alexander of Macedon, intelligent, and one who turned his back on pleasure. He knew Turkish, Greek and a Slavic language. Contemporary Arab sources praise his closeness to the ulema, the importance he attached to meeting with them, and the respect with which he received those who came to him. In the years 1464-1472 Greek scholars received special attention at his court: George of Trebizond returned from Rome to Istanbul, Kritoboulos wrote his work in these years, and the scholar Amiroutzes of Trebizond had a place close to the Conqueror. He reunited all the Orthodox under the administration of the patriarch. Domestic trade passed from the hands of the Italians to local merchants; the industries of cotton cloth, mohair, silk and broadcloth developed.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same western source also calls him harsh-tempered. His first act after the conquest was to eliminate Çandarlı Halil Pasha, who limited his power, and Orhan Çelebi, who threatened his throne; he also had two of his later grand viziers, the pashas Mahmud and Rum Mehmed, executed. Constant campaigns and the rebuilding of Istanbul increased expenses: five times he issued a new akçe and exchanged the old one at one fifth less, he made everyday goods such as salt, soap and candles into monopolies, and in Anatolia he raised the çift tax (levy on a peasant farm) from 22 akçes to 33. According to Tursun Bey, thousands of villages and hamlets ceased to be endowments and private property and were distributed as timars. According to the TDV, these measures squeezed peasants and townsfolk, offended the ulema, the sheikhs and the old lordly families, and bred discontent both hidden and open.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nArab sources compare him with his father: he kept up his father's zeal against the Franks, but lagged behind him when it came to pleasure and revelry. The discontent ran so deep that, on ascending the throne, Bayezid II was forced to agree that he would not issue a new akçe more than once, and his first act was to return the confiscated private properties and endowments to their owners. His death is attributed to gout; according to the TDV, the claim that he was poisoned rests on one scholar's interpretation of a passage in Aşıkpaşazade and is not confirmed by any other source. Among modern historians, Iorga regards his empire as an Eastern Roman Empire revived in Islamic guise; the TDV finds this wrong, and also considers exaggerated the thesis that Byzantine institutions were imitated.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nAccording to Halil İnalcık, the author of the article, the Conqueror is in the true sense the founder of a new empire: a mighty soldier who made world dominion his aim and a man of culture with a broad outlook. İnalcık's key is this: both in his ruthless measures and in his protection of science and art, a single idea prevailed, namely to make his state the supreme empire of the world in every respect. Praise and criticism alike are two results of the same goal.", "b0a541da"], "kisa": ["The founder of an empire, who took Istanbul and gathered scholars at his court; yet by the time he died, the people, the ulema and the old lordly families were so weary of his policies on coinage and land that his son's first act was to reverse them.", "6d2df24a"]},
"nasil-mehmed3":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Mehmed III? As the resolute ghazi of Mezőkeresztes (Haçova), or as a sultan in his mother's shadow?", "465c5bdc"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nAs a prince in Manisa he acquired the habit of mixing with the people and listening to their troubles; on becoming sultan he revived the Friday processions his father had neglected and restored the custom whereby viziers approached him on the way and spoke with him about the affairs of the state and the people. This gave rise among the people to the expectation that he would rule with justice. His going on campaign was more his own wish than the result of compulsion. At Mezőkeresztes (Haçova), when the imperial forces reached as far as his tent, some viziers proposed that he be disguised and smuggled away; prompted by the words of Hoca Sadeddin Efendi, he stayed where he was and did not leave the side of the standard. The rallying of the army followed. He was generally tolerant toward Christians, had some captive members of foreign missions released, and attached importance to relations with England and France. It is also recorded that he wrote good poetry under the pen name Adli.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nOn the night he ascended the throne, nineteen of his brothers, four of them adults, were strangled. According to the report of a foreign observer, when the eldest begged for mercy, the sultan could not answer and turned his head away; he could not show the courage to oppose the law of his ancestors. He wished to free himself from the influence of his mother and her circle, but his temperament, which was obedient, easily persuaded and not insistent, did not allow it. During the sipahi revolt of 1603 he was seized with fear, had two chief officials of the harem beheaded and handed them over to the troops. In the same year he had his eldest son, Prince Mahmud, strangled, suspecting that he had designs on the throne. He left no significant charitable foundation worth mentioning.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nThe sources of the age remember him as naive, credulous, calm and extremely pious; he had a nature that was quickly saddened and could not keep its composure in the face of bad news. Hasanbeyzade Ahmed and, quoting him, Solakzade, Katip Çelebi and Naima write that after Eger he wished to return to Istanbul; the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam states that the other historians who were present on the campaign make no mention of any such incident and that Hasanbeyzade was a partisan of İbrahim Pasha. A Venetian report presents him as intelligent and brave but lustful and timid; the TDV is of the opinion that this portrayal is not entirely accurate. The report of the English ambassador Lello is one of the chief testimonies to the crisis of 1603.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nFeridun Emecen's judgment is this: Mehmed III made the first and last show of determination of his reign in the critical hours at Mezőkeresztes. He was probably unable throughout his reign to shake off the weight of the killing of his nineteen brothers, and the death of his son made him withdraw into himself still further. The unfavorable opinion of him was shaped not by his person alone; the stamp of Safiye Sultan's team, the long Habsburg war, the Celali movements that shook Anatolia, the losses in the east and the ruined economy together prepared it.", "07c24db9"], "kisa": ["The sultan who rallied the army at Mezőkeresztes (Haçova) by not leaving the side of the standard and the sultan who could not make his mother Safiye Sultan and his viziers heed him are the same person. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, he showed his determination once, in those critical hours.", "c53ff6f4"]},
"nasil-mehmed4":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Mehmed IV? As the sultan of the Köprülü era, or as 'the Hunter' who never raised his head from the chase?", "d14882f4"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nHis mother Turhan Sultan's appointment of Köprülü Mehmed Pasha to the grand vizierate, and then, on the latter's testament, of his son Fazıl Ahmed Pasha, ensured the recovery of the state. In these twenty years Uyvar was taken, the Treaty of Vasvár was signed, and in 1669, with the fall of Candia, the twenty-five-year question of Crete was resolved. He went in person on the Kamianets (Kamaniçe) campaign, endured the hardship of torrential rains and watched the siege from his tent; it is also stated that he disguised himself and actually took part in the siege alongside the soldiers. According to the sources, he was a man who was good-hearted, very generous, dressed simply and led an orderly life. He was devoted to history: he took history lessons from Hezarfen Hüseyin Efendi, charged his private secretary Abdi Ağa with recording the events of the period, and from time to time checked whether everything had been written down or not. During his reign the number of composers such as Itri and Hafız Post increased; Edirne lived through its old days of splendor.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nBecause of his passion for hunting he rarely came to Istanbul any more; in the words of Mehmed Halife, neglect had all but turned Istanbul into a village. Under the influence of the Kadızadeli circles he closed the coffeehouses and sentenced actors and musicians to the galleys. After his mother's death in 1683 he continued to take wrong decisions under the influence of the ignorant persons who surrounded him. The period after the rout at Vienna is the darkest time of his reign: Buda, the Morea and Athens fell. Although preachers spoke harsh words to his face, he did not give up hunting; after the fall of Buda, in the face of popular anger, he announced that he had given it up, but he could not change opinion. At the meeting where the decision to depose him was taken, the question of whether it was lawful to depose a sultan who could not raise his head from the chase while the enemy was overrunning the country was answered with silence.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nMehmed Halife's Tarih-i Gılmani is the chief witness to palace events up to 1665; Evliya Çelebi also wrote his work in this era. Evliya Çelebi reports that after his sons were born he wished to have his brothers killed and that his mother prevented this. Western travelers who saw him at a young age describe him as pale-faced and somewhat melancholic. As for the information to the effect that he was interested in western music, wished to have an opera brought from Venice or acquired oil paintings, according to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam it should be treated with caution.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nAccording to Abdülkadir Özcan, Mehmed IV reigned in a very critical period but was unable to take on an effective role. His accession at the age of seven, his failure to receive a good education and his confinement to the narrow circle of the palace explain his fondness for hunting and his lack of interest. The Köprülü era restored the state but pushed him well into the background; after his mother's death and the removal of the Köprülüs, his inexperience came to light, and his love of hunting, which had become an obsession, was the chief cause of his deposition.", "c63b1b5d"], "kisa": ["Uyvar, Candia and Kamianets (Kamaniçe) were taken in his reign; the rout at Vienna, and the loss of Buda and the Morea, also came in his reign. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the great successes belonged to his viziers, while the blame for the great losses was laid on him.", "bd52a747"]},
"nasil-mehmed5":{"baslik": ["What did you make of Sultan Reşad? A ruler loyal to the constitution, or the Unionists' colourless sultan?", "b148866d"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nAccording to the TDV article, Sultan Reşad was a mild-mannered, compassionate, pious and courteous ruler. Although he had not received a regular education, it is said that he knew Eastern culture well and knew Persian; he had joined the Mevlevi order at a young age and spent his years as a prince reading the Mathnawi. He loved reading Ottoman history and the tales of the deeds of his ancestors, and wrote poetry; the ghazal he wrote on the victory at the Dardanelles (Çanakkale) passed from mouth to mouth, was set to music and was expanded by poets in tahmis form (five-line stanzas). On his tour of Rumelia in 1911 he met people from every section of society without distinction of religion or nationality and performed Friday prayer on the plain of Kosovo with a large congregation; the tour had a positive effect on the Albanians in particular. The article writes that in his time the idea of national unity and democracy developed, and that reforms were made in education and law that laid the foundations of the national Turkish state.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same article also names his weaknesses openly: he was a colourless and timid personality; because he lacked political training and experience, he could not be the arbiter of a dynamic and anarchic political life. On the very day of the pledge of allegiance, Mahmud Şevket Pasha, commander of the Action Army, banished the former sultan to Salonica without the knowledge of either the sultan or the grand vizier. The greater part of his reign passed under the control of the Unionists; some appointments he made were turned back by the triumvirate of Talat, Enver and Cemal. He approved the law on the ownership of schools despite the plea of the Patriarch of the Phanar; the article writes that this opened the way for the Balkan states to unite. In 1914, when the country was taken into the war without even some members of the cabinet knowing, he issued the declaration of war and the proclamation of the “great jihad” although he had not wanted this at all.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nTDV reports the sultan's own defence from two sources. According to Danişmend, he would ask those who criticised him for not using his powers what his brother's fault had been, if under constitutional government he was going to interfere in everything. According to the Mufassal Osmanlı Tarihi (Detailed Ottoman History), on the other hand, he would say to those who criticised his submission to the Unionists that he had to act in this way for the survival of the sultanate, and that otherwise they would proclaim a republic. The article also writes that the Unionists came to government unprepared and left affairs to inexperienced people; the sultan's silence took place in this setting.\n\n■ WHAT DID YOU MAKE OF HIM\nThe verdict of the author of the article, Cevdet Küçük, is gentle but clear: the closed and fearful years as heir apparent, spent under the surveillance of his brother Abdülhamid, had affected him deeply; he came to the throne through a coup and spent the greater part of his life under pressure. Although he did not like factionalism or the Unionists, his colourless personality prevented sharp clashes. His period was the last attempt made for the continuation of the Ottoman state, and this attempt ended with the break-up of the state. He died on 3 July 1918, without seeing Mudros.", "85add021"], "kisa": ["A mild-mannered, courteous Mevlevi and poet sultan; he understood constitutional government as “interfering in nothing”. That is why to some he is a ruler who kept within constitutional limits, and to others a colourless ruler who submitted to the Unionists.", "7fd4554f"]},
"nasil-mehmed6":{"baslik": ["What did you make of Vahdeddin? The last sultan playing for time, or the caliph who boarded a British ship?", "403fe054"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nAccording to the TDV article, those close to him and those who worked with him describe Vahdeddin as optimistic, patient, intelligent and quick to understand; in his palace he was a courteous and affectionate family man, and he attached great importance to religious traditions. He engaged at an advanced level in literature, music and calligraphy; sixty-three of his works have been identified. He was extremely cautious in showing his political leanings: when the government announced the dismissal of Mustafa Kemal Pasha, he remained silent and advised him not to resign and return to Istanbul, but to take leave and rest. Despite all British pressure, he did not ratify the Treaty of Sèvres; he rejected the proposal to ratify it at once on the grounds that this would fan the spark in Anatolia into flame. When leaving the country, he did not take with him the sacred relics of the caliphate, saying that they were his ancestors' gift to the Turkish nation.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same sources also write that he was extremely apprehensive, indecisive and correspondingly obstinate, and that he fell under the influence of those he trusted. Because he believed that the extraordinary period could not be got through with a parliament, he dissolved the Chamber of Deputies on 21 December 1918 and postponed the elections until after the peace. In 1920 the parliament was again closed by his will; fatwas were published declaring that it was a religious duty to kill the nationalists, and the Kuva-yi Inzibatiyye (Disciplinary Forces) was formed against the National Forces (Kuva-yi Milliye). In November 1922, writing that his life was in danger, he sought refuge with Britain and was taken out of Istanbul on the ship Malaya. In exile his money went to swindlers and gambling houses; when he died he owed large debts to the tradesmen of San Remo, and his coffin was held under distraint until the debt was paid.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nAccording to what TDV reports, after Sèvres it was argued in a secret session of the Grand National Assembly that the sultan could not be regarded as the legitimate caliph; Mustafa Kemal Pasha accused the sultan of treason, but also maintained that it was not sensible to neglect the office of the caliphate. Fevzi Pasha, who had crossed from Istanbul to Ankara, explained that in Istanbul they had gone along with the occupiers so that the caliphate and the sultanate should not fall into danger altogether. The new caliph, Abdülmecid Efendi, addressed him as a traitor in 1922. Vahdeddin, for his part, wrote in his declaration from exile that it was he who had sent Mustafa Kemal to Anatolia, and that he was regarded as a traitor to the homeland because he had opposed the separation of the caliphate from the sultanate.\n\n■ WHAT DID YOU MAKE OF HIM\nThe author of the article, Cevdet Küçük, does not give a one-sentence general verdict; he depicts him as a sultan playing for time in the midst of a collapse. According to Küçük, external developments, the fall of the thrones in Germany and Austria, had a great effect on his turn towards absolutism. At Sèvres he followed a policy of leaving matters to time: while preventing the Entente states from going further by appearing to trust his grand vizier, he also placed the responsibility for the treaty on the government. Both interpretations come out of the same article; which one weighs more heavily is left to the reader.", "5093471f"], "kisa": ["He did not ratify Sèvres and at first remained silent about Mustafa Kemal's dismissal; but he closed the parliament, the Kuva-yi Inzibatiyye was formed in his reign, and in the end he left the country by taking refuge with the British. Ankara called him a traitor; he rejected this in exile.", "1a9f9ad9"]},
"nasil-murad1":{"baslik": ["How was Murad I regarded? Merciful, or stern?", "cffa03d8"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nOttoman sources portray Murad I as a ruler of medium height, with a round face and an aquiline nose; charitable, just, and one who devoted his life to ghaza (holy war). On his copper coins the title 'al-Malik al-Adil' (the just ruler) appears. His complex at Çekirge in Bursa, his mosques in Bilecik and Yenişehir, the soup kitchen he had built in İznik in his mother's name and the endowments he assigned to dervishes and akhis are tangible traces of his charity. He crossed into Rumelia six times for ghaza; in his reign Rumelia became a second homeland for the Ottomans.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nOutright criticism has little place in the sources; but the portrait in the Byzantine sources is two-sided: he is merciful toward Christians, but he does not overlook faults and resorts to severity when necessary. His son Savcı Bey had rebelled against him together with the Byzantine prince Andronikos (1373). For his enemies his death was a deliverance: the news of his killing at Kosovo was received with joy in Istanbul; Tvrtko, King of Bosnia, wrote letters to Italian cities presenting the battle as his own victory.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nByzantine sources describe him as a sultan who spoke little but spoke well when he did, who was fond of hunting, tireless and always successful against his enemies. In Paris, Philippe de Mézières had written after Kosovo that the Turks had been routed; later Serbian sources also exalt Prince (Knez) Lazar. By contrast, Constantine the Philosopher, biographer of Lazar's successor, openly acknowledges the defeat. The basic Turkish source for the period is the Gazaname of the poet Ahmedi, who may have been at the side of Prince Bayezid at Kosovo. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam states that the letters said to belong to Murad I (in Feridun Bey's compilation) are texts fabricated on the basis of the chronicles.\n\n■ HOW WAS HE REGARDED\nAccording to Halil İnalcık, the author of the article, Murad I was a ruler who, like the Babai sheikhs, considered himself a saint favoured with divine inspiration and embraced ghaza as a religious duty; the Anatolian campaigns seemed to him an unwelcome obligation. He made ties of marriage and vassalage with Christian dynasties an instrument of diplomacy. İnalcık's conclusion: in his reign the first outline of the Ottoman Empire emerged, and the title 'Ghazi Hüdavendigâr' expresses his identity as a builder of an empire through ghaza.", "22b9fa4e"], "kisa": ["The Ottoman chronicles remember him as a just and charitable ghazi; the Byzantine sources also praise him but add that he 'did not overlook faults and could resort to severity'. The news of his death had been received with joy in Istanbul.", "dc9b013f"]},
"nasil-murad2":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Murad II? As a lover of peace, or as a man too fond of revelry?", "45cd96fd"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nAccording to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, contemporary sources portray Murad II as a sensitive, good-hearted man, interested in Sufism, art and learning, and at the same time resolute. The contemporary Arab historian Ibn Taghribirdi considers him the greatest of the rulers of Rum (Anatolia): he was just toward the people, took a close interest in his affairs, and was generous and good-natured. He took Salonica (1430) and defeated crusader armies at Varna (1444) and Kosovo (1448). In his will he asked for a plain tomb, open at the top so that the rain would fall on it, and he freed all his slaves.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same TDV article does not hide his fault either: he was excessively fond of revelry and drinking, and all the sources, from contemporary Ottoman calendars to Greek and Arab histories, agree on this point. Ibn Taghribirdi, too, writes that he loved musicians, and that once this became widely known, musicians flocked to his palace from everywhere. In 1444, following Çandarlı Halil Pasha's policy of peace in the face of the Hungarians and the Karamanids, he withdrew from the places he had taken; he then left the throne to his twelve-year-old son and retired near Bursa to a life of worship. According to the TDV, this withdrawal dragged the state into a grave crisis: the viziers fell out with one another, the crusaders went on the offensive, and the people of Edirne were seized with panic.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nThe western traveler B. de la Broquière, who came to Edirne in 1432, writes that he was told Murad did not like war, and that so it appeared to him as well; in his view, had Murad wished, he could have taken the greater part of Europe. Broquière also mentions him as the ruler most obeyed by his subjects. The Byzantine historian Doukas records that in his time the Turks living from Gallipoli to the Danube were more numerous than the Ottoman subjects in Anatolia. According to the TDV, the recent disasters must have made his faults even more conspicuous in the eyes of the people and the army.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nIn the judgment of Halil İnalcık, the author of the article, Murad II was not a sultan of conquest like his grandfather Bayezid I the Thunderbolt or his son the Conqueror; the circumstances of the age played as large a part in his love of peace as the cautious policy of the Çandarlı family. Even so, in his time the state attained the strength to meet dangers and eventually to expand; the Ottomans' spread and settlement in the Balkans became definitive in his reign. İnalcık also draws the limit of his power: those who in practice determined his authority were the grand vizier and the standing army (kapıkulu) at the center, and the frontier lords in the provinces.", "7860e397"], "kisa": ["His contemporaries find him good-hearted, generous, just and drawn to Sufism; yet they all agree on the same fault: he was excessively fond of revelry and drinking. The two faces of a sultan who gave up the throne to his son in his own lifetime and then returned to it.", "1a4f3d06"]},
"nasil-murad3":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Murad III? As the beginning of the collapse, or as the victim of two wars?", "78960781"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nThe sources of the period remember him as cheerful, mild and gentle by nature, reluctant to shed blood, and a man from whose mouth the word 'no' seldom came. Salomon Schweigger, who was in Istanbul in 1578-1581, writes that he loved reading books, especially history books, that he took an interest in astronomy and permitted the building of an observatory, that he enjoyed the company of scholars, and that he attached importance to justice and inspected the streets in disguise. He had works translated on the discovery of America, the history of France and the Asian khanates. He wrote religious and Sufi poems under the pen name Muradi and was a good calligrapher. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam speaks of his era as a time when the classical form of Ottoman culture reached its peak; the repairs in Mecca and Medina and his mosque complex in Manisa, planned by Mimar Sinan, are his legacy.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same sources also write that he was eager to amass wealth, was fond of pleasure and gathered around him musicians, dancers, buffoons and dwarfs. Ottoman sources of the period, especially the reform treatises, argue that granting such people places in the standing army (kapıkulu) and timars corrupted the system and that the collapse began in this period: offices were granted irregularly, members of the palace took bribes and gave posts to the unqualified, peasants abandoned their land and fled, and the treasury fell into difficulty. His shutting himself up in the palace, and his neglecting even the Friday processions for long periods, was criticized in his own time. His companion Şemsi Ahmed Pasha is accused of opening the way to bribery and accustoming the sultan to it. When the standing army rose in revolt in 1589, and he hesitated to hand over the two statesmen they demanded, he was, according to Selaniki, threatened with deposition.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nSchweigger describes him as a pale-faced, unhealthy man and says that he lived out of sight because he was an epileptic. According to the Venetian ambassadors, he was wary of the affection the people felt for his son Mehmed. Some western sources write that he in fact had a warlike character, but that first his mother Nurbanu and then the circle of Safiye Sultan held him back. Âlî Mustafa Efendi lists one by one the persons who meddled in affairs of state. In a ghazal recorded by Selaniki, the sultan complains that he cannot find a capable administrator such as he would wish.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nThe judgment of Bekir Kütükoğlu, the author of the article, is two-sided. On the one hand, he attributes the main cause of the decay enumerated by the treatises to the adverse effects brought by two great wars, and says that the measures taken to adapt to the changing age formed the sparks of a social explosion. On the other hand, he does not hide the sultan's personal share either: the lack of perseverance and the indecision in his character reached such a degree as to prepare the ground for the interference of persons who had no business in affairs of state. The fear that sedition would break out in the Hijri year 1000 also seems to have affected his melancholic nature.", "ac3a16d2"], "kisa": ["The reform treatises write that the collapse began in his palace: bribery, offices given to the unqualified, a sultan shut away in his palace. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, however, attributes the main cause of the same picture to the burden of two great wars.", "5996f518"]},
"nasil-murad4":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Murad IV? As the will that established order, or as a tyrant who ruled through fear?", "e0654238"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nThe TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam defines him as a personality of a different character among the Ottoman sultans: a man of strong will and memory, daunted by nothing. Even in the years when he was under tutelage he took an interest in affairs of state, tried to see everything at close hand by going about in disguise, and succeeded in improving the country's internal and external situation. He went on two great campaigns at the head of the army; the Treaty of Qasr-e Shirin, signed after the recovery of Baghdad, drew a frontier that was taken as the basis in later centuries as well. To protect the Bosphorus against Cossack raids he had fortresses built at Anadolukavağı and Rumelikavağı, and he had the Kaaba, which had been inundated by floods, thoroughly repaired. The Baghdad Kiosk is counted among the finest works of art of the 17th century. With figures such as Evliya Çelebi, Katip Çelebi, Nef'i and Koçi Bey, his era is one of the most noteworthy periods of intellectual life. He wrote poetry under the pen name Muradi and composed music.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe harsh actions he took to establish his rigid authority led to many unjust practices. After the fire of 1633 he had the coffeehouses torn down on the pretext that they would cause new fires, and banned tobacco and then alcohol. The TDV notes that, despite the story that he hated tobacco and opium, he was excessively fond of drink, and that he adopted the bans because following the inclination of the bigoted sections served him in domestic politics. The people he had put to death in Istanbul and on campaigns, some of them under torture, because of a hostility to tobacco that had become pathological, make up a considerable total. He had his brothers Bayezid, Süleyman and Kasım put to death, and according to one account his uncle Mustafa I; it is said that on his deathbed he ordered that İbrahim be killed too, and that Kösem Sultan prevented this.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nContemporary sources describe him as tall, broad-shouldered, imposing and very strong: his wrestling with champion wrestlers, his wielding a heavy mace and his piercing a rhinoceros-hide shield are parts of this account. Katip Çelebi records the loss of the manuscripts destroyed in the fire of 1633. Some western sources report that he kept spies everywhere and that he read Machiavelli, whom he had had a convert translate. Evliya Çelebi gives the number of his children as thirty-two; the TDV says that those who can be identified number about sixteen. His condition on his deathbed is related by the imam to the sultan, Şami Yusuf Efendi.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nZiya Yılmazer's assessment hides neither side: he directed his ever-increasing harshness mostly against persons he believed to be wicked; this harshness gave rise to injustices, but it brought public order and security. In a reign taken up with campaigns and troubles, he was unable to build any great charitable foundations. His body, worn down by gout and drink, carried him off in his twenties, about a year after the victory at Baghdad.", "f62b35bf"], "kisa": ["The sultan who restored public order and took Yerevan (Revan) and Baghdad is the same sultan who enforced his bans with punishments that went as far as torture and who had his brothers killed. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam records both faces together: his harshness led to many injustices, but it is also clear that it secured order and safety.", "10a1792c"]},
"nasil-murad5":{"baslik": ["What did you make of Murad V? The hope of constitutionalism, or the sultan of the coup-makers?", "7b4367d2"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nAccording to the TDV article, Murad received a good education as a prince: he studied Turkish, Arabic, French, Ottoman history and the sciences, and learned the piano and Western music from Italian teachers. As heir apparent he took part in Abdülaziz's journeys to Egypt (1863) and Europe (1867). He exchanged ideas with Şinasi, Namık Kemal and Ziya Pasha on constitutionalism, democracy and liberty. On taking the throne he made Ziya Pasha chief secretary and asked for the recall of Namık Kemal and his companions, whom Abdülaziz had sent into exile (this request was not carried out). The article mentions him as a man of gentle nature; it writes that he occupied himself with painting and music and produced works, and that he was also interested in architecture.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same article openly sets out the dark sides of his accession. He sent Midhat Pasha money that he had obtained from his banker Hristaki so that the madrasa students could be incited to rise. Although the official communiqué said that the deposition had taken place by the unanimous agreement of all, the affair was a coup arranged by a few people. On the night of the accession, belongings of Abdülaziz at Dolmabahçe were plundered; part of the money was also given to Hristaki for Murad's debt of up to a million. During his reign the government was in fact in the hands of the commander-in-chief (serasker) Hüseyin Avni Pasha. His excessively Europeanised behaviour and his joining the masonic organisation in 1872 were not welcomed by his siblings. The article writes that his greatest weakness is recorded as having been his excessive fondness for drink and his extravagance.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nThe testimonies reported by TDV follow his collapse step by step: because of his abnormal behaviour at the ceremony of allegiance, the ceremony was cut short; after the mysterious death of Abdülaziz he went into shock, threw himself into the pool, and one morning broke the windows and wanted to take his own life. The government spread the story that he had a boil on his face; the ulema, for their part, argued that Friday prayer could not be performed in the country of a mentally ill sultan. The Viennese doctor Leidesdorf also gave an unfavourable report. After his deposition his supporters, masonic circles above all, spread the word that he was healthy and had been unjustly removed; there were three attempts to carry him off and restore him to the throne (1876, 1877 and Ali Suavi's raid on Çırağan, 1878).\n\n■ WHAT DID YOU MAKE OF HIM\nThe author of the article, Cevdet Küçük, does not pronounce a lengthy verdict; he leaves the picture to the facts. Murad V is a ruler whose illness had long been known, whose nerves were thoroughly shattered by events following one upon another, and who went down in history as the Ottoman sultan who stayed on the throne for the shortest time. The group of four that brought him to the throne soon broke up; according to Küçük, the only one among them who sincerely defended liberal democracy was Midhat Pasha, and in the accession rescript constitutionalism was passed over with a few fine words. That is, Murad, who carried the hope of a “constitutional sultan”, appears as a victim both of his own health and of the calculations of those who used him. He lived as a prisoner for twenty-eight years and died of diabetes on 29 August 1904.", "29bfe1b0"], "kisa": ["He was a well-educated, gentle-natured heir apparent who discussed constitutionalism with the Young Ottomans. But he came to the throne through a coup, it was said that he was in command of himself on only seven days of his ninety-three-day reign, and he spent twenty-eight years of his life as a prisoner at Çırağan.", "69d03497"]},
"nasil-mustafa1":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Mustafa I? As a saintly sultan, or as a sick man placed on the throne?", "a8c40d59"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nThe life of this prince, who lived in the 'kafes' (the princes' confinement), was spared partly because of the revulsion felt at Mehmed III's having had his nineteen brothers killed. The only source that gives favorable information about his first reign is Abdülkadir Efendi, secretary of the artillery corps: according to him, the sultan had arms and equipment brought before him and gave gratuities to the master craftsmen, had cannon fired at the Imperial Cannon Foundry (Tophane), toured the Imperial Arsenal, and bestowed gifts on the ulema, the sheikhs and the poor; he does not mention his deposition and writes that he withdrew of his own accord. During his second reign the people and the circles of the dervish lodges interpreted his states of mind as signs of sainthood. Hüseyin Tugi often calls him the saint-sultan; even his remaining standing at the holiday greetings ceremony was praised on the grounds that he was reviving a custom of the first caliphs. His being taken out to the Friday procession in splendid attire is also mentioned with praise in the sources of the age.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe historian of the period Hasanbeyzade Ahmed writes that when he was raised to the throne it was hoped he would recover, but that the physicians' medicines did no good and his state of madness steadily worsened. Peçevi and Katip Çelebi describe his strange behavior, from throwing gold into the sea to pulling at the viziers' turbans. His nephew Osman II, in a letter he wrote to the King of England, said that his uncle had been preferred out of respect for his age but was shamefully incompetent. In his second reign he had no influence even on the appointment of grand viziers; the imperial writs issued in the sultan's name were written through his mother. Karaçelebizade calls his mother the spiritual sultan. The ulema debated whether, because of his mental infirmity, his imamate (rule) was lawful.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nThe Venetian bailo describes him as a man of pale countenance, dull gaze and frail build. The Venetian ambassador Contarini records in his report of 1612 that Ahmed I had twice postponed his intention of having him killed. Some western sources relate that he shouted Osman's name in the palace corridors and cried out to be saved from the burden of the sultanate. The work of Kilari Mehmed Efendi appears to have been written to legitimize his reign and speaks of his sainthood. It is rumored that Murad IV had a role in his death; Mehmed b. Mehmed, however, states that he died a natural death.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nAccording to Feridun Emecen, his mental weakness was already known during his first reign; his confinement, the manner in which he was brought to the throne and Osman's tragic end shook him still further. His state of mind, bordering on insanity, was given a spiritual aura in the eyes of the people and the sheikhs of the dervish lodges. It is likely that the badly spelled imperial writs attributed to him came not from his own hand but from that of his concubine. He had neither a child nor any charitable foundation. After being deposed he lived for fifteen years in the room where he was kept shut up; there is no information about this period.", "5e29e65a"], "kisa": ["The same behavior was regarded on one side as a sign of sainthood and on the other as mental infirmity. He was raised to the throne twice and deposed twice; according to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, in his second reign he exercised no authority at all.", "e61de60d"]},
"nasil-mustafa2":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Mustafa II? As the last ghazi sultan, or as a ruler who returned to his father's path after Zenta?", "a25cc683"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nAs soon as he came to the throne he tried to take control of the state into his own hands; he ordered that the Divan (imperial council), which had not been meeting regularly, should work four days a week. In the imperial rescript he sent to the grand vizier, he said that as long as sultans plunged into amusement the people found no peace, that owing to the negligence that had prevailed since his father's time the enemy had been taking the lands of Islam, and he declared that he had made pleasure and ease forbidden to himself. He persuaded those who, out of concern for the expense, did not want him to go on campaign by saying that he would eat and drink like a soldier. He is the last Ottoman sultan to go on campaign at the head of his armies. In the years of peace after Karlowitz the budget was balanced, extraordinary taxes were abolished, the people in the war zones were exempted from the jizya for one year, and for the first time gold coins bearing the tughra (imperial monogram) were struck. The sources report him to be intelligent, mild-natured, just and versed in the learning of his time. He took calligraphy lessons from Hafız Osman and wrote poems and hymns under the pen names İkbali and Meftuni.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nIn 1697, in the choice of route that led to Zenta, the sultan too inclined toward the direction of Temeşvar; while the army was crossing the river it fell victim to a surprise attack by Prince Eugene. After Zenta he plunged, like his father, into the pleasures of the hunt and passed his time in Edirne. He allowed his tutor, the Shaykh al-Islam Feyzullah Efendi, to interfere in affairs of state; grand viziers came and went at his prompting, and one was even executed. In the five years after Karlowitz serious discontent arose among the army, the people and the ulema, and in the end, in the Edirne Incident, it targeted the sultan directly. Even as he was leaving the throne to his brother, he said that the rebels would one day depose him too and asked that they be punished.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nHis contemporary Dimitrie Cantemir gives two judgments together. On the one hand, he finds him of sounder character than previous sultans, mature and possessed of a rarely seen moderation, neither miserly nor extravagant; he writes that he won fame through the Peace of Karlowitz. On the other hand, he says that in his first five years he devoted himself entirely to affairs of state, that afterward he began to imitate his father's mistakes, and that his only success in the wars was to halt the enemy within the frontier. Silahdar Fındıklılı Mehmed Ağa wrote the Nusretname at the sultan's command. According to some western sources, the belief that he had been poisoned was widespread among the people.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nAbdülkadir Özcan divides the reign in two: the first half passed in campaigns and war, the second in relative peace and quiet. The discontent that had accumulated because of the failures and the lost territories reached such a level that even in the years of peace it targeted the sultan directly. Because of his illnesses and his grief over the latest events, he died in Edirne about five months after being deposed.", "145f2d83"], "kisa": ["The sultan who, on coming to the throne, declared pleasure and ease forbidden to himself and went on campaign at the head of the army withdrew to Edirne after Zenta, left the state to his tutor Feyzullah Efendi and lost the throne in the Edirne Incident. The same Cantemir both praises him and writes that he came to resemble his father.", "9faf6c2e"]},
"nasil-mustafa3":{"baslik": ["What did you make of Mustafa III? A far-sighted ruler, or an astrology addict who started a war?", "a8341c40"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nAccording to the TDV article, the sources show Mustafa III as a ruler of sound judgement, far-sighted, active, good-hearted, compassionate, charitable and generous; he is just, orderly and thrifty, speaks well, is a good calligrapher and reads carefully the reports that come before him. His first measures pleased the broad masses: he remitted or halved certain taxes and fees, took strict measures for the safety of the pilgrimage route, abolished the corps of halberdiers (Baltacı) of the Old Palace, which had become involved in corruption, and cut palace expenses. He made the huzur lessons (scholarly sessions in the sultan's presence) a custom, one that was kept alive until the end of the state. After the earthquake of 1766 he had the Fatih Mosque rebuilt and played a major part in reconstructing the city from the Grand Bazaar to the walls; he founded the Laleli Complex. He reformed the Tophane (cannon foundry) and the Artillery Corps, and trained Prince Selim by taking him to artillery trials.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nHis measures of economy exposed him to the charge of “miserliness”. Although he understood that the Janissary Corps needed to be put right, he did not dare make a serious attempt; when he let this necessity slip in front of the treasurer (defterdar) Hilmi Efendi, fearing that he would spread the secret, he first dismissed him and then had him executed. He was excessively devoted to ilm-i nücûm, that is, reading the future from the stars; he would not do anything without an auspicious hour and chose his viziers by looking at their stars. Against Ragıb Pasha's caution, he wanted war, trusting in his treasury; the war against Russia, opened without preparation in 1768, resulted in the burning of the fleet at Çeşme, the occupation of the Crimea and the Principalities, and the crossing of the Danube. When he saw that the treasury was exhausted, he was obliged to borrow from his son Selim and his daughter Şah Sultan.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nAccording to what TDV reports, Şem'dânîzâde, a historian of the age, writes that the “nühûset of fenn-i nücûm” (the ill omen of astrology) had descended upon the sultan. Ahmed Resmî Efendi records that during his embassy to Berlin astrologers were requested from Frederick II, and that the king attributed his success to knowledge of history, a trained army and a full treasury. In 1771, while the war was going badly, his being referred to as “ghazi” in the Friday sermon was loudly protested by some dervishes, among them Mevlevis. According to Cevdet Pasha, his passion for economy brought importance to the office of treasurer.\n\n■ WHAT DID YOU MAKE OF HIM\nThe verdict of the author of the article, Kemal Beydilli, is severe: Mustafa III did not have the education and resolve to restructure the state; his having an offer of alliance, by then far too late, conveyed to Prussia in 1763 shows that he was a politically weak statesman; he bears great responsibility for the opening of the war of 1768. Beydilli nevertheless finds it likely that, had the ulema and the statesmen opposed it, he would not have insisted on war; he sees his fault in the fact that, as the ruler who was to have the last word, he left events to his own wishes. He was a sultan who in his own poems complained of the inadequacy of his statesmen and did not think it very possible that the state would regain its former strength. The anguish of the war led to his early death; he left behind a state condemned to a disastrous peace.", "e7a42498"], "kisa": ["The sources remember him as a just, thrifty, far-sighted and generous sultan; the TDV author, however, weighs this same sultan as a politically weak statesman who bore great responsibility for the opening of the Russian war of 1768.", "af2cbcbf"]},
"nasil-mustafa4":{"baslik": ["What did you make of Mustafa IV? A foolish puppet of rebels, or an unjustly condemned sultan?", "51bb8366"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nThe TDV article lists the signs showing that Mustafa IV did not turn his back entirely on reform during his short reign. He took strict measures against those involved in the unrest and immorality in the city, regardless of whether or not they were Janissaries. Recalling the Privy Secretary Ahmed and the Kethüda (steward) Hacı İbrahim from the time of his uncle Selim, he said that had they been alive he could at once have made one of them grand vizier; he was grieved that no capable statesmen remained around him. He considered stationing trained troops in the Artillery Barracks at Taksim, took a close interest in the condition of the engineering school and set about reorganising it, and had a detailed code of regulations prepared. According to Zinkeisen, he covertly supported the idea of reviving the Nizam-i Cedid and assigned this task to Süleyman Agha, one of the former instructors of the Levend Farm.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same article does not conceal the grave facts either. From his days as a prince he had been in opposition to Selim; those close to him, including his sister Esma Sultan, worked among the Janissaries to bring him to the throne. When he came to the throne through the Kabakçı Revolt, he gave a sworn assurance that the rebels would not be called to account; but their interference in affairs of state could not be stopped, and his reign passed in such great turmoil and disintegration that people soon came to regret the deposition of Selim. On the Russian front the army disintegrated. When Alemdar arrived at the palace gate on 28 July 1808, he had the palace gates closed and ordered the killing of his cousin Selim and his brother Mahmud; Selim was murdered. It is also reported that he attacked Mahmud in person.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nAccording to the material compiled by TDV, Çelebizade Âsım, Mustafa Nuri Pasha, Cevdet Pasha, the Mufassal Osmanlı Tarihi (Detailed Ottoman History), Karal and Danişmend describe him as a supporter of the old order, weak of mind, ignorant, grasping, an intriguer and a man who did whatever the rebels wanted. Baysun, too, finds him of little judgement, unaware of the requirements of the time, avid for rule and merciless. It is reported that at the moment he was deposed he muttered, “I have not stepped down from the throne; who has put Mahmud on it?” After his death, rumours spread among the soldiers that the coffin was empty and that, if necessary, someone from the Crimean dynasty would be brought to the throne.\n\n■ WHAT DID YOU MAKE OF HIM\nThe author of the article, Kemal Beydilli, openly opposes this widespread verdict: it is clear that the epithets above are not compatible with the truth; his condemnation through negative judgements is in need of correction. According to Beydilli, Mustafa IV was deposed before he had the opportunity to take any action that would show what he really thought about the reforms; no definite answer can be given as to whether, had things calmed down, Selim's path or a reforming path like Mahmud's would have been followed. There are strong indications that, had the Rusçuk friends and Alemdar not intervened out of their fixation on restoring Selim to the throne, he too would have continued the work of renewal. As for the ambition to rule, it was not peculiar to him alone; Beydilli considers that the same ambition can be attributed to Selim as well. His end: after Alemdar's death, he was strangled on 17 November 1808 on the order of his brother Mahmud.", "29fe8921"], "kisa": ["Almost all historians condemned him as a witless, grasping and merciless sultan of the rebels. The author of the TDV article, however, writes that these epithets are not compatible with the truth and that the verdict needs to be corrected.", "4ab75579"]},
"nasil-orhan":{"baslik": ["How was Orhan Ghazi regarded? A friend, or a devious ally?", "f4ab3e17"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nThe Arab traveller Ibn Battuta, who saw Orhan in Bursa, considers him the greatest of the Turkmen sultans in wealth, territory and troops: he has about a hundred castles, spends most of his time going round and inspecting them, and does not stay in any one city for more than a month. The traveller also notes that Orhan sent him money. The contemporary Genoese statesman Lucanio dal Vermeda says that all the Genoese know of the kindness and favour they have received from Orhan, and refers to him as the brother and beloved father of the people of Pera. The endowments he assigned to his soup kitchens, madrasas and dervish lodges (zaviyes) in Bursa, İznik, Mekece and Gebze are also proof of his charity.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe harshest accusation comes from his father-in-law, the Byzantine emperor John Kantakouzenos. When Orhan's son Süleyman Pasha refused to evacuate the fortress of Tzympe, which he held in Thrace, Kantakouzenos appealed to his son-in-law Orhan; in his view Orhan was an accomplice in the affair. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam also writes that Orhan, while wishing to preserve good relations with the emperor, stalled him and did not go to the meeting, pleading illness. Patriarch Philotheos, a partisan of Kantakouzenos, for his part laments that countless Greeks (Rum) were killed and taken captive in the Turkish raids.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nGregory Palamas, Archbishop of Thessalonica, who was taken captive off Gallipoli in 1354, relates that he was brought into Orhan's presence and that Orhan arranged a religious debate between his own ulema and the bishop; when the debate grew heated, it was brought to an end. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam regards this as a sign of the importance the Ottoman administrators attached to reaching an accommodation with the Greek population. In the speech he made on withdrawing from the throne, Kantakouzenos says that the Turks surpassed Byzantium in arms, numbers and daring in war. In an inscription of 1337 Orhan is styled 'sultan al-ghuzat' (sultan of the ghazis). Among modern historians, Lowry has argued that these ghazi titles do not reflect reality.\n\n■ HOW WAS HE REGARDED\nHalil İnalcık, the author of the article, does not find Lowry's claim correct: ghaza was the basic state ideology, especially in the principalities of western Anatolia. İnalcık regards Kantakouzenos's alliance with the Ottomans as an entirely normal policy in the circumstances of the time; the rivalry between two families for the Byzantine throne was opening Thrace to the Ottomans. Orhan's support for the emperor whose son-in-law he was, his agreement with the Genoese and his search for an accommodation with his Greek subjects are parts of this picture; both Kantakouzenos's accusation and the Patriarch's lament tell of the price of the alliance on the Byzantine side.", "f66b5484"], "kisa": ["The Genoese call him their 'beloved father', while the Byzantine emperor whose son-in-law he was accuses him of being a partner in the occupation of Thrace. Both testimonies come from the same years.", "8ae9e319"]},
"nasil-osman1":{"baslik": ["How was Osman Ghazi regarded? A shepherd, or a warrior ghazi?", "b3e81b94"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nThe most reliable contemporary witness about Osman Ghazi is the Byzantine historian Pachymeres, who presents him as the most daring of the leaders raiding Byzantine territory. The warriors (alps) and ghazis of the frontier would gather under the banner of whichever leader was most successful in raids for booty; what set Osman apart from the other alps was his ability to rally them and their nökers (companions-in-arms) around himself. He distributed the places he conquered to his comrades as homelands; he consulted men learned in Islamic law, such as Edebali and Dursun Fakih, asking how newly taken places should be organised. According to Ottoman tradition, the testament he left to his son Orhan counsels him to abide by the Sharia, to look after those under his command and to be generous.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe criticism directed at Osman concerns his lineage rather than his character. In the 1380s Kadi Burhan al-Din, to belittle him, referred to him as the son of a boatman; Timur too, in the letter he wrote to Bayezid I the Thunderbolt, sought to demean the Ottoman sultan as descended from a family of Turkmen boatmen. When the ancestry of the dynasty turned into a diplomatic dispute in the time of Timur's son Shah Rukh, the court historians wrote a genealogy linking Osman to the line of the eldest son of Oghuz Khan. No contemporary source reports any personal fault; it is related that in his last years, because of gout in his foot, he left affairs to Orhan.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nThe chronicles (Aşıkpaşazade, Neşri) rest largely on the lost work of Yahşi Fakih, son of Orhan's imam; checking the place names has shown that this narrative contains accurate information. Yazıcıoğlu Ali writes that the frontier lords assembled and elected Osman khan according to Oghuz custom. Modern historians diverge: M. Fuad Köprülü regards the Kayı as the first ethnic nucleus of the state; Paul Wittek, however, argues that the dynasty had nothing to do with the Kayı tribe and that the genealogy reaching back to Oghuz Khan was produced for political ends in the reign of Murad II. The family trees in the chronicles also disagree with one another.\n\n■ HOW WAS HE REGARDED\nHalil İnalcık, the author of the article, opposes both extremes. Those who portray him as a shepherd are mistaken: Osman was a warrior ghazi (alp gazi) who, on the frontier, organised Turkmens and 'garibs' (strangers who had left their homeland) for holy war (ghaza). On the other hand, to look at the legendary material in the chronicles and treat the whole early period as fable, declaring it a 'black hole', is in his view also taking the easy way out. İnalcık's conclusion is this: the chronicles are incomplete but not empty; when they are tested against the contemporary Byzantine witness and endowment deeds, Osman can be traced as a real frontier leader.", "1a8dc1b8"], "kisa": ["The Ottoman chronicles portray him as a khan of Oghuz Khan's line, chosen by the lords; his enemies, by contrast, belittle him as a 'boatman's son'. The only contemporary witness we have is a Byzantine historian.", "a01f8ea6"]},
"nasil-osman2":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Osman II? As an early reformer, or as an inexperienced and stingy youth?", "53642cb1"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nLocal and foreign sources present him as brave, proud, envious of his ancestors' victories, and extremely skilled in the use of arms and in horsemanship. Before Khotyn he personally made great efforts in the assaults on the Polish-Cossack fortifications. Breaking with the model of a sultan cut off from the people and shut away in his palace, he adopted the type of a ghazi ruler who sought to become one with the people. An Arabic elegy written in the reign of Murad IV stresses that after the holy war he wished to perform the pilgrimage, which none of his ancestors had done, and that he had no other ulterior motive. The poems he wrote under the pen name Farisi reflect a high literary power.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same sources remember him as stingy, harsh by nature and a man not loved by the army or the ulema. In the campaign against Poland he was sparing in distributing gratuities to the troops and cut off the arpalıks (revenue grants) of the ulema; his having the soldiers' pay counted out one by one for three days angered the leading Janissaries. His impulsive behavior on the march disturbed the standing army (kapıkulu) and the viziers. At Khotyn, rivalry among the pashas and administrative incompetence prevented success. His intention to make the pilgrimage was not welcomed by the ulema; nor did his marriage to the daughter of the Shaykh al-Islam Esad Efendi improve relations between them. When the revolt began, with the inexperience of his youth, he was unable to keep a cool head.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nThe English ambassador Thomas Roe writes that his mingling with the people in ordinary clothes and raiding taverns and boza houses did not befit the majesty of a ruler and made him a man who was not feared but despised; according to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, some Ottoman sources also say this in a veiled way. Hüseyin Tugi, who witnessed the events, wrote from the standpoint of the standing army; the information he gave passed into later chronicles and fed the image of Osman as a great reformer. Karaçelebizade and Peçevi connect his intention to make the pilgrimage with the resentment born of the failure at Khotyn. Intentions such as moving the capital or abolishing the Janissaries, on the other hand, are according to the TDV propaganda of his opponents that seeped into the sources. Venetian reports relate that by marrying four freeborn women he wished to break down the tradition of the harem.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nFeridun Emecen's judgment is measured: the young sultan was easily taken in by the words of those around him and may have intended to carry out certain reforms, perhaps in order to revive the brilliance of the eras of Selim the Grim and Suleiman the Lawgiver. But the identity of a great reformer and innovator ascribed to him is bound up with the political messages of later centuries. The majesty he lost among the people is clearly visible in the conduct of the army and the ulema toward him. His tragic end was an event such as had never before been seen in Ottoman history.", "56482276"], "kisa": ["Modern historical writing remembers him as a great reformer who fell victim to the Janissaries; the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, however, says that this 'reformer' label is bound up with the political messages of 19th- and 20th-century historians. His contemporaries find him brave but stingy and harsh.", "888ffe09"]},
"nasil-osman3":{"baslik": ["What did you make of Osman III? Just and thrifty, or a man of strange temperament?", "7672334b"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nAccording to the TDV article, the point on which contemporary historians agree about Osman III is that he hated bribery and lying. That he tried to be thrifty, to act justly and to concern himself closely with the needs of the people is recounted along with illustrative incidents. It is stated that he took strict measures against banditry on the pilgrimage route and against corruption in the pious foundations (waqfs), and sent decrees against the unruly levends in Anatolia and Rumelia. He was present in person at the scene of the Hocapaşa (1755) and Cibali (1756) fires; two-thirds of the walled city burned. He had the Nuruosmaniye Complex, begun by Mahmud I, completed and opened it in 1755, had the İhsaniye quarter established in Üsküdar and gave the order for the first lighthouse at Ahırkapı. The budget continued to show a surplus; the first treaty with Denmark was signed in his time. He wrote his imperial rescripts (hatt-ı hümayun) in a fine and legible naskh hand.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same contemporaries also write that he lacked “teennî”, that is, the patience that does not act in haste: he would decide suddenly and regret it afterwards. In his three-year reign he made six new appointments to the grand vizierate and four to the office of Shaykh al-Islam. The sixty-three-day grand vizierate of Bıyıklı Ali Pasha ended in execution; Hekimoğlu Ali Pasha escaped execution only through the plea of the queen mother (valide sultan) and was sent into exile. Behind the scenes of all these changes appears the chief eunuch of the Abode of Felicity, Ebülvukūf Ahmed Agha, whom he had known since his years in the kafes. The official chronicler records that on his tours in disguise he had several non-Muslims who did not obey the dress prohibitions hanged; as for the bans he introduced on women's clothing and on migration to Istanbul, the sources of the age report that they could hardly be enforced.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nAccording to what TDV reports, the historian Şem'dânîzâde Süleyman Efendi attributes the frequent changes of office to the sultan's strange temperament and to the fact that no one could behave in a way that suited his disposition. The official court chronicler Hâkim Mehmed Efendi records the harsh punishments on his tours in disguise and the finer points of the tobacco ban; İsmâil Ziyâî writes that he went about in the dress of the ulema (religious scholars) and introduced himself as “Osman Agha of Edirne”. Some Western sources attribute his death to an operation on a tumour in his thigh. As for the widespread tale that he wore shoes studded with silver nails in the harem so as not to see the concubines, according to the author of the article no source for it has been found.\n\n■ WHAT DID YOU MAKE OF HIM\nThe author of the article, Fikret Sarıcaoğlu, does not read the frequent changes of grand vizier merely as ill temper: he considers that they may also be thought of as an attempt to reduce the role of the office of grand vizier, which had gained excessive weight in the preceding period. He writes that it is not possible to determine the mark left on his personality by the very long years spent in the kafes; he nevertheless adds that it is possible to look for traces of his years of confinement in ordinary behaviour such as mingling with the people in disguise or eating on the road the fruit he had bought on an outing. He also finds the reports that the heir apparent, Prince Mehmed, had been poisoned on his orders to be unfounded, on the grounds that the man alleged to have been responsible became grand vizier and a prospective imperial son-in-law in the following reign. The portrait that emerges: the honest but impatient sultan of a short, quiet reign.", "359631ab"], "kisa": ["A sultan who hated bribery and lying, was thrifty and took an interest in the people; but also one who changed six grand viziers in three years, made sudden decisions and regretted them afterwards. Contemporary historians agree on both.", "b6f0acbc"]},
"nasil-selim1":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Selim the Grim? As a shedder of blood, or as a just ruler?", "10596620"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nOttoman sources remember him as a man who followed affairs of state closely, wanted whatever he planned to be done at once, and was extremely disciplined and determined; he was true to his word, courteous by nature and elegant in speech. He disliked pomp and dressed simply. He read a great deal and had a deep interest in history: in Egypt he had Kemalpaşazade translate Ibn Taghribirdi's history. He knew Persian very well and has a divan (collected poems) of his Persian verse. He made it a principle to take the opinions of scholars and statesmen on political matters. He had the body of the Mamluk sultan Qansuh al-Ghawri, whom he had defeated at Marj Dabiq, found and buried in Aleppo, and had prayers recited for his soul. From Tabriz he sent about a thousand men of learning and art to Istanbul.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same sources also write that he had a harsh temperament that did not shrink from shedding blood. He took the throne by deposing his father through the pressure of the Janissaries; afterward he had his nephews put to death, and then his brothers Korkut and Ahmed. He had some members of a Mamluk embassy killed on suspicion of an assassination plot and sent the ambassador back with grave insults. In the Divan (imperial council) he often grew angry with the pashas and sometimes walked out of the meeting. The historian Celalzade Mustafa Çelebi, who was in his service, reports that on his deathbed Selim told Pîrî Mehmed Pasha that he had greatly oppressed him. In western sources he is known above all for his mercilessness and bloodshed.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nAccording to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, although western sources find him merciless, some ambassadorial reports bring his justice to the fore. The accounts of his close confidant Hasan Can passed into the Tâcü't-tevârîh of the latter's son, Hoca Sadeddin; Kemalpaşazade, İdris-i Bitlisi, Celalzade and Âlî also include anecdotes about his deeds. His fame gave rise to the genre called 'Selimname', written specifically in his name. The TDV corrects two widespread beliefs: the claim that he had 40,000 Kizilbash killed in Anatolia is not true; according to recent studies, severe punishments were meted out mostly to Safavid propagandists and the members of the dervish lodges that supported them, and some were exiled. The story that he took over the caliphate at a ceremony in Hagia Sophia also has no historical value.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nAccording to Feridun Emecen, the author of the article, the eight-year reign is a turning point in Ottoman history. His averting of the Safavid threat and his bringing to the fore a strict Sunni outlook heralded an important transformation in political and social life; his putting an end to the Mamluk Sultanate was the first step in a project to gather the Islamic world under a single banner. Emecen notes that some of the anecdotes about him have been passed on by certain authors as historical fact, and that most of them must have arisen from his distinctive conduct, which could not be compared with that of the other sultans.", "3d108e8f"], "kisa": ["Westerners wrote of his mercilessness, some ambassadors of his justice. He himself said on his deathbed that he had greatly oppressed his vizier, but that he had done so for the good of the state and the people.", "1b171831"]},
"nasil-selim2":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Selim II? As magnanimous, or as dissolute?", "e443d100"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nAccording to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the sources of the period present him as a generous, magnanimous ruler who did not wish to hurt anyone's feelings. He liked to have scholars and poets around him; he showed great respect to the Shaykh al-Islam Ebussuud Efendi. The poems he wrote under the pen names Selimi and Talibi are counted among the fine examples of divan literature. Like his ancestors he was fond of hunting, could draw the stiffest bows and was a skilled archer. Mimar Sinan's Selimiye Mosque in Edirne, the repair of Hagia Sophia and the porticoes of the Masjid al-Haram in Mecca are his works. He closely followed the work on the new fleet, which was built in a short time; he greeted the capture of Tunis with joy.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same sources also remember him as fond of pleasure and amusement and a regular at drinking gatherings. Âlî Mustafa Efendi writes that while he was a prince he had with him more than twenty-seven poets, boon companions and conversationalists, and that he spent his days in poetry, music, eating and drinking, and conversation. Whereas his father often went out to the Friday procession, he neglected it; Fresne-Canaye of the French embassy saw him leave the palace for Friday prayers only twice in three months. After the Janissary revolt in the first days of his reign, he left all affairs to Sokollu Mehmed Pasha. Before his death, according to Selaniki, he had repented of drink and revelry.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nBusbecq, who was gathering information during the struggle among the princes, describes him as a man resembling his mother, addicted to drinking, not at all courteous, lazy and liked by no one; the soldiers, he says, used to mock him. In 1553 the Venetian Navagero calls him miserly, dissolute and two-faced. Fresne-Canaye writes that he saw the sultan in the garden at Seraglio Point amusing himself with his acrobats, mutes and dwarfs, his cheeks swollen from drink. S. Gerlach attributes his death to his drinking an excessive amount of water after eating a great deal of mutton sausage; another account reported by the TDV is that he fell ill when he suddenly gave up drinking. In Âlî Mustafa Efendi's portrayal, by contrast, there is a sultan who stood out for his imposing bearing and who spoke with a lisp.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nThe point stressed by Feridun Emecen, the author of the article, concerns the institution more than the personality: Selim II is the first sultan born in Istanbul who ascended the throne, and the first Ottoman ruler who went on no campaign at all during his eight-year reign. According to Emecen, his not appearing before the people at the Friday procession and not going on campaign at the head of the army began a sharp change in the conception of the sultanate. Even so, Emecen adds that although he left affairs to Sokollu, he did not refrain from closely following and intervening in the matters that caught his attention.", "5af6b1bf"], "kisa": ["Ottoman sources remember him as a generous, magnanimous ruler who did not wish to hurt anyone's feelings; western ambassadors call him 'miserly, dissolute, two-faced'. As the first sultan never to go on campaign, he also changed the conception of the sultanate.", "bd1cac6e"]},
"nasil-selim3":{"baslik": ["What did you make of Selim III? The renewer of his nation, or a ruler “as pliant as water”?", "25ee85fe"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nAccording to the ambassadors' impressions reported in the TDV article, Selim III was a man of calm manner, mild character and charitable disposition; he was a ruler who worked with great seriousness for the state to regain its former strength, who had devoted himself to his nation and believed that he had been sent for its salvation. The printing press was an important occupation of his: he himself determined the books to be printed and rewarded the craftsmen. He had Vauban's works translated and printed, and took enough interest in the science of war to write a treatise on rockets and cannon. He had a military engineering school established in Hasköy (1795) and large barracks in Levend and Üsküdar; he developed the Selimiye district. According to the author of the article, the reform programme was not only military but comprehensive enough to embrace all branches of civil administration. He was a poet with a divan (collected poems) under the pen name “İlhâmî”, a Mevlevi and the patron of Şeyh Galib; according to the music section of the TDV article, no Ottoman sultan's love of music and skill as a composer reached his level.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nThe same article openly sets out his weaknesses. His mild temper, tolerance and forgiving nature were human virtues; but for a statesman embarking on radical reform they were a serious weakness that endangered the lives of those who had set out with him. He did not show a firm line of character and was described as “su gibi meyyal” (as pliant as water); in time no one feared him any longer, and he lost his majesty. In 1807, without using the trained troops at his disposal, he gave up everything without a fight; in the hope of remaining on the throne, he abolished the New Revenue Treasury with his own hand and delivered his reform team to the executioner. He himself, after leaving the throne, said with remorse, “My forbearance is the cause of all this.”\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nAccording to what TDV reports, in 1789 the Prussian ambassador Diez saw him as superior to his nation in his qualities and destined to be its renewer, but added that long years were needed for a state that had been declining for a hundred years. The Prussian ambassador Knobelsdorf (1792) gives the number of the circle conducting the reform work as forty; Olivier argues that this “inner cabinet” divided authority. As for Zinkeisen's comment that he ought to have left matters to a strong grand vizier, the author of the article finds it invalid, a judgement made under the impact of the defeat. After he was killed, his virtues were long recounted like a legend in the coffee houses of Istanbul.\n\n■ WHAT DID YOU MAKE OF HIM\nThe verdict of the author of the article, Kemal Beydilli, is twofold. On the one hand: time showed that, despite his good intentions, he lacked the firmness of character, perseverance and foresight needed to sustain this great undertaking for years; the general opinion is that he did not have the personality to see his task through. On the other: before fifteen years had passed, people began to concede that Selim, whom the opposition had attacked in harsh words, had been right; the suppression of the Greek revolt in the Morea within a few months by Egyptian forces trained in the Western manner laid before everyone's eyes the value of the army he had wished to create.", "a7537d09"], "kisa": ["An enlightened reformer devoted to his nation; but a sultan who, because of his mildness, delivered those who had set out with him to the executioner and in the end abandoned his work with his own hand. That he had been right, however, was conceded before fifteen years had passed since his killing.", "291d1355"]},
"nasil-suleyman1":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Suleiman the Lawgiver? As the sultan of a golden age, or as the hero of a myth?", "2ed36da2"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nContemporary Ottoman and western sources describe him in similar terms. In 1520 a Venetian ambassador depicts him as an amiable and good-natured young man; in 1553 another Venetian ambassador writes that he had won renown for being very just, that he did wrong to no one, and that he was more devoted to his religion than his ancestors had been. The Süleymannames praise him as a ghazi sultan, a just ruler, a protector of Islam and a patron of literature and art; they describe him as generous, refined, modest and dervish-like in manner. The historian Celalzade Mustafa Çelebi stresses three of his qualities: his justice, his being a protector of the people, and his being a conqueror on a world scale. He was not impulsive; he reached his decisions by discussing them with his viziers and convening consultative councils. He wrote poetry under the pen name Muhibbi; in his time poets such as Baki and Fuzuli and scholars such as Ebussuud enjoyed patronage. Even the Safavid Shah Tahmasp had greeted his holy war with admiration.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nHe held the interest of the state above everything and did not hesitate to sacrifice his family for its sake: the deaths of his sons Mustafa and Bayezid are cited as examples of this. According to a Venetian source, his making Hürrem his wife through a formal marriage contract, contrary to custom, did not please the people; most Ottoman historians write that the faction made up of Hürrem, Mihrimah and Rüstem Pasha played a role in the affair of Prince Mustafa, and they accuse the sultan of having fallen under their influence. His making it commonplace for viziers to be sons-in-law of the dynasty later became the target of sharp criticism by Koçi Bey. The financial strain of long campaigns and a religious hardening fed social reaction; an anonymous treatise written in the 1550s and Lutfi Pasha's Asafname describe the decay in the institutions already in those years.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nLater generations regarded his era as an ideal age; this idea grew stronger in years of crisis and in time turned into the concept of a 'golden age'. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, 19th-century Ottoman historians, with the tendency to idealize that they had learned from their European colleagues, saw the age of the Lawgiver as a kind of Age of Felicity (the era of the Prophet). The TDV considers a weak contrivance the count found in some sources seeking to give him a mystical image: 'ten viziers, ten scholars and ten poets of the tenth sultan'.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nAccording to Feridun Emecen, the author of the article, this age truly left its mark on the 16th century: the Ottomans became the world-scale empire of Europe, assumed a decisive role in the European political balance, and the Lawgiver won a place among the rulers of his time that bore no comparison with theirs. But Emecen says that the myth that formed around the epithet 'the Lawgiver' has overshadowed a dispassionate view; this period, in which the great victories have been brought to the fore, should be assessed with a cooler approach.", "0b293611"], "kisa": ["Even when his grandson was on the throne, people looked back on his era with longing. But the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam reminds us that a myth was built around the name 'the Lawgiver' and that social tension and decay also began in those same years.", "ea80b6f6"]},
"nasil-suleyman2":{"baslik": ["How do you remember Süleyman II? As a well-meaning, dervish-like man, or as a fragile sultan in the hands of centers of power?", "65ec4372"], "metin": ["■ PRAISE\nThe sources describe him as a well-meaning sultan, far from dissipation, fond of bestowing favors, pious and dervish-like. He came to the throne amid the devastation that followed the rout at Vienna; although he was ill, he went on campaign in a litter as far as Sofia. When Niš fell, the palace aghas and the ulema demanded the dismissal of the grand vizier; he pointed out the danger of changing the commander-in-chief while the enemy was facing them and rejected this. He supported Köprülüzade Fazıl Mustafa Pasha and stood behind his financial measures; in this period Niš and Vidin were recovered, as was Belgrade after an eight-day siege. The Sancakburnu fortress, destroyed in the İzmir earthquake, and the Eyüp Sultan Mosque, damaged by fire, were repaired in his time. As a prince he took calligraphy lessons from Tokatlı Ahmed Efendi and showed proficiency in the thuluth and naskh scripts.\n\n■ CRITICISM\nBecause of his long life of imprisonment he could not receive a good education; he had a fragile nature and was for the most part under the influence of the centers of power in the palace. His placing, despite Köprülüzade's warning, a woman steward (kethüda) at the head of the Harem after his mother's death and assigning her a has (crown revenue grant) was a practice without precedent. When some of the women and aghas in the Harem said that Mehmed IV, too, had been taken to Istanbul and then deposed, he was seized with apprehension and wished to stay in Edirne; he was persuaded only through a consultative council. The first years of his reign passed in turmoil, in which brigands such as Yeğen Osman defied him and Skopje in the Balkans was burned and looted.\n\n■ CONTEMPORARIES AND HISTORIANS\nThe sources report that when people came to raise him to the throne, he thought he was going to be killed, asked permission to perform his prayers, and said that rather than dying every day for forty years he considered it better to die one day sooner. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam interprets his giving the grand vizier, as he welcomed him on his return from Belgrade, his own fur robe, dagger and aigrette and praying for him in tears as a reflection of his helplessness in the face of the news of failure coming from the front and of his state of mind. The sources record that before his death he received the grand vizier alone and prayed in tears for the army's victory. Physically he is described as resembling his ancestors, with an aquiline nose and a large build.\n\n■ HOW DO YOU REMEMBER HIM?\nThe picture drawn by Abdülkadir Özcan is one of understanding mingled with sorrow: a sultan who bore the marks of forty years in the 'kafes' (the princes' confinement), well-meaning but lacking the education and the will to carry the burden. The bright moments of his reign sprang less from himself than from his trust in Fazıl Mustafa Pasha. His reign, which lasted three years and eight months, ended in Edirne with the dropsy from which he had long suffered; he left no children.", "d4b7f846"], "kisa": ["The sultan who lived for forty years in the 'kafes' (the princes' confinement) and came to the throne thinking he was being led to his death went on campaign in his short reign with his ailing body and saw Belgrade recovered. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam remembers him as well-meaning but uneducated and open to influence.", "25dde147"]},
"nedim-lale-devri":{"baslik": ["Nedîm comes to the fore as the pioneer of Tulip Era poetry", "d3c40dbb"], "metin": ["Throughout the Tulip Era of 1718-1730, Nedîm developed a new style of poetry with his simplicity of expression and witty manner. This style, which grew up in the circle of Nevşehirli Damad İbrahim Pasha, is considered the greatest representative of the \"localization\" current in divan poetry — in place of abstract conceits, the daily life of Istanbul and its culture of entertainment entered poetry.", "608e220a"], "sanatci.ad": ["Nedîm (original name Ahmed)", "60c38afb"], "sanatci.hayat": ["He was born in Istanbul, probably in 1092 (1681); his father was Kadı Mehmed Efendi, grandson of a kazasker (chief military judge), and his mother belonged to the Karaçelebizâde family. He died in 1143 (1730).", "040eee15"], "sanatci.onem": ["In TDV's description, \"the poet who created a style in divan poetry known by his own name\" — with his plain and witty manner he became the best-known name of the Tulip Era.", "8d247a98"]},
"nedim-olumu":{"baslik": ["The death of Nedîm during the Patrona Halil revolt", "06bf909e"], "metin": ["Nedîm lost his life during the Patrona Halil revolt of 1730; according to the sources, he died either after falling ill from the terror of the revolt or by falling from the roof of his house while trying to escape — the sources diverge on the details of how he died. With his death the brightest voice of the Tulip Era in poetry also fell silent; the era itself ended with the same revolt.", "d255366d"], "sanatci.ad": ["Nedîm (original name Ahmed)", "60c38afb"], "sanatci.hayat": ["He was born in Istanbul, probably in 1092 (1681); his father was Kadı Mehmed Efendi, grandson of a kazasker (chief military judge), and his mother belonged to the Karaçelebizâde family. He died in 1143 (1730).", "040eee15"], "sanatci.onem": ["In TDV's description, \"the poet who created a style in divan poetry known by his own name\" — with his plain and witty manner he became the best-known name of the Tulip Era.", "8d247a98"]},
"nefi-idami":{"baslik": ["Nef'î is executed because of his satires", "5e459c60"], "metin": ["Nef'î, known for his satires, was executed in 1635 on the orders of Murad IV because of his collection of satires, Sihâm-ı Kazâ (Arrows of Fate). Grand Vizier Bayram Pasha handed him over; Nef'î was strangled in the palace woodshed and his body thrown into the sea — the enmities he had earned with his pen brought about his end.", "8999f9d1"], "sanatci.ad": ["Nef'î (original name Ömer)", "6231c83f"], "sanatci.hayat": ["He is estimated to have been born around 980 (1572) in the Pasinler (Hasankale) district of Erzurum; he came from the family of a sanjak governor (sanjakbey). He died in 1044 (1635).", "21fd5d24"], "sanatci.onem": ["In TDV's description, \"a divan poet famous for his satires and odes\" — with his powerful and assertive style he is considered the most remarkable name in Ottoman satirical literature.", "d42cd3f1"]},
"osman2-hac-niyeti-katl-1622":{"soru": ["Why did Young Osman's intention to make the pilgrimage cost him his life?", "20f7a968"], "baslik": ["The torn fatwa and Yedikule", "dd96def6"], "metin": ["When Osman II began preparing for the pilgrimage five months after returning from Khotyn, rumours spread that he would gather troops from Anatolia to do away with the Janissaries and would move the capital; the TDV regards these largely as the propaganda of his opponents. According to an account in Venetian reports, when the Shaykh al-Islam Esad Efendi could not persuade him to build a mosque instead of going on the pilgrimage, he issued a fatwa that for the sultan to rule with justice was better than the pilgrimage; it is also alleged that the sultan, spurred into action again by a dream, tore up the fatwa. Seized in the revolt that broke out at the news that the tents had been moved across to Üsküdar, the sultan was strangled at Yedikule on 20 May 1622. Some historians write that, as proof of his death, his ear and nose were cut off and shown to Sultan Mustafa's mother.", "a73f72ac"], "not": ["The tearing of the fatwa and the detail of the ear and nose appear in the sources with the qualification \"it is alleged\" or \"according to some historians\"; the killing itself is certain.", "38bf59fb"]},
"patrona-halil-derdi-neydi":{"baslik": ["What was Patrona Halil's grievance: the rebels' demands and the calculations behind them", "86e739d9"], "metin": ["The declared cause. On 25 September 1730 the rebels rose up saying 'Our case is one under the sharia'. A few days before the revolt, in notes they sent to the Shaykh al-Islam and the preacher of Hagia Sophia, and in a paper they left at the Orta Mosque, they had hinted at their intention by writing 'We want a sultan who is Mahmûdü'l-hısâl (of praiseworthy character)'.\n\nTHE DEMANDS, in order.\n① The surrender of thirty-seven persons, headed by the grand vizier, the Shaykh al-Islam and the steward (kethüda) of the grand vizierate.\n② After the executions, the deposition of Ahmed III for the sake of their own safety.\n③ From the new sultan, the abolition of the excessive taxes laid on the people and of the malikâne (life-term tax farm) system.\n④ Then offices: those of kul kethüdası (steward of the Janissary corps), grand vizier, deputy grand vizier (kaymakam) and Kapudan Pasha (grand admiral).\n\nTHE SOURCE of the anger. According to TDV, the revolt was a reaction to a series of developments reaching back to Passarowitz: the loss of Belgrade and Temeşvar, the grand vizier's falling out with the war party because of his peaceable policy, the failure in the Persian war that had been opened reluctantly, the imdadiyye (aid) taxes that became permanent, the tax burden on the artisan guilds, the unemployment created by migration from village to city and the extravagant spending of statesmen. Details: lale-devri-tepkiler.\n\nTHE CALCULATION behind it. TDV attributes the preparation of the revolt to Kaymakam (Kapudan Pasha) Mustafa Pasha, who coveted the grand vizierate: he gathered supporters through gifts and entrusted the job to Patrona Halil, whom he had earlier saved from execution. İbrahim Pasha, for his part, pointed to this son-in-law as responsible for the events and asked for his execution. The absence of Mustafa Pasha's name from the rebels' first list increased suspicions; in the end he too was handed over together with the other two viziers.\n\nWasn't it a PERSONAL cause? Halil's refusing the 100,000 gold coins offered to him by saying 'All the wealth of Istanbul is already mine' looks like a stance of principle; but TDV also writes that within a few weeks he acquired a fortune of 3000 purses and had appointments given to his supporters.\n\nTWO readings. TDV's article on the Tulip Era stresses that part of the ulema stoked the discontent but that the uprising broke out for political reasons; the article on the Patrona Revolt brings forward the economic and social accumulation. The two do not exclude each other.", "a9ec4d22"], "kisa": ["They came out saying 'Our case is one under the sharia' and asked first for the heads of 37 people, then for 'a sultan of fine character'; but according to TDV the strings of the revolt lay in one vizier's ambition for the grand vizierate.", "a190dd46"], "bag": ["Significance: the demands were proclaimed in the language of the 'sharia', but the concrete requests concerned taxes, persons and offices — showing that the revolt carried a political-economic rather than an ideological calculation.", "066cb274"]},
"patrona-isyancilarin-yaptiklari":{"baslik": ["The rebels' Istanbul: looting, dragged corpses, demolished kiosks (October–November 1730)", "9f774219"]},
"ruslara-niye-direnilemedi-1806-1812":{"baslik": ["Why could the Ottomans not hold out against the Russians in 1806-1812 — was it the army, technology, logistics or internal strife?", "25c658f9"], "ozet": ["In the summer of 1810 Sohum was lost, and in the autumn Rusçuk (Ruse). There is no one-sentence answer to the question \"Was the Russian army larger, or technically more advanced?\": the sources consulted point to at least five separate causes, and these feed one another. The card does not declare any of them to be the sole cause.", "3f5aaac8"], "metin": ["Cause ① — the throne changed hands twice while the war was going on. The war began in November 1806; in May 1807 the Kabakçı Revolt overthrew the sultan, in July 1808 a counter-coup overthrew the second sultan and brought in a third, and in November 1808 the grand vizier was killed. The TDV article `mustafa-iv` records how this was reflected at the front: \"The events in Istanbul spread here too and caused an upheaval; the Janissaries rebelled, the grand vizier was chased away, the army's senior officials fled, the agha of the Janissaries, Pehlivan Agha, who was handling these affairs, was killed by his own comrades, and Çelebi Mustafa Pasha was appointed grand vizier, but order could not be restored. The turmoil within the army led to a great weakness in the face of the Russians.\" Moreover, the time gained was spent not on the enemy but on internal score-settling: the one-year armistice made at Slobozia on 24 August 1807 was \"used not for taking the measures necessary to bring the war to a successful conclusion, but for the internal struggle caused by the change of sultan.\"\n\nCAUSE ② — the only modern army available was not committed to the war, and was then destroyed. Selim III had his New Order (Nizâm-ı Cedîd) army; although there were more than 10,000 trained soldiers in Istanbul in 1807, the sultan did not use them even against the revolt and abolished the corps with his own hand (TDV `kabakci-isyani`). A study examining the organisation of the campaign through the sharia court registers identifies the same gap: \"Considering that this war came on the eve of the New Order system introduced by Selim III, one would expect New Order soldiers to have taken part in it. However, Selim III did not use these soldiers in this war\" (Karakaya 2020, p. 116). As for its successor, the new Sekban corps (Sekbân-ı Cedîd) founded in 1808, it lived for three months: on 16 November its barracks were burned, its aghas were killed and the survivors scattered (TDV `sekban-i-cedid`). The TDV article `yeniceri` states the real structural deadlock in a single sentence: between the new and old armies a \"relentless enmity\" had developed, \"so much so that even in times of need it was never possible to use these two forces together.\"\n\nCAUSE ③ — who raised the soldiers: not the centre but the provinces. The manpower as measured by the same study: \"In the research carried out on the 1806-1812 Ottoman-Russian war, it is seen that the average number of soldiers remained around 60,000\", and at the front \"alongside the soldiers of the standing army (kapıkulu), it is seen that the troops of the ayan in particular were used in the war\" (Karakaya 2020, p. 116). The contribution of one Anatolian district (Safranbolu) is entered in the court registers in the form of demands for soldiers, avârız and imdâd-ı seferiyye taxes (extraordinary and campaign-aid levies), mules and sheep — that is, the army was assembled not through a central system of mobilisation but from soldiers and provisions gathered district by district. This is also the reason for the political weight of the ayan: the state depended on them for taxes, public order and soldiers (TDV `ayan`).\n\nCAUSE ④ — money. The revenue of the New Revenue Treasury (Îrâd-ı Cedîd Hazinesi), set up to finance the reform, had reached 64,500 purses in 1797-98; but because the growth of the new army \"increased the burden on the population of Anatolia and Rumelia, and the people were crushed under heavy taxes\", it fed the opposition, and when the New Order was abolished, \"one of the first things done was the plundering of the revenues transferred to the New Revenue Treasury\" (TDV `nizam-i-cedid`). In other words, in the middle of the war the army's source of finance was broken up.\n\nCAUSE ⑤ — several fronts and diplomacy at the same time. The war was not on a single front: the pilgrimage routes had been closed by the Wahhabi revolt, the British fleet had appeared before Istanbul in February 1807 (TDV `kabakci-isyani`), and in the Caucasus local powers had changed sides — in Abkhazia, Sefer Bey, the son of Keleş Bey and a Russian subject, wrote letters to the tsar in 1808 and 1809 stating that he wished to submit to him, and on 17 February 1810 the Russians proclaimed that Abkhazia had been joined to Russia (TDV `sohum`).\n\nAND the outcome can be read in two cities. Sohum Fortress \"fell into Russian hands on 11 July 1810 because sufficient military support could not be provided at that time\" (TDV `sohum`). Rusçuk, by contrast, \"withstood a long and bloody siege carried out by the Russian armies under the command of Count Kaminski; on 26 September 1810 it surrendered on condition that the garrison and the civilians could leave the city in safety\" (TDV `ruscuk`). Read together, the two do not yield the picture that \"Russian soldiers were better\": Rusçuk held out for months, and the following year the Russians were forced to withdraw \"after the heavy defeats and setbacks suffered in June 1811\". The issue, then, is not technical superiority in a single battle but the capacity to sustain a war: an uninterrupted supply of soldiers, money, command and political stability.\n\nWhat THIS card does not say. No quantified comparison of the Russian army with the Ottoman army of the period in terms of muskets, artillery, training and the education of officers was found in the sources consulted. The statement \"their weapons were better\" is too strong a claim to be written without being measured; the five causes above rest on measurable records, while the sixth (technical superiority) could not be measured.", "04d3cab8"], "bag": ["View: The cause was military — the old army was untrained and mutinous, and the new army was destroyed almost as soon as it was established. Counter-view: The cause was political — a state that went through two depositions, one killing of a ruler and an assassination of a grand vizier within six years could not possibly have maintained continuity at the front; the army was a result of this instability, not its cause. Third reading: both are secondary, and the real issue was financial and logistical capacity — soldiers were being gathered district by district, money was dissipated through the plundering of the reform treasury, and distant fortresses (Sohum) were falling because they could not get support. The sources present these three readings not as substitutes for one another but as intertwined.", "0b65dbbc"]},
"savas-93-harbi-1877-1878":{"baslik": ["The War of '93 — the Russo-Ottoman War of 1877-1878", "8a0cf459"], "tarih_metin": ["Declaration of war 24 April 1877 · Fall of Plevna 10 December 1877 · Treaty of San Stefano 3 March 1878", "8df6f08b"], "yer": ["Balkan (Danube) front — Plevna, Shipka Pass; Caucasus front — Kars, Erzurum", "59d469ce"], "oncesi": ["After the Treaty of Paris (1856), Russia, pursuing a Pan-Slavist foreign policy, encouraged the Balkan peoples to revolt against the Ottomans; the allegation that tens of thousands of Bulgarians had been killed in the suppression of the Bulgarian Uprising that broke out in May 1876 gave the war a religious/humanitarian legitimacy in the eyes of European public opinion. In addition, the growing burden of the Ottoman foreign debt had also turned European public opinion against the state. Russia declared war on 24 April 1877.", "bbea8a50"], "akis": ["On the Danube front, the Ottoman forces under the command of Gazi Osman Pasha established a fortified position at Plevna and resisted for months; in September 1877 the repeated joint assaults of the Russian and Romanian forces came to nothing. But at the end of the long siege provisions and ammunition ran out, and Osman Pasha was forced to surrender on 10 December 1877. This resistance delayed the course of the war but could not change its outcome; the Russian army crossed the Balkans and advanced as far as Edirne, and on the Caucasus front as far as Erzurum.", "7c436b2a"], "sonuc": ["The guns fell silent with the Armistice of Edirne of 31 January 1878, and on 3 March 1878 the Treaty of San Stefano (Ayastefanos) was signed: a \"Principality of Greater Bulgaria\" with wide borders was established, the independence of Serbia, Montenegro and Romania was officially recognised for the first time, a large territory in eastern Anatolia including Erzurum was ceded to Russia, and the Ottomans agreed to pay Russia a war indemnity of 802,500,000 francs. The human cost of the war was heavy: hundreds of thousands of Muslim refugees (muhacir) fleeing from Bulgaria and the Caucasus sought shelter in Istanbul and Anatolia. Britain, disturbed by such a great Russian expansion, exerted diplomatic pressure to soften the harsh provisions — in return it took over the administration of Cyprus; some of the harsh provisions were revised a few months later at the Congress of Berlin (1878).", "742b09c1"], "tartisma": ["Only the original provisions of San Stefano have been described in this card; the extent to which the Treaty of Berlin changed San Stefano (such as the reduction of Greater Bulgaria) has not been separately verified, as the body of the TDV text that was read does not give this detail.", "56bc0f69"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman State", "86202f73"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["At Plevna, Gazi Osman Pasha", "a89259b4"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Russia, Romania, Serbia, Montenegro", "b57f43c4"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-bursa-1326":{"baslik": ["The Surrender of Bursa (6 April 1326)", "2789bfbd"], "tarih_metin": ["2 Jumada al-Awwal 726 · 6 April 1326", "814cf92f"], "yer": ["Bursa (Prusa), at the northern foot of Mount Uludağ", "9f2737c8"], "oncesi": ["When the alliance of the tekfurs (Byzantine lords) of the Bursa plain was defeated at the Dimbos (Dinboz) pass, the city was left on its own. Instead of attacking the walls, Osman Gazi chose to cut the city off from its surroundings: he had two blockade towers named Aktimur and Balabancık built to keep watch on Bursa, gave one of them to his nephew Aktimur and withdrew his forces. The Turkmen and Kızık villages on the slopes of Uludağ are thought to have been founded during these long years of blockade. Unable to obtain help from outside, the townspeople were gradually driven into hunger and poverty.", "35db8ed6"], "akis": ["The decisive outcome was brought not by a pitched battle but by negotiations for surrender. Orhan, who had taken over the headship of the beylik in the spring of 1326, came before the walls with all his forces and called on the city to surrender. According to the TDV article on Orhan, the agreement (ahidname) reached with the tekfur had three conditions: the soldiers entering the city would not plunder or take captives; those who wished to leave could take their property and depart under the protection of Ottoman soldiers; and 30,000 gold coins would be paid to Orhan. As Aşıkpaşazade relates it, when the tekfur left the city, Ahi Hasan was the first to climb onto the tower of the walls, and then the Muslims settled in the city.", "53986afa"], "sonuc": ["The Byzantine commander was allowed to go to Istanbul; his chief adviser, who had brought about the surrender, stayed in the city and entered Ottoman service, and the metropolitan of Bursa also continued in his office. The Greek population was moved to the quarters below the citadel, and only Turks were settled inside the citadel. Orhan converted the monastery within the citadel into a mosque, had the Bey's Palace (Bey Sarayı) built on the Tophane ridge and in 1327 had the first silver akçe of the beylik struck here. The complex consisting of a mosque, soup kitchen, madrasa, bathhouse and inn that he founded east of the citadel in 1339-1340 became the nucleus of the city's new market centre. Bursa was made the centre of the beylik, and the tombs of the early rulers from Osman Gazi to the Conqueror also rose here.", "b333ed51"], "tartisma": ["The beginning of the blockade is given differently in two TDV articles: the article on Osman I places the battle of Dimbos in 1303 and says that the city remained under siege for twenty-three years; the article on Bursa, however, places the tekfur's defeat at the Dinboz pass and the beginning of the siege in 1308 and speaks of a blockade lasting \"more than ten years\". The day of the surrender (6 April 1326) is the same in both articles.", "4617aae7"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman Beylik", "d8cb2121"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Orhan Bey", "6726cdef"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["The fortress of Bursa, subject to Byzantium", "624fdda3"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["The tekfur of Bursa (his name does not appear in the sources)", "5b66491e"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-canakkale-bozgunu-1656":{"baslik": ["The Dardanelles Rout (26 June / 13 July 1656)", "818d581e"], "tarih_metin": ["26 June 1656 (the day the naval battle began) — the losses/withdrawal continued until 13 July 1656 (the atlas's date of change)", "832807db"], "yer": ["The Dardanelles, the narrow passage between Kilitbahir and Kal'a-i Sultaniyye", "08080fdf"], "oncesi": ["In the tenth year of the Cretan War (1645-), Venice was continuing its strategy of regularly blockading the Dardanelles in order to cut off Ottoman supplies to the island. In 1654 Kapudan Pasha (Grand Admiral) Kara Murad Pasha had defeated the Venetian fleet in the narrow passage between Kilitbahir and Kal'a-i Sultaniyye and temporarily lifted the blockade — but this victory did not last; Venice gathered its strength and massed once more before the strait.", "b08492aa"], "akis": ["On 26 June 1656 the two fleets met in the strait. The TDV article `canakkale-bogazi` does not give the details of the engagement (tactics, duration); it records only the result: the Ottoman fleet of eighty-six vessels was routed by the Venetians. In the period that lasted until 13 July, the card's own date of change, the fleet dispersed, Istanbul's sea link to the Mediterranean was in effect cut, and Venice occupied Tenedos (Bozcaada) and Lemnos.", "9b1a0869"], "sonuc": ["When the Strait came under Venetian blockade, the flow of provisions (especially grain) reaching Istanbul by sea was cut off; prices rose in the city and unrest grew among the people. In this atmosphere of military and administrative crisis, on 26 September 1656 Turhan Sultan, mother of Mehmed IV, brought Köprülü Mehmed Pasha, an elderly and stern administrator, to the grand vizierate — according to the TDV article `koprulu-mehmed-pasa`, this appointment was an attempt to halt the systematic weakening of the Ottomans against Venice. As soon as Köprülü took office, he also launched a counter-operation at sea: in 1657 a new naval battle of the Dardanelles lasting three days ended in an Ottoman victory (after the enemy flagship blew up when hit by a cannonball fired from Kumburnu), the blockade was lifted, and in the same year Tenedos and Lemnos were retaken.", "7f08b982"], "tartisma": ["The TDV article that was read (`canakkale-bogazi`) does not give the tactical detail of the battle (how the rout came about); it reports only the result. For this reason the card does not answer the question \"how was it lost\"; it relates only \"what happened and what followed\".", "88e6e97c"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman State", "86202f73"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Kapudan Pasha (Grand Admiral) Sarı Kenan Pasha", "8dd1e8c4"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["eighty-six ships: fifty galleys, nine galleasses (mavna), twenty-seven galleons", "a51e2ab6"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Republic of Venice", "f30b04e7"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["not named in the TDV article that was read", "dfd87a77"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-kirim-savasi-1853-1856":{"baslik": ["The Crimean War (1853-1856)", "9a0abab0"], "tarih_metin": ["Declaration of war 4 October 1853 · Raid on Sinop 30 November 1853 · Treaty of Paris 30 March 1856", "d0edee21"], "yer": ["Danube front, Caucasus front, the Crimean peninsula (siege of Sevastopol), the Black Sea", "c80acecb"], "oncesi": ["Russia, using as a pretext the question of control over the Christian holy places in Jerusalem (against Catholic France), claimed a right of protection over the Orthodox subjects in Ottoman territory, and when this demand was rejected it declared war on 4 October 1853.", "ae3755e2"], "akis": ["The first great blow of the war came at sea: on 30 November 1853 the Russian fleet bombarded and burned the Ottoman squadron in the harbour of Sinop; more than 2,000 soldiers were killed (as martyrs), twelve ships were sunk and the civilian quarters of the city were also damaged (see the card on naval misfortunes). This raid caused a great stir in European public opinion and led Britain and France to enter the war on the Ottoman side. The main theatre of the war shifted to the Crimean peninsula, to the port of Sevastopol besieged by the allies; the siege ended with the fall of the city on 8 September 1855, and this accelerated the peace process.", "b535b93e"], "sonuc": ["The war ended with the Treaty of Paris of 30 March 1856. The treaty abolished Russia's right of protection over the Principalities (Wallachia and Moldavia), established an international regime of navigation on the Danube, declared the Black Sea neutral and demilitarised, and officially deemed the Ottoman State \"an equal member of the European state system\" — thereby also officially ending the Russian superiority established by Küçük Kaynarca in 1774. But the victory did not come without a price: the financial burden of the war forced the Ottomans to sign the first foreign loan contract in their history on 24 August 1854 — this borrowing was the first link in the chain that half a century later would lead to the Ottoman Public Debt Administration (Düyun-ı Umumiyye) (1881) (see the card on the Public Debt Administration).", "36044b90"], "tartisma": ["The TDV source that was read (the article on the Treaty of Paris) gives no total figure for the military and civilian losses of the war; the losses at Sinop are documented with concrete numbers, but no total casualty figure has been given in this card for the Sevastopol front as a whole (none has been invented).", "3a5fba92"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman State, Britain, France (from 1854), Piedmont-Sardinia", "b62d6c53"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Russia", "4af42a63"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-otlukbeli-1473":{"baslik": ["The Battle of Otlukbeli (11 August 1473)", "309a3556"], "tarih_metin": ["16 Rabi al-Awwal 878 · 11 August 1473 (Wednesday)", "cdda1351"], "yer": ["The hills of Otlukbeli (Başkent), at the locality of Üçağızlı near Tercan", "726b54c1"], "oncesi": ["Having taken Trebizond and established his dominance over the Karamanids, the Conqueror had as his most powerful rival in the east the Aq Qoyunlu state, centred on Diyarbekir and Tabriz, which held most of Iran and Eastern Anatolia. Uzun Hasan sought an alliance with Venice, which was at war with the Ottomans, and also with Hungary, the Knights of Rhodes and Cyprus; he persistently asked Venice for cannon and muskets, the greatest deficiency of his army, but because of Ottoman precautions the weapons sent never reached him. In 1472 an Aq Qoyunlu army plundered and burned Tokat; the TDV interprets this as a response to the new customs duty that the Conqueror had imposed at Tokat on the silk road. As the price of peace Uzun Hasan demanded that Cappadocia and Trebizond be left to him; in a harsh letter the Conqueror challenged him to war in the spring. After seeking peace with Venice and Hungary in the west and coming to terms with the Mamluks, he set out from Üsküdar in March 1473 and entrusted Istanbul to his young son Cem.", "1c9b6598"], "akis": ["Although the army marched in battle order for forty days, it did not encounter Uzun Hasan; when communications were broken, the rumour reached Istanbul that the Aq Qoyunlu had won, and Prince Cem set about establishing his own authority. On 4 August, on the bank of the Euphrates, Uğurlu Mehmed drew Has Murad Pasha into a narrow pass with a feigned retreat; the pasha, who did not heed Mahmud Pasha's warning, was drowned while trying to cross the river, about 4000 soldiers died and many notables were taken prisoner. When, seven days later, the demoralised army encamped at Üçağızlı, it was forced to fight on unfavourable ground against an enemy that had already occupied the hills of Otlukbeli. Davud Pasha's fierce attack prevented Gâvur İshak from coming down and cutting the roads and enabled the Ottoman units to gain a foothold on the hill. Prince Mustafa came out onto level ground and bore down on Zeynel Mirza's wing; when Zeynel was killed by the azebs, the Aq Qoyunlu right wing collapsed. On the other wing Uğurlu Mehmed held a stream valley and stopped Prince Bayezid. Seeing that the break-up had begun, the Conqueror threw still more of the Janissaries into the battle. Realising that resistance was impossible, Uzun Hasan left in his place Alpagot Pir Mehmed Bey, who closely resembled him, and fled; the Ottomans took the latter prisoner thinking he was Uzun Hasan, and only later understood that they were mistaken.", "f5d75c6c"], "sonuc": ["According to Angiolello, in the battle, which lasted eight hours, the Aq Qoyunlu lost 10,000 men and the Ottomans only 1000; the TDV stresses that superiority in cannon and muskets played a large part in the victory. Among the prisoners were mirzas of Timur's line and Uzun Hasan's nişancı (chancellor) and imam; most of the captured Turkmen soldiers were killed, but the fleeing army was not pursued. The Conqueror took Bayburt and on the way back also seized the fortress of Şarkîkarahisar. The victory gave a great boost to the morale of the Ottomans, who had feared a danger from the east ever since the defeat inflicted by Timur, and showed that the classic Turkmen armies could no longer cope with regular units bearing firearms. The Aq Qoyunlu never recovered; the vacuum they left was filled by the Safavids, who would become a far more serious rival to the Ottomans.", "069f8e19"], "tartisma": ["Has Murad Pasha's failure to obey orders is attributed in the sources to two different causes: according to some, those in his retinue goaded him not to leave the glory of victory to Mahmud Pasha; according to others, he fell victim to his own inexperience. The strength of the Ottoman army is given within a wide range, between 70,000 and 100,000; the casualty figures in the TDV, for their part, rest on a single source (Angiolello).", "9d42976e"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman army", "e562e57a"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Sultan Mehmed the Conqueror (centre) · Prince Bayezid on the right wing · Prince Mustafa and Koca Davud Pasha, beylerbey of Anatolia, on the left wing · Grand Vizier Mahmud Pasha", "97a84f6a"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["between 70,000 and 100,000", "f06466e3"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Aq Qoyunlu army", "968b6c58"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Uzun Hasan (centre) · his son Zeynel Mirza on the right wing · his son Uğurlu Mehmed on the left wing · Gâvur İshak in the vanguard", "2f0384fd"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["about 70,000 cavalry (40,000 of them armoured and armed with lances)", "501151e7"]},
"savas-p75b-kirim-cepheleri":{"baslik": ["The fronts of the Crimean War: the Danube, the Crimean peninsula, the Caucasus", "20b2efdd"], "tarih_metin": ["October 1853 - November 1855 (three main fronts)", "d265cfda"], "yer": ["The Danube (Wallachia, the Bulgarian shore), the Crimean peninsula (Gözleve, Sevastopol), Eastern Anatolia-Caucasus (Kars, Erzurum), the Black Sea", "de9ae144"], "oncesi": ["War was declared on 4 October 1853 (chronology; Demiray). Russia had begun by occupying Wallachia and Moldavia.", "e15a6013"], "akis": ["The Danube front. Köremezli (2021) sees Ottoman strategy as built on defence: Vidin and the fortifications of Kalafat on the left bank of the river were among the most important pillars of the Ottoman defence in the Balkans, and Ömer Lütfi Pasha ordered his soldiers first to fortify the island in front of the fortress and then to seize Kalafat. The Battle of Oltenița was fought on Friday, 4 November 1853 (the Russian commander Pavlov attacked); Köremezli describes it as a move the Ottomans designed to draw the Russian headquarters away from Kalafat, and adds that the Russian commander Gorchakov did not share this interpretation. The Battle of Cetate was fought near Kalafat on 6 January 1854 and, according to Köremezli, was one of the bloodiest engagements of the war; numerical superiority lay with the Ottomans. The Defence of Silistra (15 May-25 June 1854; TDV Silistre speaks of the redoubts) ended with the Russian army withdrawing before the fortress fell; the Battle of Giurgiu (Yergöğü) (7 July 1854) was the last important engagement on this front, and its outcome was indecisive. Köremezli writes that, because victory in the Crimean War came with the capture of Sevastopol, developments on the Danube soon lost their importance.\n\nTHE CRIMEAN peninsula. TDV Treaty of Paris: the landing of the allied forces in the Crimea (TDV says 26 September 1854) gave rise to the main front that would determine the end of the war; the fighting involved heavy losses, and the fall of Sevastopol after a long siege (8 September 1855) brought the parties closer to peace. On the Battle of Gözleve fought by Ottoman forces in the Crimea, Köremezli says 'the Ottoman forces gained the upper hand over the Russians', while İmamoğlu (2019) reports from a jihad treatise of the period that the Ottoman army going to Gözleve set out from Varna with thirty battalions of infantry and sixty squadrons of cavalry. A day-by-day account of the battles of the Alma, Balaklava and Inkerman and of the siege of Sevastopol was not found in any peer-reviewed source that could be read: `not found`. The TDV article on the Crimea writes that during the war the Russian administrators took very harsh measures against the Crimean Tatars.\n\nTHE CAUCASUS-EASTERN Anatolian front. According to Şahin (2013), the Ottoman state intended on this front to protect its frontiers with a strong defence; besides the regular units, large numbers of bashi-bazouks and volunteers were put under arms, but these irregular units could not give the regular units the support that was wanted. The routs at Akhaltsikhe (Ahıska) and Başgedikler in November 1853 shook the morale of the people of Kars and Erzurum; Şahin attributes the failure on the front to entering the war without adequate preparation and without a plan, and to the incompetence of the commanders. In 1855 the Russian commander Muravyov decided to besiege the fortress of Kars; the siege became a time of hardship that lasted until the end of September, famine and cholera broke out in the city, at a meeting also attended by the British general Williams it was decided on 24 November 1855 to surrender, and the instrument of surrender was signed on 27 November (Şahin; TDV Treaty of Paris says 26 November 1855).", "92b29d1d"], "sonuc": ["The fall of Sevastopol and the passing of Kars into Russian hands are two outcomes that leave open the question of whether the war ended in favour of or against the Ottomans and their allies; TDV Treaty of Paris records that the return of Kars was negotiated in exchange for a part of Bessarabia. The war ended with the Treaty of Paris of 30 March 1856.", "a1cae5a1"], "tartisma": ["① The dates differ between sources: for Oltenița the chronology entry gives 11 November 1853, while Köremezli says Friday, 4 November 1853 (23 October by the Russian calendar). For the surrender of Kars TDV says 26 November, Şahin says decision on 24 November/signature on 27 November. For the landing in the Crimea TDV writes 26 September 1854; this could not be verified with another source. On the card each date is cited with its source. ② The question 'Was the Ottoman Empire the subject or the object of the war?' becomes visible in a comparison of these three fronts: on the Danube command and initiative were Ottoman (Köremezli), in the Crimea the main burden lay on the allies, and in the Caucasus Ottoman unpreparedness and the limits of irregular troops come to the fore (Şahin). This is a summary and is not written in a single sentence in the sources: it is this page's interpretation. ③ Not found: the details of the battles on the Crimean peninsula and the real share of the Ottoman forces there; Candan Badem's book 'The Ottoman Crimean War' (Brill, 2010) appears to be the main source that would fill this gap, but it could not be accessed.", "86b599cd"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["The Ottoman Empire, Britain, France (and Piedmont)", "fd56e77e"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Danube: Ömer Lütfi Pasha · Crimea: Ömer Pasha with the allied commanders · East: Zarif Mustafa Pasha, then Vasıf Pasha; at Kars the British general Williams", "326468a9"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no total figure in the sources; not found", "9747732d"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Russia", "4af42a63"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Muravyov in the Caucasus; Gorchakov and Dannenberg on the Danube front", "3d4b1ba8"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no total figure in the sources; not found", "9747732d"]},
"savas-p75b-nizip-1839":{"baslik": ["Battle of Nizip (24 June 1839): why did the army collapse, and what was the mistake?", "290dbfe9"], "tarih_metin": ["24 June 1839 — the battle was decided on the same day, within a few hours", "ed72c5cd"], "yer": ["Nizip, on the banks of the Euphrates (in today's Gaziantep province)", "40068f27"], "oncesi": ["The Peace of Kütahya of 1833 had not brought a lasting solution; Mahmud II was preparing to settle accounts with the governor of Egypt. According to Dönmez, in 1838 the sultan agreed to the Baltalimanı commercial treaty (16 August 1838) in order to obtain British support, finalised the decision for war in 1839 as his health was deteriorating, and the Sublime Porte joined in this decision. Lord Palmerston had suggested waiting for the reforms to bear fruit; the sultan did not wish to wait. Moltke had joined Hâfız Pasha's army at Harput in the spring of 1838. According to the TDV article on Moltke, when the Egyptian vanguard forces moved, Moltke and Mühlbach came before the pasha and asked that these vanguard forces be attacked; the proposal was not received favourably, and Hâfız Pasha could not be persuaded to take the necessary military measures.", "59c7f2ee"], "akis": ["In Kocaoğlu's account, the two armies drew up in battle order at Nizip and began waiting for a suitable time to attack. The Prussian staff officers, seeing that the Ottoman army was in a position to defeat the Egyptian army, advised an immediate attack; the ulema (religious scholars) in the army said that attacking on a Friday was not in accordance with religion. The next day the staff officers proposed a night raid; this time the ulema declared that a surprise attack would not befit the sultan's dignity. In the meantime the Egyptian army took up positions so as to envelop the Ottoman wings; Moltke advised Hâfız Pasha to withdraw by way of Birecik, but Hâfız Pasha, considering retreat dishonourable, did not move from where he was. When the Egyptian army attacked, the Ottoman army broke up within four hours; thousands of dead, tens of thousands of prisoners and the cannon were left on the field (Kocaoğlu).", "f4dea7bb"], "sonuc": ["The defeat, in the TDV's words, reopened the roads of Anatolia to İbrahim Pasha. Mahmud II died before the news reached Istanbul (1 July 1839; for details: `sebep-sonuc-p75b-donanma-iskenderiye-1839`); in the same weeks the Ottoman State lost first its army and then its fleet, and on 27 July 1839 the five great powers asked in a joint note that the Egyptian question not be settled without their being consulted. The Egyptian question was closed only with the Treaty of London of 15 July 1840 and Europe's military intervention (TDV Mahmud II, Mehmed Ali Pasha of Kavala).", "c759d002"], "tartisma": ["① The question 'What was the mistake?' has three layers in the sources. The command layer: the main view, based on Moltke's own account, is that Hâfız Pasha rejected proposal after proposal (an attack on the vanguard, a night raid, a withdrawal to Birecik) and that the ulema delayed the attack on grounds of the calendar and propriety (TDV Moltke; Kocaoğlu). The junction layer: the author of the TDV article on Moltke interprets Hâfız Pasha's 'inability to act independently' and his failure to join the other Ottoman army at Konya and confront the Egyptian forces together as a first experience that contributed to Moltke's later principle of 'march separately, unite and strike'. The political layer: according to Dönmez, the timing of the war was the sultan's decision; Europe's proposal to wait was not heeded. ② Superiority in numbers makes explanation harder: according to the figures given by Kocaoğlu (citing Karal), the two armies were roughly equal in numbers, so the problem was one of command more than of strength. ③ The limits of the sources: the most detailed witness is Moltke, and he is someone recounting that his own proposals were rejected; in the same article the TDV also notes that Moltke's service in Turkey later gave rise to a 'Moltke legend', and that the other Prussian officers who came with him remained in the shade. The Ottoman command's own explanation and Egyptian-side sources could not be read: `not found`. For this reason the verdict has not been reduced to the single sentence 'the mistake was Hâfız Pasha's'.", "a6296dab"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman army", "e562e57a"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Serasker (commander-in-chief) Hâfız Pasha; among his staff advisers were the Prussian officers Moltke and Mühlbach", "8475ef15"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["Kocaoğlu, relying on Enver Ziya Karal, writes 30,000 infantry + 5,000 cavalry + 3,000 artillerymen, 160 guns; this figure could not be confirmed from another source", "b786fabc"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Egyptian army", "aba42fc7"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["İbrahim Pasha (son of Mehmed Ali Pasha of Kavala)", "20a6dd4f"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["Kocaoğlu (relying on Karal): 40,000, 160 guns; could not be confirmed in another source", "46794626"]},
"savas-p75b-sinop-1853":{"baslik": ["Raid on Sinop (30 November 1853): the squadron sheltering from the storm", "037924e4"], "tarih_metin": ["30 November 1853 (chronology entry; Demiray says 29/30 November; the TDV `sinop` article says 'early 1854', see discussion ③)", "17b117c7"], "yer": ["Sinop harbour, Black Sea", "83132273"], "oncesi": ["The Crimean War had begun on 4 October 1853. According to Özcan, the Sublime Porte had a squadron of light vessels formed to protect the Black Sea coasts and to escort the flotilla that was to carry war materials to Batum; command was given to Patrona (vice admiral) Osman Pasha and Riyale (rear admiral) Hüseyin Pasha. The instructions of the Admiralty were to fight if, on encountering Russian ships, there was a chance of winning, and in stormy weather to take shelter in the harbour of Sinop. The squadron, which left Istanbul at the beginning of November, was damaged in a storm and dropped anchor at Sinop in a wretched condition. Sinop harbour was protected by five batteries, but according to Özcan this protection was insufficient against an enemy attack; the Admiralty had asked that as soon as the weather cleared, the squadron come as a body to places near the Bosphorus.", "4074d4d0"], "akis": ["Having learned from an Austrian steamer that the Ottoman squadron was at Sinop, Nakhimov made reconnaissances and was reinforced with supporting ships. On the morning of 30 November the weather was foggy and rainy, and the north wind was favourable for a surprise attack; the Russian galleons formed a line opposite the Ottoman ships and anchored. Özcan writes that Osman Pasha did not assign a ship to keep watch outside the harbour and, because he did not think a raid likely, did not set the squadron in motion, and that only when the Russian ships came to the harbour mouth and anchored did he, 'waking from the sleep of heedlessness', give the order to prepare for battle. The engagement began at very close range; the Russian howitzer shot and shells started fires in the Ottoman wooden ships. The Ottoman flagship Avnillah ran aground with heavy damage. Major Ali Bey, commander of the frigate Naveki Bahri, blew up the ship by firing the powder magazine so that it would not fall into Russian hands (Özcan). Of the squadron, only the steamer Taif managed to leave the harbour, flee towards Gerze and, fighting with Russian steamers, bring the news to Istanbul; Osman Pasha, wounded, was taken prisoner by the Russians.", "d0f00a3a"], "sonuc": ["According to the TDV article on Sinop, more than two thousand soldiers were killed and twelve Ottoman ships were sunk; in the town, according to an official document, seven small mosques, two schools, 247 houses and 170 shops burned, five Muslim civilians were killed, and sixteen Christians also died. For the number of those killed, Özcan says 'nearly two thousand'. The news caused a great stir in European public opinion; according to Özcan, it was upon this that Britain and France entered the war on the Ottoman side (TDV Treaty of Paris: the allies' entry into the war). In Russia the raid was celebrated with festivities, and Nakhimov and his officers were promoted. On the Ottoman side, a Naval Martyrs' Cemetery and a Martyrs' Fountain were built at Sinop for the fallen, and a tax remission was granted (Özcan).", "2e5098df"], "tartisma": ["① Why was the squadron there, and why was it alone? Özcan writes that the Naval Council discussed the drawbacks of there being no protected harbour in the Black Sea, decided that sailing ships unable to put out to sea in bad weather should not be sent again and that the squadron at Sinop should be brought to Istanbul as soon as possible, and set aside six steamers to assist the return, but that the raid gave no opportunity for this decision to be carried out. ② Özcan's thesis: the British ambassador, Lord Stratford Redcliffe, played a role in preparing the 'last straw' of the Ottoman-Russian war; the ambassador saw to it that the squadron was formed of light vessels, aiming to make a possible defeat a justification for Britain and France to enter the war. Özcan bases this on the texts of two telegrams. This is the assessment of the article's author; no such claim was found in the other sources consulted (TDV Sinop, Treaty of Paris, Tanzimat), and therefore it appears in the card not as a verdict but as 'the thesis of one peer-reviewed article'. ③ Date: the TDV article on Sinop says 'early 1854'; Demiray writes 29/30 November 1853 (the double date may be due to the difference in calendars); in the scanned text of Özcan's article the day figures could not be read. The chronology entry is 30 November 1853.", "5d3bf743"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman squadron", "a19512ec"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Patrona (vice admiral) Osman Pasha (who had assumed command of the squadron together with Riyale (rear admiral) Hüseyin Pasha)", "252d72ba"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["a squadron consisting of light vessels, frigates, corvettes and steamers; the figures Özcan used in giving the numbers of ships could not be read in the scanned text — no number has been written", "0601f031"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Russian Black Sea squadron", "04038c5e"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Admiral Pavel Stepanovich Nakhimov", "3c5256ca"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["according to Özcan, six galleons (including two-deckers) and two frigates, with three more steamers joining on the day of the raid; the galleons carried Paixhans shell guns", "aee853fb"]},
"savas-rodos-1522":{"baslik": ["Siege of Rhodes (28 July – 20 December 1522)", "dba5cb3f"], "tarih_metin": ["The fleet put to sea on 4 June 1522 · the sultan crossed to the island on 28 July · surrender on 1 Safar 929 / 20 December 1522", "d270e65f"], "yer": ["The city and island of Rhodes", "e0a71429"], "oncesi": ["The Knights had settled on Rhodes in 1309, turned the city, with its walls enclosing 81 hectares, into the strongest fortress of medieval Europe, and from here carried out corsair raids on Turkish ships and coasts. In 1480 Mesih Pasha's fleet of 160 ships besieged the island for three months; when the last great assault on 28 July failed, he withdrew, after which the walls were renewed from end to end. After Sultan Selim the Grim took Syria and Egypt in 1516-1517, the island became a Christian outpost in the middle of the busy sea route between Istanbul and Cairo. Having taken Belgrade, Suleiman the Lawgiver chose as his second target Rhodes, which again his great forebear had failed to capture; the shipyard Selim had built and the great fleet he had had prepared in his last years left hardly any need for further preparation for this campaign.", "d87e3bf9"], "akis": ["The fleet sailed on 4 June; on 18 June the sultan set out overland from Üsküdar at the head of his army, reached Marmaris on 26 July by way of İznik, Kütahya, Denizli, Çine and Muğla, and crossed to the island on 28 July. Throughout the siege he watched the fighting, often rode around the island and held frequent councils with Pîrî Mehmed Pasha. Life in the camp was not confined to war: twenty-five qadis found to have committed abuses in Anatolia were dismissed, and the news from Hürrem Sultan of the birth of his son Mehmed also arrived at this time. The sources describe the fighting as \"hard and bloody\"; the details of the assaults are not known. The Knights surrendered on 20 December.", "e81a5723"], "sonuc": ["L'Isle-Adam, who was received at the divan that was convened, was given leave to quit the island; according to one account, the sultan consoled the aged knight and even proposed inviting him to Islam and giving him a suitable office. Under the terms of the agreement the Knights boarded their ships and departed; the Grand Master left the island on 1 January 1523. In those same days Cem Sultan's son, who had been living on Rhodes, was found and put to death, and Cem's daughters and wife were sent to Istanbul. The sultan entered the city on 29 December and on 2 January and had the khutbah read in his name. The ruined walls were repaired and a strong garrison was installed; in the upper part of the city a mosque, a madrasa, a soup kitchen and a bathhouse were built, and the tax revenues of the small surrounding islands were assigned to this endowment. The Knights' Gothic cathedral of Saint John was converted into a mosque in 1523. The island remained Ottoman until the Italian occupation of 1912.", "7f5fe217"], "tartisma": ["The campaign calendar differs between two TDV articles: the article on Suleiman I says that the sultan left Üsküdar on 23 Rajab (18 June), while the article on Rhodes says that he set out on campaign on 21 Rajab 928 (16 June). The Rhodes article gives the garrison of 1524 as 1,343 men in one place and 1,378 in another. The date of the surrender is 20 December 1522 in both TDV articles.", "e3dffcdb"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman army and fleet", "a9f3c5bb"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Sultan Suleiman the Lawgiver · Grand Vizier Pîrî Mehmed Pasha", "fe9d7206"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Knights of Saint John (Hospitallers)", "6a12a491"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Grand Master Philippe Villiers de l'Isle-Adam", "facb37aa"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-trabzon-1461":{"baslik": ["The Surrender of Trebizond (1461)", "85472e39"], "tarih_metin": ["Summer 1461 · TDV: \"August or September 1461\"", "f2b10e65"], "yer": ["Trebizond, on the eastern Black Sea coast", "9f1237f1"], "oncesi": ["The TDV attributes the long life of this small empire, which had stood since 1204, to several factors: the eastern Black Sea mountains that separate the coast from the interior and allow no passage, strong walls, lively trade with the Venetian and Genoese colonies, and a marriage diplomacy that was prepared to accept vassalage to powerful neighbours (the Seljuks, the Mongols, Timur). After the conquest of Istanbul, Emperor Kalo Ioannes began to pay tribute to the sultan. In 1456 Sheikh Junayd, who had won the Turkmens of Canik to his side, attempted to take the city and establish a state of his own; he came right up to the walls but did not succeed. According to the article on Mehmed II, Trebizond was regarded as being under the protection of Uzun Hasan of the Aq Qoyunlu; the campaign was therefore also seen as a move against Uzun Hasan. Before setting out on the campaign, the Conqueror signed a truce with the Knights of Rhodes.", "5d8e1fda"], "akis": ["First Hızır Bey carried out a reconnaissance operation with the fleet. Then the sultan, at the head of the army, reached the city overland across difficult mountain roads and began the siege; the fleet of Kasım Bey, sanjak governor of Gallipoli, gave support from the sea. According to the sources the siege lasted four or six weeks, and the city surrendered under an agreement. The Conqueror stayed a few days in Trebizond and left the administration of the city to Kasım Bey.", "704cd050"], "sonuc": ["Under the agreement David Komnenos and his whole family were put on ships with their movable goods and sent to Istanbul; palace officials and wealthy families followed them. The emperor was given extensive lands in the district of Serres, and most of the nobles were assigned timars. Of some 1500 young people selected from the city and its surroundings, 800 were sent to Istanbul to be trained as Janissaries. The property of those who remained in the city was recognised; a unit of 400 Janissaries and azebs (light infantry) was stationed there, and Turks were installed in the vacated houses. According to the survey register of 1486, Muslims who had come from Anatolian towns, either by deportation or of their own accord, made up about a fifth of the population. The Chrysokephalos cathedral, where the emperors had been crowned, was turned into the Câmi-i Atîk (Old Mosque) in commemoration of the conquest. For Uzun Hasan the matter was not closed: as a condition of peace he demanded that Trebizond be given to him, and in 1472 he sent a nephew of the former emperor against the city — this was one of the knots of the conflict that would end two years later at Otlukbeli.", "30e71434"], "tartisma": ["The TDV article on Trebizond gives the surrender with two possibilities, as \"August or September 1461\"; the duration of the siege too varies between four and six weeks according to the sources. As for the fall of Amasra, the TDV article on Mehmed II places it in the summer of 1459, immediately after the Smederevo campaign.", "2905820c"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman army and fleet", "a9f3c5bb"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Sultan Mehmed the Conqueror · Hızır Bey with the reconnaissance fleet · Kasım Bey, sanjak governor of Gallipoli", "2897fe54"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Empire of Trebizond (Komnenos dynasty)", "8f519803"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Emperor David Komnenos", "5af9c587"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-viyana-karlofca-1683-1699":{"baslik": ["The Second Siege of Vienna and the Great Turkish War (1683-1699)", "22a62e94"], "tarih_metin": ["Siege 14 July - 12 September 1683 · the war as a whole 1683-1699 · Treaty of Karlowitz 26 January 1699", "f85e5d28"], "yer": ["Vienna (siege) — the war in general on the fronts of Hungary, the Morea, Podolia and Dalmatia", "429c2485"], "oncesi": ["When Protestant Hungarian leaders asked for Ottoman protection against Austria, Kara Mustafa Pasha, in contrast to the inclination of the previous grand vizier Fazıl Ahmed Pasha to maintain the 1664 Treaty of Vasvár, decided on a direct campaign against Vienna. The army set out in the spring of 1683 and arrived before Vienna on 14 July; according to the TDV article `merzifonlu-kara-mustafa-pasa`, the fact that the great siege guns had not been taken on the campaign was a deficiency from the outset.", "1271666c"], "akis": ["The siege, which began after the city rejected the offer to surrender, lasted two months, and the shortage of food came to the surface. Kara Mustafa Pasha's personal enmity with the Crimean khan Murad Giray was a mistake that led the khan to remain neutral throughout the campaign. The Polish king Sobieski arrived with an army of 100,000 men and 200 guns, held the hill of Kahlenberg (\"the German mountain\") and got behind the Ottoman army. In the battle that began on 12 September, İbrahim Pasha's unit collapsed first, and the expected support from Murad Giray did not come; when Sobieski attacked the centre, Kara Mustafa Pasha was forced to withdraw after resisting for five or six hours.", "49e1a64c"], "sonuc": ["After the rout, Kara Mustafa Pasha's enemies at the palace (Yusuf Agha, Sarı Süleyman Agha) maligned him to the sultan and had him executed on 25 December 1683. The defeat led to the formation of the Holy League, which brought together Austria, Poland, Venice and the Papacy, and the war continued for sixteen years on four fronts (Hungary, the Morea, Dalmatia, Podolia) — in Ottoman history this period is also referred to as the \"Great Turkish War\". The war ended on 26 January 1699 with the Treaty of Karlowitz: the Ottomans ceded the greater part of Hungary (including Transylvania) and the Morea to Austria and Venice, and Podolia to Poland. Karlowitz was the first treaty of great territorial loss that the Ottomans signed in Europe, and within the framework given by TDV it is regarded as a turning point at which Austria was able for the first time to force its rivals into such a comprehensive peace.", "c2980fed"], "tartisma": ["The tactical details of the siege (at what point İbrahim Pasha collapsed, the exact nature of the enmity with Murad Giray) are given only in summary in the TDV article that was read; the full sixteen-year, four-front course of the war has not been detailed in this card, and only the beginning, the rout and the outcome have been narrated.", "1be643a3"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman State", "86202f73"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Grand Vizier Merzifonlu Kara Mustafa Pasha", "205cd1b1"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no total figure given in the source", "f6e6426c"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Holy Roman Empire (Austria) + Poland-Lithuania + (from 1684) the Holy League: Venice, the Papacy, later Russia", "75cec463"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["At Vienna, the Polish king John III Sobieski (relief army of 100,000 men, 200 guns)", "bab8f153"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no total figure given in the source", "f6e6426c"]},
"savas2-atina-1687-parthenon-patlamasi":{"baslik": ["The loss of Athens and the explosion of the Parthenon (21-26 September 1687)", "128acdac"], "tarih_metin": ["The siege began on 21 September 1687 · explosion of the Parthenon 26 September 1687 · Venetian withdrawal and Ottoman return April 1688", "954a578a"], "yer": ["Athens (the Acropolis) and the Parthenon (at that time the mosque of Athens)", "a54f0de0"], "oncesi": ["As part of the Morea campaign (one front of the War of the Holy League), the Venetian fleet under Morosini's command, after seizing the whole Morea peninsula between 1685 and 1687, turned north towards Athens. In the Ottoman period the Parthenon had been used as a mosque for centuries, and the garrison had stored gunpowder and ammunition inside it, probably because it was the most solid structure in the fortress — this decision became the direct cause of the coming disaster.", "12a936d4"], "akis": ["In the siege that began on 21 September 1687, Venetian artillery pounded the Acropolis. On 26 September a cannonball fired under Königsmarck struck the Parthenon, which had been reported to have ammunition stored inside. The explosion completely destroyed the middle part of the building: fourteen of the forty-six columns surrounding it were toppled, the columns of the interior were shattered and the roof was entirely blown open — a building that had stood almost undamaged since AD 438 (as a church and then as a mosque) was turned into a ruin in a few seconds.", "624bd7f2"], "sonuc": ["The city surrendered shortly afterwards; 560 soldiers and 3000 Turks left Athens, while Venice forcibly deported 6000 Christian inhabitants to the Morea. But the Venetian presence in Athens did not last long: because of epidemic disease and the difficulty of defending the city, the Venetian troops withdrew by their own decision in April 1688, and the Ottomans returned at once — that is, the city remained in Venetian hands for only eight months. The Parthenon was never restored to its former state: the returning Ottoman administration contented itself with building a small mosque within the ruins, and no restoration of the great structure was undertaken.", "a4c8808e"], "tartisma": ["Some online/secondary sources write that the Ottomans did not retake Athens until 'twenty years later' (about 1707); the TDV article `atina` that was read did not give this detail, whereas the site's own chronology entry (1688-04-01, 'The recovery of Athens — the Venetian withdrawal') gives April 1688, and this is closer to the standard account of the Morean War, consistent with Venice's general military and health difficulties in the Morea (epidemic, supply). This card has taken the date 1688 as its basis; the 'twenty years later' claim could not be verified from TDV, and its source is unclear.", "70931988"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Republic of Venice", "f30b04e7"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Francesco Morosini · artillery commander, the Swedish Count Otto Wilhelm Königsmarck", "0b3e960a"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Ottoman State (Athens garrison)", "b5115c94"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["not named in the TDV article that was read", "dfd87a77"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["560 soldiers (based on the number who left the city)", "f3c5d431"]},
"sebep-sonuc-1595-on-dokuz-sehzade":{"oneri": ["The nineteen deaths of 1595 were not a decision of the moment: a century-old order of succession, a crowded…", "fad6b7d7"], "sebep.b": ["Murad III leaving behind a large number of sons within an order of succession in which the throne was bound by no rule, every prince was considered a candidate and the law code permitted fratricide", "bbdcf91b"], "sonuc.b": ["The strangling of nineteen brothers on the night of Mehmed III's accession — and the reaction aroused by the funerals preparing the ground for the end of the practice", "1c3bd383"], "bag": ["Not all the links are sourced: the number of children, the encouragement within the harem and the reaction of 1595 are recorded in TDV; linking these to one another as ‘causes’ is this card's own interpretation.", "d34951ed"], "metin": ["① The absence of a rule. TDV's article 'Ahmed I' writes that until Ahmed I accession was not bound by any rule, and its article 'Şehzade' that the succession of the eldest son to the throne was an unofficial tradition; the princes were sent out to sanjaks to gain experience in government. The Conqueror's law code laid down that whoever came to the throne could kill his brothers for the sake of the ‘order of the world’ (nizâm-ı âlem). ② The absence of a rule fed war. TDV's article on Bayezid II writes that this provision gave the struggle among the princes the character of a fight for survival; the article on Prince Bayezid also states that fear that the same provision would be applied played a part in the rebellion of 1559-62. The wars between brothers during the Interregnum, Cem and Bayezid II, Selim the Grim and his brothers, Prince Mustafa in 1553 and Prince Bayezid in 1562 are the links of this chain (details are in the lists ‘Killings and executions within the dynasty’). ③ A crowded harem. Selim II was the first Ottoman ruler who went on no campaign at all (TDV). For the reign of his son Murad III, TDV records that, encouraged by his mother Nurbânû Sultan and his sister İsmihan Sultan, the sultan turned to concubines. TDV regards the large figures given for the number of his children as ‘exaggerated reports’; what can be gathered from the sources is that forty-nine of his children were alive at his death. TDV's article 'Mehmed III' also speaks of circles that reacted against his father's shutting himself up in the palace and becoming ineffective in government. ④ The night of 1595. At the accession of Mehmed III nineteen princes, four of them grown and the rest very young, were strangled; the mothers of the dead, the daughters of Murad III and women of the palace were sent to the Old Palace. In 1574 Murad III too had had five brothers killed in the same way, but the number in 1595 was almost four times as large. ⑤ The result. According to TDV, the nineteen coffins that left the palace the next day aroused great reaction in every section of society, and this reaction probably opened the door to the end of the practice. In 1603 Mehmed III also had his elder son Mahmud killed on suspicion of coveting his throne. At his death his son Ahmed was fourteen and did not yet have a son; for this reason his brother Mustafa, the only other surviving male member of the dynasty, was not touched (TDV 'Mustafa I').", "43a7d313"]},
"sebep-sonuc-amasya-1555":{"oneri": ["The Shah never gave battle", "670cbc9a"], "sebep.b": ["During the Nakhchivan Campaign, Shah Tahmasp's avoidance of a pitched battle and devastation of the army's route, Suleiman the Lawgiver's threat to Ardabil, held sacred by the Safavids, by wintering in Amasya, and the shah's request for a truce", "3fa28be9"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Amasya: the first treaty between the two states, in which the Safavids accepted Ottoman rule in Iraq and Eastern Anatolia", "dad38f4f"], "bag": ["Significance: The TDV describes this peace as the first treaty concluded between the Ottoman State and Iran. Although it had the appearance of a friendly exchange of letters, it made the other side accept Ottoman rule along the line stretching from Baghdad to Kars and kept the eastern frontier calm for a quarter of a century.", "338561c7"], "metin": ["The raids that the Safavids made into Eastern Anatolia after the Tabriz Campaign sent Suleiman the Lawgiver on campaign once again. Knowing that he could not withstand the great army, Tahmasp did not come out to face it; he burned the places the army was to pass through, had the water poisoned and moved the population away. Because of supply shortages and the approaching winter, the Ottoman army withdrew from Nakhchivan. While Suleiman the Lawgiver was in Erzurum, the shah's request for a truce was accepted (26 September 1554). The sultan's wintering in Amasya and his threat to destroy Ardabil alarmed Tahmasp: his envoy Ferruhzad Bey arrived in Amasya on 17 May 1555, was received in the divan on 22 May and presented the letter seeking peace.", "ac004014"]},
"sebep-sonuc-belgrad-1739":{"oneri": ["The 1739 Treaty of Belgrade — the recovery of lost Belgrade", "62d1d3de"], "sebep.b": ["Austria's defeat in the war of 1736–1739, which Russia and its ally Austria waged against the Ottomans on two fronts, and the operation carried out to retake Belgrade", "9da8e650"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Belgrade: Belgrade and Northern Serbia were recovered; Azov was to be demolished and become an area from which both sides would stay away", "3929618a"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, Austria's eastward expansion came to a halt and its frontier in the Balkans changed little over the following 140 years or so; Russia, for its part, was kept away from the Black Sea until the Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca of 1774.", "951e406e"], "metin": ["The peace opened a long period of calm between the two empires. When Austria was hard pressed in the reign of Maria Theresa by the wars of succession and the wars with Prussia, the Ottomans chose not to take advantage of this weakness; they held aloof from the insistent offers of alliance made by King Frederick II of Prussia. Russia, having waged the war far more effectively than its ally, began to emerge as a serious rival to the Habsburgs in the Balkans. The price of the return to Belgrade was also heavy: according to the survey of 1741, 721 of the 1,546 villages in Northern Serbia had been abandoned. Belgrade Fortress was rebuilt and made the largest fortress in the country.", "76b0567f"]},
"sebep-sonuc-berlin-1878":{"oneri": ["Who undid San Stefano, and why did the Ottomans again leave the table with losses?", "ac172c9e"], "sebep.b": ["The objections of Britain, Austria and the Balkan states to the Greater Bulgaria that Russia had created at San Stefano", "d520e887"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Berlin: the division of Bulgaria into three, the independence of Serbia, Montenegro and Romania, Bosnia-Herzegovina handed over to Austrian occupation, and Kars, Ardahan and Batum ceded to Russia", "c00b2d63"], "bag": ["Significance: San Stefano was a treaty ending a war that concerned only two states; the Congress of Berlin made the question the common concern of Europe's great powers. According to TDV, the principle of Ottoman territorial integrity laid down in the 1856 Treaty of Paris was openly violated, and territory was given even to Iran and Greece, which had had nothing to do with the war. The congress, which was expected to secure a general peace, did not produce the desired result and became the source of later disputes.", "e1a4c38d"], "metin": ["San Stefano settled the Eastern Question solely in Russia's interest, without taking the European balance of power into account. Britain could not accept Russia's gaining so much influence over Ottoman territory, nor could Austria accept the birth of a large Bulgaria in the midst of its own Balkan designs. The new Balkan states were also discontented: Serbia wanted Bosnia-Herzegovina and part of Macedonia, and Romania, although it was to receive Dobruja, did not want to cede to Russia Bessarabia, where Romanians lived. When no European state would consent to Russia's reaching down to the Aegean and the Adriatic, Russia accepted the congress it had at first not wanted. Before the congress, Britain asked for Cyprus as a base in the Eastern Mediterranean against Russia and made it known that it would take the island by force if it were not given; the Sublime Porte, hoping for British support at the congress, handed over the island on 4 June 1878, and a supplementary convention dated 1 July stated that if Russia returned the places it had occupied in the east, Britain would withdraw, and that ownership belonged to the Ottomans. The congress met on 13 June under the presidency of Bismarck. The Ottoman delegation was headed by Field Marshal Mehmed Ali Pasha; with him were the Minister of Public Works, Karatodori Pasha, and the Ottoman ambassador in Berlin, Sadullah Bey. The Ottoman delegation expected the terms of San Stefano to be softened, but each state protected its own interest, and even the British representatives, from whom help was expected, expressed views against the Ottomans. The sixty-four-article treaty signed a month later also shaped the following years through articles not included on the provisions card: the application of the 1868 regulation in Crete, with Europe to be kept informed; the cession of Kotur to Iran; the closure of the Danube to warships while it remained open to merchant ships; reforms in the places inhabited by Armenians; and the retention of the status of the Straits laid down in the London treaty of 1841 and the Paris treaty of 1856.", "622cb3da"]},
"sebep-sonuc-bolge0073-misir-sudan-siyaseti":{"sebep.b": ["Mehmed Ali of Kavala's decision to create a new army on the European model, and that army's need for men and natural resources", "caae44b6"], "sonuc.b": ["Sudan's passing under Egyptian administration with the surrender of Sennar (the Funj), and the beginning of the sixty-one-year \"Turkiyya period\"", "01363b09"], "bag": ["Significance: The Sudan campaign was the first step in Egypt's transformation from an Ottoman province into a power pursuing its own policy of expansion. Sudan's present-day borders are likewise the borders drawn by the administration that began with this campaign.", "a126072b"], "metin": ["① The four causes listed by the source. The article on Sudan in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam lists one by one Mehmed Ali's aims in 1821: to gain possession of the gold and emerald deposits, to make greater use of the sources of the Nile, to employ Sudanese soldiers in the army, and to neutralise the last Mamluk remnants who had taken refuge in the region. For this purpose the campaign was directed at the Dongola and Funj regions. So, according to the source, the answer to the question \"soldiers, gold or trade route?\" is \"all of them\"; but there is an order among them. ② The real driving force: the new army. The TDV article on Mehmed Ali Pasha of Kavala links the cause directly to the army. After the Hejaz campaign, the pasha turned his eyes to Sudan in order to obtain the human and natural resources that would form the basis of the new army he was thinking of creating to preserve his authority and continue his expansionist policy. The campaign organised in 1820 lasted two years. According to the same article, Mehmed Ali, having seen the inadequacy of his army in the Hejaz and Sudan campaigns, decided to create a new army called the Nizam-i Cedid and entrusted this task to the French colonel Joseph Sève (Süleyman Pasha). In other words, Sudan had been conceived both as a reason for the new army and as its first source. ③ And this calculation did not work out. This is the most striking sentence in the source: \"Although the necessary manpower was initially obtained from Sudan, it was not sufficient, and Egypt and Syria thus came to the fore\" (TDV). When the idea of an army formed of Sudanese soldiers failed, Mehmed Ali was obliged to take his own Egyptian peasants under arms; compulsory military service became the greatest source of discontent in the following years. ④ The gold and the trade route really did exist. The source lists the foreign trade of the Funj Sultanate item by item: the main trade goods were slaves, ivory, tamarind, butter, cream, musk and gold ingots; in Cairo, Asyut, Esna, Daraw, Qus, Aswan and Naqada there were commercial agencies that organised the coming and going of the caravans, and Egyptian merchants bought large numbers of slaves from here in particular (TDV, Funj). Sennar was the nodal point of this trade, and Egypt seized this node directly. ⑤ The Mamluk question. At a banquet he gave in Cairo on 1 March 1811, Mehmed Ali had had all but a few of the Mamluk beys killed; the survivors had fled south. According to the TDV article on the Funj, after the conquest the Mamluks of Sennar fled further south, left no trace and vanished from the stage of history. In this respect the campaign was as much the completion of an elimination as a territorial gain. ⑥ How long it lasted. From 1821 to 1882 Sudan remained a country governed by Mehmed Ali and his sons with the fullest powers in the name of the Ottoman State; in Sudanese history this period is called the \"Turkiyya\" (TDV). In 1841 Sudan was formally entrusted to Mehmed Ali by a firman, and the pasha was counted as governor of the provinces of Nubia, Sennar, Kordofan and Darfur. The period was completely brought down in 1885 by the movement that Muhammad Ahmad al-Mahdi launched in 1881. ⑦ So did Abyssinia, Djibouti, Eritrea and Somalia ever come onto the agenda? They did — but not in 1821, and none of them as the target of a campaign. For the time of Mehmed Ali, the source mentions no military plan for these regions; the Red Sea coast passed to Egypt by the sultan's firman. Massawa in present-day Eritrea and Sawakin on the Sudanese coast were given to Mehmed Ali by Sultan Abdülmecid in 1846 as a sâlyâne (a holding in return for a fixed annual payment); on the pasha's death (1849) Massawa returned to the control of the governor of the Hejaz (TDV, Massawa and the Province of Habesh). That is, twenty-five years after Sennar, and by appointment, not by conquest. ⑧ The Horn of Africa was opened a generation later. The real expansion came in the time of his grandson, Khedive Ismail Pasha: by a firman dated 27 May 1866, the district governorships (kaymakamlık) of Sawakin and Massawa were placed under the control of the governorship of Egypt; by another firman dated 1 June 1875, Zeila, on the coast of present-day Somaliland, and its environs were added, and Egypt agreed to send Istanbul 15,000 gold pieces for Zeila (TDV, Zeila). In 1874–1875 Ismail Pasha seized the coastal regions of Somalia in the name of the Ottoman State; his effort to spread into the interior was blocked by Britain, and in 1883 Egyptian troops withdrew from Somalia because of the Mahdist uprising (TDV, Somalia). ⑨ Abyssinia: war, yes; a plan, in 1821, no. According to the TDV article on Ethiopia, in 1872 Khedive Ismail Pasha, also encouraged by the British, wished to annex the sources of the Blue Nile to Egypt, but failed; after 1874 he organised three military campaigns against Ethiopia, which laid claim to Massawa, and in 1876 the fighting stopped when King Yohannes IV chose to come to an agreement. This was half a century after the surrender of Sennar. ⑩ Djibouti: the source does not speak of this. The TDV article on Djibouti mentions no Egyptian venture in the 19th-century history of the region; Obock there was bought by the French from a local chief in 1859 (TDV, Zeila and Somalia). This is an answer in itself: Djibouti never came onto Egypt's agenda at all. ⑪ The short answer. In 1821 Egypt's eyes were on the upper reaches of the Nile: soldiers, gold and the node of the Sennar trade. The African coast of the Red Sea — Massawa, Sawakin, then Zeila and the Somali shore — came into Egypt's hands a generation later and largely by Istanbul's firmans; the reckoning with Abyssinia belongs to the 1870s; Djibouti never came onto the agenda.", "56d31553"]},
"sebep-sonuc-bucas-1672":{"oneri": ["Why did the peace that won Podolia not last even a year?", "40c4c95f"], "sebep.b": ["Mehmed IV's Polish campaign: the capture of Kamianets (Kamaniçe) after a ten-day siege and the pressure put on Lviv (İlbav)", "c48f0660"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Buczacz: Podolia became an Ottoman province (eyalet), and Poland agreed to pay an annual pişkeş (tributary gift)", "74e7b1a2"], "bag": ["Significance: With Kamianets and Podolia, the Ottomans established themselves in a region regarded as the key to the Crimea; Ukraine under Doroshenko became an autonomous entity attached to the Ottomans, and the way to emigration was opened to the Lipka Tatars. But it was precisely this gain that was unacceptable to Poland: the Polish Diet found the treaty too harsh, rejected it and imposed new taxes. From 1673 the war began again and lasted until Karlowitz; the intervening treaties of 1676 and 1678 took Buczacz as their basis. Poland, unable to recover Kamianets by war, regained it only in 1699 through an article added at Karlowitz.", "affa5e12"], "metin": ["When Kamianets fell on 27 August 1672, the Ottoman side began to press Poland towards a lasting peace: on 4 September the Polish king was informed that the campaign would continue unless he accepted that Podolia belonged to the Ottomans, and on 6 September the army marched on Lviv, where the king was staying. On 19 September the king asked for peace; arguing that Lviv lay outside Podolia, he offered money in return for the lifting of the siege. When the army laid siege to the palanka of Buczacz on 3 October, the Polish envoys arrived and on 14–16 October held talks with a delegation headed by the kaymakam Kara Mustafa Pasha. The treaty was signed on 18 October; Fazıl Ahmed Pasha sealed the Turkish text and the Polish envoys the Latin one. At the envoys' request, the palanka of Buczacz, which lay outside Podolia, was returned to Poland, and that same evening a pardon (aman) for the Polish king was proclaimed by firman. However, Poland held on to some of the castles and palankas it had undertaken to return and turned to a hostile policy towards the Ottomans; the Ottoman government decided on a second campaign.", "8a5ad46f"]},
"sebep-sonuc-bukres-1812":{"oneri": ["What was saved at the table while Napoleon marched on Moscow?", "bd02bd79"], "sebep.b": ["The breakdown of the Ottoman-Russian alliance formed against the French when the Russians incited the Greeks of the Morea and the islands to revolt, and the war that resumed in 1806", "76aa0d95"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Bucharest: Bessarabia passed to Russia, and the Serbs were granted partial autonomy", "f12f7097"], "bag": ["Significance: With the Prut frontier, Bessarabia entered a period of Russian rule that was to last 106 years. According to the TDV, together with the autonomy granted to the Serbs, a period began in which the Orthodox and Slav peoples were openly incited and supported against Ottoman rule.", "33a756ee"], "metin": ["The autonomy provision was not carried out at once: the revolt of the Serbs, who wanted broader rights, was suppressed in 1813, Belgrade was retaken, and the leader of the revolt, Karađorđe, fled to Austria. But in 1815 a second Serbian uprising broke out under the leadership of Miloš Obrenović; although it too was suppressed the same year, from then on the privileges granted to the Serbs began to be broadened, and the question came back to the table with the Akkerman Convention of 1826.", "4aa66331"]},
"sebep-sonuc-celali-isyanlari":{"oneri": ["The name of one sheikh became the name of every uprising in Anatolia for a century.", "4e2a47a7"], "sebep.b": ["The mass of unemployed levend (irregular soldiers) and sekban (musket-bearing mercenaries) that built up in Anatolia during the years of the Long War, sipahis who had lost their revenue grants, the debased akçe and the oppression of provincial administrators", "f924eb41"], "sonuc.b": ["The 'Great Flight' in Anatolia: peasants abandoning their homes and fleeing to fortresses, to Istanbul and to Rumelia; followed by Kuyucu Murad Pasha's bloody purge", "691f118c"], "bag": ["Significance: The Celali movement began as a cause bound up with sect and the claim to rule; by the seventeenth century it had turned into a movement against the centre by people cut off from the state apparatus. While the Long War went on, Anatolia was left defenceless; the revolts meant that sufficient forces could not be assigned to the Austrian and Iranian fronts, which at times led to defeats. The population and the productive order of Anatolia were deeply shaken.", "956a4271"], "metin": ["The name comes from Sheikh Celal of Bozok, who rose in revolt around Tokat in 1519 claiming to be the Mahdi. His revolt was harshly suppressed, but from then on the people began to call every armed rebel who did them harm a 'Celali'; the Ottoman conception of the state, for its part, regarded these movements as rebellion against the sultan (huruj ala al-sultan). In the first half of the sixteenth century most revolts arose from Turkmen groups of Shi'ite leanings nourished by Safavid propaganda. In the second half of the century the ground shifted: timar holders and 'yevmlü' (day-wage) soldiers who had gathered around Prince Bayezid and then scattered, suhtes (madrasa students) who could find no position from the madrasa, peasants who abandoned their farms because there was not enough land, and companies of levends turned out of the households of the beys began to roam about in armed bands. After 1596 the movement became organised around great chiefs. Karayazıcı Abdülhalim seized the citadel of Urfa and sent out decrees under the name 'Halim Shah'; after his death his brother Deli Hasan overran Kütahya and marched on Afyonkarahisar; Tavil Ahmed, Canbolatoğlu Ali and Kalenderoğlu Mehmed followed them. Villagers took refuge within city walls, and the wealthy migrated to Istanbul and Rumelia. The state restored calm first by giving the chiefs offices, then by force through Kuyucu Murad Pasha's campaigns of 1607-1608. The remnants later joined the revolts of Abaza Mehmed Pasha and Abaza Hasan Pasha; after the time of Köprülü Mehmed Pasha the sources began to say 'bandit' (eşkıya) more often than 'Celali'.", "ab5aa12c"]},
"sebep-sonuc-edirne-1829":{"oneri": ["With the Russian army in Edirne, was there any room left to bargain?", "4b1ee7cf"], "sebep.b": ["The intervention of Britain, France and Russia in the Morean revolt of 1821, the sinking of the Ottoman fleet at Navarino and Russia's declaration of war in 1828", "deab6cdf"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Adrianople: the independence of Greece was accepted, and the mouth of the Danube and the Caucasian fortresses passed to Russia", "d1dd5d41"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, the Ottomans, unable to halt the Russian advance, could achieve no success in diplomacy either and were forced to accept the terms dictated by the victor; the payment of 11.5 million Hungarian gold pieces that they accepted was heavier than the treasury could bear.", "aeb7bf97"], "metin": ["On 22 August 1829 the Russian army entered Edirne without meeting resistance and also occupied Kırklareli and Lüleburgaz; while Russian ships attacked the Bosphorus, a squadron blockaded the Dardanelles. The Ottoman delegates who went to Edirne through Prussian mediation could not soften the Russian terms, apart from a small change in the commercial indemnity. The burden was later reduced: in 1830 the indemnity was lowered to 8 million Dutch gold pieces, and a further 1 million was written off on condition that the full independence of Greece be accepted; in January 1834 it was reduced to 5 million. The Russian withdrawal, which was tied to the indemnity, took place from Wallachia and Moldavia only in 1834, and from Silistra under the convention of 8 April 1836.", "6c6fcef5"]},
"sebep-sonuc-hunkar-iskelesi-1833":{"oneri": ["Why did a Russian fleet enter the Bosphorus while the Egyptian army was at Kütahya?", "64a31e5a"], "sebep.b": ["The forces of Mehmed Ali Pasha defeating the Ottoman army at Konya and threatening Istanbul; the acceptance of Russian help by the Sublime Porte, which found no support from France and Britain", "1dedc447"], "sonuc.b": ["The defensive alliance of Hünkâr İskelesi and the secret article providing for the closure of the Dardanelles in Russia's favour", "980eeac1"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, the secret article turned the Egyptian question into an international question of the Straits; it caused a crisis in Europe, gave rise through Metternich's mediation to the Münchengrätz Agreement (18 September 1833) and, when the eight-year term expired, was replaced by the London Straits Convention of 1841.", "c64e9a2b"], "metin": ["When İbrahim Pasha's army defeated Grand Vizier Mehmed Reşid Pasha at Konya on 21 December 1832 and reached Kütahya on 2 February 1833, both the capital and the dynasty came under threat. While calling on Russia for help, the Sublime Porte also wished to use this invitation as a card to spur France and Britain into action; Russia, however, gave it no opportunity: it anchored its fleet at Büyükdere (20 February) and then landed its ground forces at Beykoz (5 April). Through the Kütahya talks, the provinces of Egypt and all Syria, together with Adana, were left to Mehmed Ali's side (firman of 6 May). The Russian forces still did not withdraw; the alliance proposal brought by Orlov was signed on 8 July as the heavy bill for the help, and two days later the Russian fleet and ground forces left the Bosphorus. The TDV also sees behind the indignation felt in Istanbul at the Russian alliance the propaganda that Mehmed Ali Pasha conducted in Europe.", "f9d73540"]},
"sebep-sonuc-karlofca-1699":{"oneri": ["How did the side that lost the war manage to sit bolt upright at the negotiating table?", "35286fc6"], "sebep.b": ["The sixteen-year war on many fronts that began with the 1683 defeat at Vienna: Venice's advance northward from the Morea, the fall of Azov in 1696 and the defeat at Zenta in 1697", "b6bdcf0e"], "sonuc.b": ["Karlowitz: Hungary and Transylvania ceded to Austria, Podolia to Poland and the Morea to Venice; with Russia, only a truce", "f248d009"], "bag": ["Significance: TDV regards Karlowitz as a turning point in Ottoman history — for the first time the state accepted by treaty a loss of territory on such a scale, the peace was made at the table of mediating powers (England and the Netherlands), and the Ottoman-Austrian equality that had been accepted at Zsitvatorok but could not be put into practice was written anew. Even so, according to TDV the Ottoman delegation showed none of the abjectness of a defeated party: by holding on to Temeşvar and to fortresses in Bosnia such as Bihać, it preserved the possibility of recovering the lost places in the future. Ottoman statesmen saw the treaty more as a truce than as a lasting peace — an outlook that found expression in 1711 at the Pruth and then in the war for the Morea declared on Venice.", "c5337a74"], "metin": ["On the road to peace both sides were weary. On the Ottoman side, the army had withdrawn to the Danube after Zenta; Venice, advancing northward from the Morea, had taken Athens and many fortresses in Dalmatia and Bosnia; and the Russians had captured Azov in 1696. Grand Vizier Amcazade Hüseyin Pasha persuaded Mustafa II, who would consent to peace only once part of the losses had been recovered; his arguments were that the people were being crushed under extraordinary taxes, that villages were emptying, that finding soldiers and munitions had become impossible, that public order had broken down in Anatolia and that there were revolts in Rumelia. On the other side, Austria wanted peace because of the question of the Spanish succession, which was expected to erupt; Venice fell in with it, but Poland and Russia, not having obtained what they wanted, favoured continuing the war. The preliminary protocol signed at Edirne on 27 January 1698 accepted the principle of 'alâ hâlihî' (each side keeps what it holds). The talks that began at Karlowitz on 13 November 1698 ran to thirty-six sessions over seventy-two days in a large tent with four doors. The reisülküttab (chief secretary) Rami Mehmed Efendi studied the texts of earlier treaties and obtained reports from experts; even the Austrian delegate Marsigli wrote that the Christian delegates were at times reduced to a pitiable state. The longest wrangle was with Venice: agreement was reached when Austria declared that if the Ottoman demands were not accepted, Venice would carry on the war alone. Because the Russian delegate did not recognise the protocol, only a two-year truce was made with Russia; peace was signed in Istanbul on 14 July 1700. The terms were ill received in Vienna, Warsaw and Venice. The fact that the second delegate, Mavrokordato, later received a decoration from the emperor gave rise to accusations that he had acted against Ottoman interests; TDV says these accusations must be treated with caution.", "8373db96"]},
"sebep-sonuc-kasr-i-sirin-1639":{"oneri": ["How did three days of talks make a frontier for two centuries?", "e2bd586e"], "sebep.b": ["Murad IV's recapture of Baghdad, the withdrawal of the Safavid army to Qasr-e Shirin, and the exhaustion of both sides from the wars fought on the Azerbaijan and Iraq fronts since 1630", "4cf61a2e"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Qasr-e Shirin (Zuhab): Iraq remained with the Ottomans and Yerevan with the Safavids", "f48a09af"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, it put a definitive end to the Ottoman-Safavid dispute and was taken as the basis in later centuries: the Treaty of Kerden of 1746 renewed it, and the Erzurum treaties of 1823 and 1847 settled frontier questions on its basis.", "81507167"], "metin": ["The peace also brought economic returns. Shah Abbas's transfer of his capital from Tabriz to Isfahan and his founding of New Julfa for the Armenian merchants had shifted the trade routes southward; in the climate of peace, Iranian silk and textiles were carried via Baghdad to Aleppo, and via Mosul to Diyarbekir and the cities of Anatolia. According to the figures reported by the TDV, Iran's silk exports rose by about half between 1640 and 1670. This balanced period lasted until Nader Shah, founder of the Afshar dynasty, came to power in 1736.", "35eec015"]},
"sebep-sonuc-kasrisirin-arka-plan":{"oneri": ["A sixteen-year war began with the loss of a city and ended with the recovery of that same city.", "23cfbcc9"], "sebep.b": ["Baghdad's passing into the hands of Shah Abbas's forces during the turmoil caused in the city by Bekir Subaşı", "b86ef9cf"], "sonuc.b": ["The Treaty of Qasr-e Shirin (Zuhab): Baghdad remained with the Ottomans, Yerevan (Revan) with the Safavids", "b93b6daf"], "bag": ["Significance: the two great campaigns of Murad IV's reign — Yerevan (1635) and Baghdad (1638) — prepared the ground for this treaty. The treaty closed the last of the Ottoman-Safavid wars that had continued intermittently since Chaldiran in 1514, and opened a long period of peace between the two states.", "e0818efd"], "metin": ["When Bekir Subaşı, who held Baghdad, was not granted the governorship, he defended the city against the Ottoman army while at the same time asking Shah Abbas for help; at the end of these events the city passed into Safavid hands in the Hijri year 1033 (1623-24). In Istanbul the young Murad IV, who had only just ascended the throne, gave priority to recovering Baghdad. The first attempt was the siege by Hâfız Ahmed Pasha, which produced no result in 1626. In 1630 Husrev Pasha set the Shahrizor region in order and besieged Baghdad for the second time (the siege began on 6 October 1630); he too failed. Meanwhile Shah Abbas had died in 1629 and his grandson Shah Safi had succeeded him. When the Safavids expanded into Georgia and attacked Van, Murad IV took personal command of the army: on 8 August 1635, after eleven days of resistance, Yerevan surrendered, and the evacuated city of Tabriz was devastated. However, once the army withdrew, Shah Safi retook Yerevan on 1 April 1636 and sent an envoy to Istanbul to seek peace. The sultan did not receive the envoy and let it be known that the answer would be given at Baghdad. The great army that set out on 8 May 1638 arrived before Baghdad in mid-November; at the end of a siege lasting weeks, the fortress commander Bektash Khan surrendered the city on 24 December 1638. Negotiating the terms of peace, however, fell not to the sultan but to the grand vizier Kemankeş Kara Mustafa Pasha, whom he had left in Baghdad.", "95c4984d"]},
"sebep-sonuc-kasrisirin-kalicilik":{"oneri": ["A treaty made in three days was still the basic document on the table two hundred years later.", "c032ddd9"], "sebep.b": ["The drawing of the Ottoman-Safavid frontier in its main lines by the Treaty of Qasr-e Shirin", "8e099403"], "sonuc.b": ["The settlement of frontier questions on the basis of Qasr-e Shirin in the Treaty of Kerden of 1746 and the Erzurum treaties of 1823 and 1847", "79fd5706"], "bag": ["Significance: the TDV defines this treaty as the one that determined the present Turkish-Iranian border. The southern section of the same line today lies between Iraq and Iran; that is, the framework drawn in 1639 continued to live on in the background of two separate modern borders even after the Ottoman State had ceased to exist.", "e8f89fad"], "metin": ["The talks at Zuhab were over in three days, but relations between the two states were long conducted according to this treaty. The Treaty of Kerden, signed with Nader Shah in 1746, renewed the order of 1639. The first Treaty of Erzurum in 1823 was signed after Iran had marched on Erzurum and then sued for peace because of a cholera epidemic in its army, and Iran returned the places it had taken; the second Treaty of Erzurum in 1847 likewise tried to settle the frontier questions once again on the basis of 1639. The peace also had an economic side: Iranian silk, whose trade had revived with the moving of the Safavid capital to Isfahan and the concentration of Armenian merchants in New Julfa, flowed into Ottoman lands along the Baghdad-Aleppo and Mosul-Diyarbekir roads. According to the calculations reported by the TDV, Iran's silk exports grew by half between 1640 and 1670. This calm period lasted until Nader Shah, founder of the Afshar dynasty, came to power in 1736. A caution is also needed: to say that 'the frontier was determined' does not mean that a line measured and marked in the present-day sense was drawn. As the historian Sabri Ateş emphasises, this broad frontier region could only be turned into a definite, internationally recognised boundary through the efforts of mixed international commissions that worked intermittently between 1843 and 1914.", "41e20771"]},
"sebep-sonuc-korfez-basra-isgali-kuveyt":{"sebep.b": ["The Zand occupation of Basra (1776-1779)", "ae2060f1"], "sonuc.b": ["The shift of the India trade and the British desert mail to Kuwait", "9fcb3446"], "bag": ["Significance: In the three years during which Basra was closed, a small pearling and fishing town set out on the road to becoming the largest port and entrepôt of north-eastern Arabia. All of Kuwait's later political importance rests on this commercial rise.", "46a5d08a"], "metin": ["① Foundation. The name Kuwait is accepted to mean ‘little fort’, after the fort there (TDV). According to some sources the city was founded by the Utub, a branch of the Anaza tribe that was settled in the region at the end of the 17th century. For the date of foundation, the TDV gives the first half of the 17th century in one place and the beginning of the 18th century in another; Lorimer places the foundation in 1716. Of the three branches of the Utub, the Al Sabah came to the fore, and from the end of the 18th century the kaymakams (district governors) began to be appointed from among the Sabah sheikhs. The other two branches left the city. One of them, the Al Khalifa, settled at Zubara on the coast of Qatar (the TDV places this migration in 1776, Lorimer in 1766) and took Bahrain in 1783 (TDV). ② Why Kuwait? Basra was a river port, and in the second half of the 18th century ships, which were growing ever larger, had difficulty going up the Shatt al-Arab to Basra. Kuwait, on the other hand, lay on the shore of a deep bay suitable for large ships. For this reason the trade between Istanbul, Aleppo, Baghdad and Basra on the one hand and India on the other gradually shifted to Kuwait (TDV). Carsten Niebuhr had written in the 1760s that about 10,000 people lived in the city and that there were some 800 boats in the harbour (TDV). ③ When Basra closed. The Iranian occupation of Basra (1776-1779) turned the India trade towards Kuwait. The port grew busy, the city expanded and it became an important commercial centre on the route from Aleppo to the Gulf (TDV). According to Lorimer, from 1778 the English East India Company's desert mail to Aleppo began to be dispatched from Kuwait instead of Basra. ④ The first stop of the British. In 1793 the British, having fallen out with the Ottoman administrators in Basra, moved their commercial centre to Kuwait until 1795. In 1820 they also moved their political agent in Basra to Kuwait. As the people did not want this official, in 1821 they settled him on the island of Failaka at the entrance to the harbour (TDV). ⑤ Relations with Iran. The rise of Kuwait coincides with the period of Iran's retreat in the Gulf. After Karim Khan's death in 1779, civil war began in Iran. Lorimer sums up this period as the years in which Iran lost Basra in 1779 and Bahrain in 1783. The Utub of Kuwait were at war with the Ka'b tribe on the Iranian side in 1780 (Lorimer).", "1072b6ae"]},
"sebep-sonuc-kucuk-kaynarca-1774":{"oneri": ["Why was a peace signed in a besieged army camp misread for a century?", "9811fc1f"], "sebep.b": ["The war of 1768, declared without preparation: the occupation of the Crimea and of Wallachia and Moldavia, the raid at Çeşme, the collapse of the Bucharest talks and, after the rout at Kozluca in 1774, the encirclement of the army headquarters at Shumen (Şumnu)", "757943f7"], "sonuc.b": ["Küçük Kaynarca: the political separation of the Crimean Khanate from the Ottomans, freedom for Russian ships in the Black Sea and the Mediterranean, and an indemnity of 15,000 purses", "f5c01ade"], "bag": ["Significance: According to TDV, this war both laid bare Ottoman weakness and testified to Russia's emergence as a great power. In the official French translation that Russia printed in 1775, the church to be built in Istanbul was rendered not as a 'Greek' (Rum) church but as a 'Russian' one; this falsification gave Russia a claim to a general protectorate over the Ottoman Orthodox and became one of the causes of the Crimean War. The claim was formally ended only by the Reform Edict of 1856 and the Treaty of Paris. On the other hand, Russia's recognition of the caliphal tie with the Crimea is counted as an important gain, even if it did not make up for the loss.", "b9b6b770"], "metin": ["The war was declared on account of Russia's interference in Poland's internal affairs and its attacks on the Crimean frontier — with just cause, but without any preparation. The Russians soon occupied the Crimea and Wallachia and Moldavia and crossed the Danube. In the Ottoman army, bad leadership, indiscipline, the collapsed menzil (post station) system and shortages of provisions heralded a general disintegration. The Russians were not at ease either; the Pugachev revolt that was shaking the country turned them towards peace talks — Frederick the Great had described this war as a 'war of the one-eyed against the blind'. In the talks held at Focșani and Bucharest in 1772-1773, granting independence to the Crimea was considered contrary to Islamic law, and the surrender of fortresses and an indemnity were also rejected amid the sharp reaction of the ulema in Istanbul; the war resumed in March 1773. In the spring of 1774 the Russians inflicted a heavy defeat on the Ottoman forces at Kozluca (on 25 June according to TDV) and besieged the headquarters at Shumen. Because even the interpreters had fled in the rout, Marshal Rumyantsev's peace letter went untranslated for several days. With the camp, reduced to 7-8,000 men, under siege, Rumyantsev insisted on negotiating while the war continued, without declaring a truce. The Ottoman delegates, Ahmed Resmi Efendi and Münib Efendi, held talks at Küçük Kaynarca on 16, 17 and 21 July; when the approval of the gravely ill grand vizier arrived, the treaty was signed on the evening of 21 July 1774. Catherine II found the terms more satisfactory than expected.", "494c3856"]},
"sebep-sonuc-mondros-1918":{"oneri": ["How did an armistice open the door to occupation?", "c3bb9cbb"], "sebep.b": ["The collapse of the Palestine–Syria front, and Bulgaria's signing of a ceasefire and withdrawal from the war, which left Thrace and Istanbul open to attack", "585fe0aa"], "sonuc.b": ["Armistice of Mudros: the disbanding of the army and navy, and the right of occupation given to the Entente powers by Articles 7 and 24", "26993965"], "bag": ["Significance: The TDV regards Mudros as the herald of the end of the Ottoman State's six-century history; the occupations that began with the abuse of Articles 7 and 24 gave rise to the War of Independence led by Mustafa Kemal Pasha and to the founding of the Republic of Turkey.", "6091cf80"], "metin": ["The British offensive that began on the Palestine–Syria front on 19 September 1918 routed the Ottoman forces; with Bulgaria's withdrawal on 29 September, the land link with the allies was cut. Two peace offers made by the Ottomans through Spain (5 and 12 October) went unanswered; the government of Talat Pasha, having received a vote of no confidence, was replaced by that of Ahmed İzzet Pasha (14 October), and Admiral Calthorpe was reached through the captive British General Townshend. Trusting in Wilson's principles, the government sent its delegation in the expectation that there would be no interference in the administration and no landing of foreign troops in the country; the text signed, however, dismantled the Ottoman defence system almost entirely. The British occupation of Mosul within a few days on the basis of Article 7 confirmed the suspicions; the Committee of Union and Progress dissolved itself on 1 November, and Enver, Cemal and Talat Pashas secretly left the country.", "3df03dd8"]},
"sebep-sonuc-p75b-donanma-iskenderiye-1839":{"sebep.b": ["The death of Mahmud II immediately after the defeat at Nizip, and Koca Hüsrev Pasha's seizure of the grand vizier's seal", "d0bba312"], "sonuc.b": ["Kapudan Pasha (Grand Admiral) Ahmed Fevzi Pasha takes the fleet to Alexandria and leaves it in the hands of Mehmed Ali Pasha, governor of Egypt", "1a8520b2"], "bag": ["Significance: because the fleet was handed over without a fight, the Ottoman State was in a short time left without an army, without a sultan and without a fleet; from this moment the Egyptian question turned into an international crisis resolved by European intervention (joint note of 27 July 1839).", "c914fb1b"], "metin": ["① The question: 'How can an entire fleet be handed over to a rebel governor?' Dönmez's article seeks to answer precisely this question with a new document, the report of Avidick, Fevzi Pasha's interpreter, found in the British National Archives. The first surprising finding is this: there was no agreement whatsoever between Fevzi Pasha and Mehmed Ali. Dönmez also writes that Fevzi Pasha was known as someone who did not like the governor of Egypt. The fleet (25 ships and about 25,000 sailors, as Dönmez cites Koçu) had put out into the Mediterranean shortly before the sultan's death, and at the time of the change of reign it was in the vicinity of the Dardanelles.\n\n② The setting. With the death of Mahmud II (1 July 1839), the balance among the factions at the Sublime Porte was upset. During the funeral ceremony Hüsrev Pasha took the imperial seal by force and had himself declared grand vizier; the TDV writes that 'everyone regarded this conduct as outright usurpation' (3 July 1839, TDV Hüsrev Pasha; TDV Abdülmecid says 2 July). Hüsrev Pasha was Fevzi Pasha's rival and summoned him to Istanbul.\n\n③ The chain of fear. According to Avidick's report, Fevzi Pasha now feared that he would be removed from the post of Grand Admiral, that his enemy Tahir Pasha would replace him, and that he himself might be killed. The man who magnified this fear for him was Osman Bey, the second-ranking commander in the fleet (riyale, rear admiral) (Topal Osman Pasha; he has a separate article in the TDV): he recounted as events that had actually happened the rumours that Hüsrev and Halil Pasha had poisoned the sultan and were preparing to hand the state over to Russia. This rumour itself is treated in a separate card (`tartisma-p75b-husrev-donanma-rusya-vehmi`).\n\n④ Encouragement from outside. According to Osman Bey's account, the meeting with Admiral Lalande, commander of the French fleet in the Mediterranean, was presented as a plan to 'join with Mehmed Ali and save the Ottoman state'; according to Avidick, the admiral did not ask for the fleet's surrender but advised joining the governor of Egypt and marching on Istanbul, and said that he would join them. The French ambassador rejected this, saying that Avidick was not reliable; Lalande, for his part, later claimed that Fevzi Pasha had threatened to run the fleet aground. Here the sources contradict one another.\n\n⑤ Alexandria. The fleet reached Alexandria on 14 July (Dönmez; based on the report of the European consuls of 15 July). Mehmed Ali at first thought it was an attack and panicked; when he learned the situation, he received Fevzi Pasha. Shortly afterwards the governor of Egypt, on the pretext of a British attack, brought the fleet into the harbour and took away the weapons of the Ottoman officers and sailors; Dönmez describes this as a 'fait accompli'. The officers were dressed in Egyptian uniforms, and those who wished to return were placed under watch; it is recorded that Fevzi Pasha and the commanders were 'remorseful'. In place of Fevzi Pasha, Damat Said Mehmed Pasha was made Grand Admiral (early 1840; Dönmez's footnotes rest on correspondence dated January-February 1840).\n\n⑥ The outcome. On 27 July 1839 the five powers delivered a joint note; on 15 July 1840 the Treaty of London was signed; when Mehmed Ali did not accept it, a joint naval operation was carried out and Mehmed Ali concluded the Convention of Alexandria with Napier (27 November 1840). The fleet was taken back at Alexandria in January 1841 (Dönmez; TDV İbrahim Pasha, Mehmed Ali Pasha of Kavala). ⑦ 'A simple betrayal?' To Dönmez's question, the TDV article on Hüsrev Pasha calls Fevzi Pasha 'Firârî, Hâin' (the Fugitive, the Traitor) and writes that his aim was to join the Egyptian forces, come to Istanbul and rescue the state from the hands of Hüsrev Pasha; Dönmez, for his part, frames the event as 'a result of the factional conflicts at the Sublime Porte'. The two readings do not exclude each other.", "33989b69"]},
"sebep-sonuc-p75b-soci-tuapse-1838":{"sebep.b": ["The Ottoman Empire's renunciation of its rights over the Circassians in favour of Russia by the Treaty of Adrianople (Edirne) of 1829", "1b9e0566"], "sonuc.b": ["The Russians' seizure of the ports of Sochi and Tuapse in 1838 and their building of new forts and guard posts in the interior of Circassia", "7654e207"], "bag": ["Significance: Circassia's connection with the outside world by way of the Black Sea was cut; the possibility of receiving arms and support from the coast narrowed. This preparation is the military groundwork of the deportation of 1861-1864.", "088015a3"], "metin": ["① What is written in the source. The TDV article 'Circassians' (Sadık Müfit Bilge, 2020): the Ottoman Empire, which lost its territories in the Caucasus by the Treaty of Adrianople (Edirne) at the end of the Russo-Ottoman war of 1828-1829, renounced its rights over the Circassians in favour of Russia; the Russians, who were trying to cut the Circassians' relations with the outside world by way of the Black Sea, seized the ports of Sochi and Tuapse in 1838 and built new forts and guard posts in the interior of the country. This sentence is the sole basis of the chronology entry ('1838, no day in the source'); TDV gives neither day nor month.\n\n② Context. According to the same TDV article, before the Caucasian-Russian wars (1763-1864) began, the Circassians occupied an area of more than 100,000 km², a quarter of the Caucasus, stretching from the Black Sea coast in the west to the Sunzha river in the east, and from the Sea of Azov in the north to the Caucasus mountains in the south. The community living on the coast between Taman and Sochi, which Ottoman sources called the 'Aşağıra Circassians', had recognised Ottoman sovereignty. The seizure of Sochi and Tuapse was an attempt to cut this coastal strip's relations with the Ottomans. The TDV article 'Abazas' shows that Sochi is also an old frontier name in Abaza history (Bagrat's taking of places 'from Tiflis as far as Sochi'). The TDV article on the Treaty of Adrianople writes that fortresses ceded to Russia in Anatolia, such as Akhaltsikhe (Ahıska), Akhalkalaki, Anapa and Poti, were mentioned in the calculation of the war indemnity.\n\n③ Not found. The day of the move of 1838, which commander led it and how many soldiers were landed on the coast are not in these sources. The process by which Sochi and Tuapse later left the Ottoman frontiers and passed under Russian administration also appears in the TDV article 'Circassians' only in this single sentence.", "8fe9ab4e"]},
"sebep-sonuc-paris-1856":{"oneri": ["Admission to Europe on paper", "a9a49644"], "sebep.b": ["Russia's demand for an internationally valid right of protection over the Orthodox population in the Ottoman Empire, the dispute over the 'holy places' and the Crimean War, which began in 1853", "e7a6090e"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Paris: the Ottomans were accepted as an equal member of the European states system, and the Black Sea was neutralised", "cbf265be"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, it ended the superiority that the treaties of Küçük Kaynarca, Akkerman and Adrianople had given Russia, changed the Straits regime of 1841 and was interpreted as having checked Russia's expansion towards the Ottomans, which had been under way since Catherine II. Because of the Reform Edict, it also drew the state into an important constitutional change.", "afbe2766"], "metin": ["The guarantees given soon ceased to work. When France was defeated by Prussia in 1870–1871, Russia announced, before that war had ended, that it would no longer abide by the Black Sea clause; the TDV regards this as the first sign of the great Ottoman-Russian war that was approaching. The guarantee of territorial integrity could not prevent the great dismemberment in the war of 1877–1878. The burden of debt left by the war and the social conflicts that arose in the implementation of the Reform Edict shaped the period that followed. The TDV reports that at the Congress of Berlin (1878) Bismarck rebuked the Ottoman delegates, who were trying to defend their rights, by telling them that European law was not for them — a sign of how far the status won in 1856 had remained on paper.", "71cfb416"]},
"sebep-sonuc-pasarofca-1718":{"oneri": ["The 1718 Treaty of Passarowitz — the Morea stayed, Belgrade was lost", "809d732f"], "sebep.b": ["The desire to avenge Karlowitz: the war declared on Venice; Austria's alliance with Venice in 1716 and its entry into the war; the defeats at Petrovaradin and Belgrade", "bddbeceb"], "sonuc.b": ["Passarowitz: the Morea remained Ottoman; the Banat, Oltenia (Lesser Wallachia), northern Serbia including Belgrade, and northern Bosnia passed to Austria", "c5eb1153"], "bag": ["Significance: According to TDV, the treaty served Austria more than Venice; with its great territorial losses the Ottoman state not only withdrew from Central Europe but also began to cease being a strong state in European eyes. The commercial treaty signed in the same days granted Austrian merchants free trade at a 3% customs duty and the right to maintain consuls and dragomans in all ports; in return the Ottomans too would be able to open consulates (şehbenderlik) in Austrian territory, and a consul (şehbender) was sent to Vienna. Belgrade and northern Serbia were recovered only by the 1739 Treaty of Belgrade.", "c503d80b"], "metin": ["Ottoman statesmen regarded Karlowitz as a truce. After halting Russia at the Pruth, they declared war on Venice, which they claimed had violated the treaty; the Ottoman army and navy had the upper hand in this war. Grand Vizier Damad Ali Pasha hoped Austria would remain neutral, but Prince Eugene favoured war, and on 13 April 1716 the Austrian-Venetian alliance was signed. In Istanbul some statesmen proposed placating Austria by returning a few unimportant islands to Venice; when the grand vizier threatened them with death they fell silent, and war was declared. Ali Pasha fell in battle at Petrovaradin, the Banat and Temeşvar passed to Austria, and in 1717 Belgrade fell as well. The question of peace set two poles against each other at court: at the head of the war party were Numan Pasha and the Köprülü family, and tradesmen who were profiting from high prices also wanted the war to go on; the people, however, were exhausted, and the sultan favoured peace. Charles VI's harsh terms, demanding Bosnia, Serbia and Wallachia, enraged Ahmed III, but through the mediation of the English and Dutch ambassadors it was agreed to negotiate on the basis of the existing situation. In the negotiations that began on 5 June 1718, Austria first made the surrender of Rákóczi and his men a condition; the Ottomans refused, and Austria backed down. In truth neither side was in a condition to fight: Austria was on the brink of a new conflict with Spain. The Ottomans asked for a fifteen-year peace; Austria's proposal of twenty-four years was accepted. After seventy days, the treaties with Austria and Venice were signed on 21 July 1718.", "ef9861d9"]},
"sebep-sonuc-sevr-1920":{"oneri": ["Debated for nineteen months at the partition table, forced on Istanbul for signature, never applied: what killed Sèvres…", "1720b20a"], "sebep.b": ["Paris Peace Conference: the victorious powers began to discuss the future of the Ottoman lands", "d3f8317e"], "sonuc.b": ["The Treaty of Sèvres was signed — the Ankara government did not recognise it, and the text could not be applied", "d11a8e42"], "bag": ["Significance: Sèvres was the written form of the solution Europe devised for the 'Eastern Question'. According to TDV, the 1923 Treaty of Lausanne preserved the distribution of the Arab mandates made at Sèvres, while introducing important changes in all matters concerning the new Turkish state.", "d797533f"], "metin": ["The decision-making body of the conference was the Council of Ten, composed of the prime ministers and foreign ministers of Britain, France, Italy, the USA and Japan; it determined the regions to be wholly separated from the Ottomans — Arabia, Syria, Mesopotamia, Palestine, Armenia and, on a British proposal, Kurdistan. The Allies could not agree: France and Italy wanted the secret wartime agreements to be implemented, whereas the USA wanted Ottoman rule to continue in regions with a Turkish majority, in accordance with Wilson's twelfth point. The Greeks, supported by Britain, France and the USA, landed at İzmir on 15 May 1919; this landing led to the rapid organisation of the national resistance in Anatolia. Faced with the demand of Indian Muslims that the caliph remain in Istanbul, the British government on 6 January 1920 rejected the plan to expel the Turks from Europe. On 16 March 1920 Istanbul was officially occupied; on 23 April the Grand National Assembly began its work in Ankara. The text was given its final form at San Remo (18-26 April 1920) and communicated to Tevfik Pasha on 11 May; Tevfik Pasha stated that he could not accept the terms. Lloyd George set the Greek army in motion on 22 June; after Bursa fell on 8 July and Thrace on 20 July, the Council of the Sultanate convened by the sultan on 22 July, in the face of reports that Istanbul would be occupied by the Greeks, voted in favour of signature, with the exception of Lieutenant General of Artillery Rıza Pasha. What followed came quickly: after Sakarya, France made an agreement with the Ankara government on 20 October 1921 and recognised the new state, and even Lloyd George declared on 10 August 1921 that Sèvres had now been torn up. The victory of 30 August 1922 was followed by the Armistice of Mudanya on 11 October, the abolition of the sultanate on 1 November and the Treaty of Lausanne on 24 July 1923.", "a078f4bf"]},
"sebep-sonuc-usi-1912":{"oneri": ["The last Ottoman province in Africa was given up at a table in Switzerland; but the war did not end there, and in the Aegean…", "4fa24612"], "sebep.b": ["Italy's declaration of war on the Ottoman State on the grounds of protecting its citizens in Tripoli", "8d96d53b"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Ouchy: the Ottoman State accepted that it had ceded Tripoli to Italy", "af6dab0c"], "bag": ["Significance: The loss of the Ottoman State's last territory on the African continent. The treaty's loose end was tied up at Lausanne: Rhodes and the Dodecanese, which Italy was supposed to evacuate under the Treaty of Ouchy, remained in its hands, and Turkey formally renounced its rights over Tripoli.", "b90736c2"], "metin": ["As the colonial race in Africa gathered pace in the second half of the 19th century, Italy's interest in Tripoli grew. According to TDV, Italy declared war in September 1911 on the pretext of protecting its citizens living in the city from the Ottoman government, and this was the beginning of the province's transformation into an Italian colony; during the occupation a significant part of the city's population perished. By the treaty signed in 1912 at Ouchy in Lausanne, Switzerland, the Ottoman State accepted that it had abandoned to Italy this last province of its in Africa; the caliph, however, would continue to be regarded as the religious leader of the Muslim community there. The treaty did not in fact end the conflict: Ottoman soldiers carried on the resistance together with the local people and fought against Italy under the name 'Africa Groups Command' until the Armistice of Mudros in 1918. Tripoli remained under Italian occupation until 1943, then came under British control, and independence was proclaimed in 1951. The other end of the treaty was in the Aegean: according to TDV's Lausanne article, Rhodes and the Dodecanese, which the Italians kept under occupation although they were obliged to return them under the Treaty of Ouchy, were left to them in 1923 by Article 15 of Lausanne; by Article 22, the rights over Tripoli were also renounced.", "a97d4df9"]},
"sebep-sonuc-vasvar-1664":{"oneri": ["Heavy losses at Saint Gotthard, gains at the table", "e178b653"], "sebep.b": ["Habsburg intervention in Transylvania, Zrínyi's building of a fortress opposite Kanizsa and Fazıl Ahmed Pasha's campaign of 1663, in which he took Uyvar", "c54bfafe"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Vasvár: the withdrawal of both sides from Transylvania, the retention of Uyvar and Novigrad by the Ottomans, and a twenty-year peace", "739b5091"], "bag": ["Significance: The TDV describes this treaty as the text that defined the widest Ottoman frontiers in Hungary. It also carries an innovation in legal terms: whereas at Zsitvatorok the peace rested on the sultan's personal pledge, Mehmed IV's ahidname of ratification also bound the rulers who would descend from him — the principle of the continuity of the state. But the peace alienated the Hungarian nobles; this discontent led to the Thököly revolt and, in the second half of the 1670s, to the two empires confronting each other again.", "b98a8982"], "metin": ["The crisis began in Transylvania: after the death of George II Rákóczi, the Ottomans took Várad on 27 August 1660, while the Transylvanian nobles consented to the stationing of German troops in some castles. In the talks held for two years with the Habsburg envoy in Istanbul, Simon Reniger, the 1662 draft of the reisülküttab (chief of the chancery) Şamizade Mehmed Efendi already contained most of the articles later signed; when Vienna dragged its feet, Fazıl Ahmed Pasha set out on campaign in 1663 and took Uyvar after a six-week siege. In 1664, after the unexpected attacks of Zrínyi and Hohenlohe in January and April, the allies besieged Kanizsa at the end of April; the siege could be raised only at the beginning of June, and at the end of June the Ottomans took and burned Zrínyi's Yenikale. On 1 August 1664, Ottoman units attempting to cross the River Rába suffered heavy losses against Montecuccoli's army (Saint Gotthard). This defeat strengthened the desire for peace at the Ottoman headquarters; eight days later the texts were exchanged in Fazıl Ahmed Pasha's tent. Leopold I delayed ratification in the hope of better terms, but because of the condition of his army and the Imperial Diet's reluctance for war, he ratified on 9 September; the confirmation took place with a ceremony near Uyvar on 27 October 1664. A note on the date: the treaty was signed on 9 August 1664; because the date in the Turkish text was wrongly converted, it appears in Western historiography as 10 August.", "598b1e60"]},
"sebep-sonuc-yas-1792":{"oneri": ["The army refused to fight", "d48bcd0d"], "sebep.b": ["Russia's annexation of the Crimea in 1783 and the Ottoman declaration of war on Russia with the aim of recovering the Crimea; Austria's entry into the war on Russia's side as well", "fa10a91f"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Jassy: the Dniester (Turla) became the frontier, and the hope of recovering the Crimea came to an end", "17bec341"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, the dream of recovering the Crimea was over, and with Russia's extension into Georgia the danger that would come from the Caucasus became apparent. The army's open declaration that it could not fight made the restructuring of the state in every field urgent; the peace was the starting point of the Nizam-i Cedid reforms.", "b7f66d88"], "metin": ["After the rout at Măcin (9 July 1791), the civil and military notables who gathered in the army camp acknowledged that the army no longer had the strength to fight and sealed a memorandum in favour of peace. The TDV describes this as a 'boycott', in the sense of the army's refusal to fight, without precedent in Ottoman history; Selim III, who wanted the war to continue, was left with no option but peace. Developments in Europe also played a part: Britain had opposed Ochakov (Özi) remaining with Russia with a firmness that went as far as the threat of war, and the danger that the French Revolution posed to monarchies had pushed Catherine II to end the Turkish war. The grand embassies sent by each side to the other after the peace were the last example of this tradition.", "1ecbc422"]},
"sebep-sonuc-zitvatorok-1606":{"oneri": ["Why did a title outweigh a fortress?", "95e3cb68"], "sebep.b": ["The financial straits into which the long war begun in 1593 had driven both sides; the Celali revolts in Anatolia, the Safavid counter-offensive in the east and the revolt of Prince Bocskay of Transylvania against the Habsburgs", "db194902"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Zsitvatorok: a peace in which the Habsburg ruler was regarded as the sultan's equal and the annual tribute was abolished", "b998d332"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, this was the first treaty in Habsburg-Ottoman relations to be decided by joint negotiation rather than depending on the sultan's unilateral goodwill; the two rulers dealt with each other as equal parties, and in diplomatic practice the Ottomans' superiority over their Christian rival came to an end.", "315f14fa"], "metin": ["Peace soundings had been made since 1596 in the winter months, outside the campaigning season, but had produced no result. In November 1604 the revolt of István Bocskay, Prince of Transylvania, against the Habsburgs interrupted the talks; the Hungarian nobles became the decisive party between the two great powers. Grand Vizier Lala Mehmed Pasha at first preferred to continue the war, but he died in June 1606 while preparing for the campaign. The treaty was later extended six times, and its terms remained in force for sixty years.", "2ac692fb"]},
"sehzade-mustafa-katli-entrika":{"soru": ["What was Hürrem's part in the killing of Mustafa?", "d7d02c34"], "baslik": ["The killing of Prince Mustafa: a palace intrigue?", "3466a5ed"], "metin": ["Suleiman the Lawgiver had his eldest son Mustafa, beloved of the army and the people, strangled in the army camp near Konya Ereğlisi, on the way to the Iranian campaign; before the decision, Rüstem Pasha had informed the sultan that the prince was preparing to seize the throne. The TDV article on Hürrem, relying on the historian Âlî, has no doubt that Hürrem, her daughter Mihrimah and her son-in-law Rüstem Pasha played a large part in the affair. The same encyclopaedia's article on Prince Mustafa, however, stresses that although Ottoman sources attribute the event to a palace intrigue in which these figures played the leading roles, this account falls far short of explaining why the sipahis and landless peasants had rallied around the prince.", "5b1a6867"], "not": ["The execution itself is certain; what is disputed is Hürrem's share in it. The sources give the day of the execution as 5 or 6 October 1553.", "8a998c64"]},
"selim3-mahmud2-28-temmuz-1808":{"soru": ["Whose accession did the cannon fire announce?", "d6348998"], "baslik": ["Alemdar broke down the gates and found a corpse", "f47a5e19"], "metin": ["When Alemdar Mustafa Pasha raided the Sublime Porte on 28 July 1808 and marched on the palace to restore Selim III to the throne, Mustafa IV had the gates closed and gave permission for his uncle Selim and his brother Mahmud to be killed. Selim, who had spent days in close company with his nephew Mahmud in the cage (kafes), struggled with some twenty assassins. It is reported that the steward Ebe Selim overpowered him and the executioner strangled him; but the marks of blows described by Cevdet Pasha point to a bloody death. Alemdar, who broke down the gates and entered, came upon the corpse laid on a mattress in the hall in front of the Audience Chamber. When cannon fire was heard from Sarayburnu, everyone, including the embassies, thought that Selim had returned to his throne; an hour later criers proclaimed that the new sultan was Mahmud, who had been spirited away with difficulty and saved.", "ecdba928"], "not": ["The manner of Selim's killing is given in the sources in two different accounts.", "0a884636"]},
"seyahat-abdulaziz-1867-avrupa-basini":{"baslik": ["Abdülaziz through the eyes of the European press (1867)", "54666ade"], "metin": ["■ The expected image\nOn the occasion of the visit, British newspapers tried to explain to their readers the condition of the Ottoman state and who the sultan was. When these pieces are examined, what is really interesting is not what is described but the astonishment of those describing it: the image of the Muslim that had formed in the European mind since the Crusades did not match at all the calm and harmless ruler who appeared before them.\n\n■ THE EXTENT OF THE DISTORTION\nAccording to an article compiling material from the newspapers of the period, the Turk's dress, manner and habits had been so exaggerated and distorted that in the Western mind they evoked a mythical creature. The common expectation was this: the bowstring and the scimitar were his constant playthings; when he took a city, it was inevitable that he would kill all the men and take the women and children captive. The sultan was the head of this vague fear.\n\n■ WHAT WAS SEEN\nThe real picture was completely different: he had neither his harem nor a military guard regiment with him, he dressed simply, and in his modern clothes he differed from Europeans only by his red fez. Many publications described him as modest, level-headed and far from ostentation; it was emphasised that he was a good musician and composer. Some wrote that he did not drink alcohol, remained faithful to the requirements of his belief and persistently refused wine. One magazine, noting that the day he arrived was a Friday, commented that the sultan paid no heed to religious days; another newspaper, by contrast, reported that he fasted and had many virtues that could serve as an example to his people. In other words, the press contradicted itself too.\n\n■ THE CONDESCENSION WITHIN THE PRAISE\nThe Christian Times, describing the Ottoman state as England's loyal ally, wrote that freedom of worship was granted to all subjects without distinction of religion, language or race, that no one could be forced to change religion, and that these guarantees did not exist even in many Christian countries of continental Europe. The same newspaper described the sultan as \"not a Saladin or a Harun al-Rashid, but a good Muslim brought up in a more liberal school\" and reminded readers that he was the religious leader of about twenty million Muslims in India.\n\n■ BUT ON THE SAME PAGES\nThe same pieces explained the poverty of the Turks by laziness and indifference, said that the capitalists of the East were the Armenians and Greeks, claimed that the Turks had lost their strength because of their religion, and argued that the Quran should be changed in accordance with the spirit of the age. In other words, the visit broke the prejudice to some degree, but what it put in its place was again a language of tutelage.\n\n■ NOTE TO THE READER\nThis card is a compilation from newspapers, not a verdict. It shows what the press of the period said; not whether what it said was true.", "4e6af575"], "kisa": ["The British press found the sultan different from what it expected — and that astonishment itself shows how distorted Europe's image of the Turk was.", "9ba88824"]},
"seyahat-abdulaziz-1867-guzergah":{"baslik": ["Abdülaziz's European tour (1867) — why and where?", "d97b7a4d"], "metin": ["■ Why he went\nThe invitation was twofold. Napoleon III, Emperor of the French, invited the sultan to France on the occasion of the opening of the International Exposition in Paris, and also conveyed through his ambassador in Istanbul that on this occasion there could be an exchange of views to strengthen general peace. When Queen Victoria of Britain also invited him to London at the same time, Abdülaziz accepted both invitations at once. In the background were the events in Crete, Serbia and Romania, that is, unrest backed by France and Russia; the journey was not a courtesy visit but a diplomatic move.\n\n■ DEPARTURE\nThe sultan left the Istanbul quay on 21 June 1867 aboard the ship Sultaniye. His convoy was protected by the corvette Forbin, carrying the French ambassador, together with three ships of the French navy and two of the British navy. With him were his ten-year-old son Yusuf İzzeddin, his twenty-seven-year-old nephew Murad and his twenty-five-year-old nephew Abdülhamid, the Foreign Minister Fuad Pasha, Ömer Faiz Efendi and senior officials. In other words, two future sultans were also aboard this ship.\n\n■ THE FRENCH LEG\nThe delegation reached the port of Toulon at the end of June; from there it travelled by train to Lyon and then to Paris. Napoleon III received the sultan in person, and together they went to the Élysée Palace. On Monday, 1 July, he attended the exhibition prize-giving ceremony held at the Palace of Industry; at the ceremony Selahaddin Bey, chief commissioner of the Ottoman exhibits, was awarded the officer's rank of the Légion d'honneur and Colonel Esad Bey the rank of knight. He toured the exhibition for the last time on 10 July, sent to Istanbul the Sèvres porcelain and crystal he had bought, and on 12 July 1867 left Paris for London.\n\n■ THE RETURN\nBesides France and England, the sultan also visited Belgium, Prussia and Austria, and returned to Istanbul on 7 August 1867.\n\n■ LEFT OPEN\nThe intermediate ports of call of the sea voyage, the cities on the Belgian-Prussian-Austrian leg and the days spent at those stops are not given day by day in the encyclopaedia article consulted or in the two academic articles examined; this detail requires a separate monograph devoted to the journey. NO invented itinerary HAS BEEN WRITTEN here.", "57c3c7a4"]},
"seyahat-abdulaziz-1867-ingiltere":{"baslik": ["Abdülaziz in London (1867) — a nine-day visit to England", "1c3a7cbc"], "metin": ["■ Arrival: Dover\nOn the morning of 12 July 1867 the sultan landed at the port of Dover. The carriages waiting at the pier and a train took the delegation first to the Lord Warden Hotel; the same day they went on to London. The English leg lasted from 12 to 21 July.\n\n■ LONDON\nIn the welcoming ceremony, amid the applause of the crowd, the party proceeded along the Mall to Buckingham Palace, where the sultan was lodged. The next morning, 13 July, he went to Windsor to visit Queen Victoria. On Sunday, 14 July, İsmail Pasha, the Khedive of Egypt, visited the sultan at Buckingham Palace. On Monday, 15 July, a formal reception was held at the palace; a separate ceremony took place at the Guildhall on behalf of the City of London.\n\n■ WOOLWICH AND THE CRYSTAL PALACE\nThe programme was not only protocol: the sultan toured the military establishments at Woolwich and then went on to the Crystal Palace at Sydenham. There, by royal command, a great musical festival was staged in which London's military bands and the palace's own orchestra and choirs took part.\n\n■ THE NAVAL REVIEW AND THE ORDER OF THE GARTER\nOn Wednesday, 17 July, a naval review specially prepared for the sultan was held at Spithead. The sultan watched the display from the royal yacht Osborne. During the same ceremony he was invested with the Order of the Garter, founded by Edward III in 1348. On the way back the party went to London by way of Osborne, Portsmouth and Clarence Yard.\n\n■ WHY IT MATTERS\nThese details are not merely a travel programme. That an Ottoman sultan watched the power of the British navy in person, out on the sea, and received England's oldest order of chivalry was the most visible moment of the British-Ottoman closeness maintained since the Crimean War. Eleven years later that same Britain would take over the administration of Cyprus.", "4c83e3c3"]},
"seyahat-abdulaziz-1867-onemi":{"baslik": ["Abdülaziz's tour — a success or an expensive show?", "208dcde4"], "metin": ["■ The positive reading\nThe visit had direct results. The encyclopaedia article consulted writes that the tour played an important role in securing general peace and that relations with Europe entered a good state. Within the same year Carol's position as prince in Romania was recognised, the withdrawal of Turkish troops from the Serbian fortresses was accepted, the annexation of Crete to Greece was rejected, and a regulation giving the island a special administration was issued. In other words, the crisis in the Balkans was closed by an arrangement without loss of territory.\n\n■ SYMBOLIC WEIGHT\nAbdülaziz is the only sultan in Ottoman history to have travelled to foreign countries and the first caliph to go to the Christian world as a friend. This is the reversal of the protocol logic of the three centuries before him: the sultan was now no longer the one visited but the one visiting.\n\n■ THE NEGATIVE READING\nThe core of the criticism is finance. The journey was made at a time when the state's foreign debt payments amounted to half of its ordinary revenue; ten years later, in 1875, the treasury would be forced to cut the foreign debt payments by half. In the same period, the concession for a 2,000-kilometre railway that would link Istanbul to Paris had been granted to Baron Hirsch — that is, rapprochement with Europe simultaneously meant becoming tied to European capital.\n\n■ WAS THE POLITICAL RESULT LASTING\nIt was not. The closeness the tour produced dissolved with France's defeat by Germany in 1870: the Sublime Porte lost the greatest supporter of its reform programme, and in 1871 Russia declared that it did not recognise the provisions of the Treaty of Paris that bound it. The chain of uprisings that began in Herzegovina four years later brought back the problem the journey was thought to have calmed.\n\n■ AN HONEST VERDICT\nThe journey did its job as a diplomatic move; the reason it could not be lasting was not the tour itself but the change in the European balance in 1870. As for the claim that its expense ruined the state, it cannot be measured on its own: the main item in the financial collapse of the period was not the journey but the servicing of the foreign debt that had accumulated since 1854.", "4149d36d"], "kisa": ["Was the journey a success or an expensive show? Both assessments can be drawn from within the same year — and both are incomplete.", "09bb4b06"]},
"tabi-devlet-vassallik":{"sebep.b": ["The fact that the direct annexation of Serbia in 1439 and its conversion into a sanjak made distant lands beyond the Danube untenable in terms of the cost of defense and administration", "c33d4b54"], "sonuc.b": ["The recognition of Wallachia, in the Treaty of Edirne-Szeged, in the status of a tributary (vassal) state governed by its own voivode — the model for Wallachia, Moldavia, Transylvania and the Crimea over the following three centuries", "d4f4d91a"], "bag": ["According to the TDV article on Wallachia (Eflak), this treaty established 'a dual sovereignty': the voivode paid tribute and remained free in internal affairs, and the land was not divided into timars — the exact opposite of the solution applied to Serbia, which in the same period was directly annexed and made into a province.", "28922bc8"], "metin": ["A tributary (vassal) state was the form of government in which the Ottomans, without annexing the territory they had conquered, left the local dynasty at its head in return for tribute and troops. Wallachia, Moldavia, Transylvania, the Crimean Khanate and the Sharifate of the Hejaz were different forms of this model; what they had in common was that the land was not divided into timars (military fiefs) and that local administration and religion continued. Because it secured the same control more cheaply than direct provincial rule, it was preferred especially in frontier regions that were expensive to defend.", "3eed6869"]},
"tanzimat-fermani-maddeleri-ve-etkileri-1839":{"ad": ["The Tanzimat Edict: what did it say, why was it issued, what did it change?", "2f003bf5"], "metin": ["The reading of the edict. The Foreign Minister Mustafa Reşid Pasha read out the Tanzimat Edict in Gülhane square on 3 November 1839; among the listeners were the ministers, high officials, the ulema, the Greek and Armenian patriarchs, the chief rabbi, representatives of the tradesmen and foreign ambassadors, and Sultan Abdülmecid watched the ceremony from the Gülhane Pavilion. After the reading cannon were fired and animals were sacrificed. Abdülmecid swore in the Chamber of the Noble Mantle (Hırka-i Şerif) that he would abide by what was proclaimed in the edict; the ministers, officials and ulema were also required to take an oath.\n\nITS PROVISIONS (summary of the edict). The edict begins by saying that because the precepts of the Qur'an and the sharia had been observed since the foundation of the state, it had been strong and its people prosperous, but that because they had not been respected for 150 years, weakness and poverty had come; it states that if the necessary measures are taken, the state will regain its former strength within five to ten years. The bases of the new laws to be prepared are these: security of life and the protection of property, honour and reputation; the regulation of taxes (to be taken from everyone in proportion to his means; the drawbacks of tax-farming (iltizam) are mentioned and it is indicated that it will be abolished); military recruitment and the fixing of the term of service (four or five years is proposed); no one to be sentenced to death without trial; everyone to be able to dispose of his property as he wishes; these rights to be the same for all subjects, Muslim and non-Muslim; an increase in the number of members of the Supreme Council (Meclis-i Vâlâ); the discussion of military regulations in the Military Council (Dâr-ı Şûrâ-yı Askerî); an effective law to prevent bribery; the announcement of the edict to public opinion at home and abroad.\n\nCAUSES. According to TDV the roots of the Tanzimat can be traced back to 1830: after abolishing the Janissary Corps, Mahmud II reorganised the central administration, set up the ministries, founded the Meclis-i Vâlâ on 24 March 1838 and gave the name 'Tanzîmât-ı Hayriyye' to the reform he planned. After Baltalimanı customs revenues had fallen and the number of foreign merchants and the burden of lawsuits had risen; the Egyptian question was straining the treasury; on 24 June 1839 the Ottoman army suffered a heavy defeat at the hands of the Egyptian forces at Nizip, and on 1 July Mahmud II died. TDV objects to the view that the text of the edict was the work of Mustafa Reşid Pasha alone: principles such as security, the avoidance of bribery and the refusal of gifts were already present in the accession rescript that Abdülmecid issued on 17 July 1839; before Reşid Pasha returned to Istanbul, the same principles had been accepted at an assembly of 38 persons (half of the seals belonging to ulema) that met under the presidency of the Grand Vizier Koca Hüsrev Pasha. TDV's conclusion: the edict was the product of the internal dynamics of the Ottoman state more than of outside influences. Nevertheless it reads the edict as the first step towards the idea of an Ottoman nation: with the aim of preventing the non-Muslims, who along with the Greek and Serbian revolts had been taking a nationalist stance, from breaking away from the empire, the equality of Muslims and non-Muslims was written down to this extent for the first time.\n\nITS SIGNIFICANCE and the reactions. The edict may be counted within the tradition of the justice decree (adaletname), but in limiting the authority of the sultan and the senior administrators, in its principle of equality and in binding itself by oath, it carried innovations that shook the tradition to its roots. Abroad, British and French public opinion viewed it favourably, Austria and Russia unfavourably: Metternich criticised the limitation of powers on the ground that it would lead to similar demands in his own country, and Russia opposed it out of concern that it would give the state strength and increase British and French influence. The reaction at home was different: every group read the edict from its own point of view; Muslims disliked the new rights granted to non-Muslims, while non-Muslims conceived great expectations; ulema, provincial notables (ayan) and some governors whose interests were damaged stirred up the people, saying that the sharia was being violated and that Muslims were being brought down to the level of the 'infidels'. Stories circulating among the people of the kind 'the infidel will no longer be called infidel' were not found in TDV; only this objection itself is sourced. Because the government feared that the edict would be misinterpreted and that a revolt would break out, it applied the reform first in places near the centre (Edirne, Bursa, İzmir, Ankara, Aydın, Konya, Sivas); Trebizond was postponed because of the reaction; by a decree of 6 December 1839 its application was also requested from Egypt, and Mehmed Ali Pasha replied that he was already applying the principles.\n\nITS EFFECTS. Taxation and finance: tax-farming was abolished and tax collection was given to revenue collectors (muhassıl-ı emvâl) sent from the centre, and the first paper money was issued in 1840; but the system was abolished at the beginning of 1842 and tax-farming was restored, and TDV says that the financial reforms of the Tanzimat failed. Military service: nothing was done for three years; on 6 September 1843 the drawing of lots was introduced, and from March 1844 a term of actual service of five years, a reserve (redif) service of seven years and a system of five armies. Law: on 3 May 1840 a penal code of 41 articles (Europeans reacted because the death penalty for apostates remained); the Code of Commerce (Kānunnâme-i Ticâret) adapted from the French Commercial Code; the Supreme Council of the Tanzimat (1854); the Land Code (under the chairmanship of Ahmed Cevdet Pasha). Administration: the Meclis-i Vâlâ, the ministries, the revenue collectors' councils (later provincial councils), the Marshalcy of Police (1845), the Municipal Prefecture (1855). Communications and science and technology: the Ministry of Posts in 1840; the telegraph line (Istanbul-Edirne-Shumen (Şumnu) in 1855); the Academy of Learning (Encümen-i Dâniş, 1851), the Ottoman Scientific Society and the Journal of Sciences (Mecmûa-i Fünûn, 1861); the first private Turkish newspaper, Tercümân-ı Ahvâl (1860). Education: the Ministry of Public Education (14 March 1857); the madrasas were left untouched, new schools were opened alongside them, and a dual structure arose. Social life: Abdülmecid's provincial tour in 1846, the Şirket-i Hayriyye steamship company (end of 1850) and the first railways (Alexandria-Cairo, 1856); the sultan's meeting directly with foreign envoys. Literature: the TDV article on the Tanzimat does not describe literary renewal separately (it merely mentions Ziyâ Bey and Nâmık Kemal among the members of the commission for the Ottoman Constitution (Kānûn-ı Esâsî)); the article on Tanzimat literature, for its part, speaks of 'the process of renewal that began some twenty years after the proclamation of the Tanzimat' and refers the reader to the article on Westernisation. For the effects on literature, no detailed basis from TDV was found for this card.\n\nASSESSMENT. According to TDV, the Tanzimat reforms responded to practical needs rather than being planned and programmed; qualified officials, sound finances and time were lacking; education was taken in hand late. The ambassadors of the great powers constantly interfered in internal affairs; Reşid Pasha himself criticised the Reform Edict of 1856 (proclaimed during the grand vizierate of Âli Pasha) as a door opened to foreign intervention, and TDV describes this as 'a tragic contradiction'.", "2972a023"], "kisa": ["The edict was a text of guarantees in which even the sultan declared on oath that he would bind himself by the laws; but its application turned into a struggle that lasted years.", "19f55ede"], "not": ["The end of the period is disputed: TDV lists the dates 1871 (the death of Âli Pasha), 1877, 1878 (the closure of the Chamber of Deputies) and 1881 (the Ottoman Public Debt Administration); 1878 is generally accepted.", "d25fc62c"], "bag": ["On the Baltalimanı commercial convention that preceded the edict see the card 'balta-limani-getirdikleri-goturdukleri-1838'; on the defeat at Nizip and the Egyptian question see the Nizip cards.", "68a00e36"]},
"tartisma-ahmed1-kardes-katli-terki":{"oneri": ["The sources put forward four separate explanations: the lack of an heir, public reaction, centres of power in the palace…", "52522354"], "ad": ["Why did Ahmed I not have his brother Mustafa killed — and was fratricide really ‘abolished’?", "7a6e0c5c"], "metin": ["View 1 — There was no heir, and killing him would have endangered the dynasty. TDV's articles 'Mustafa I' and 'Şehzade' state that Ahmed came to the throne at the age of fourteen, that he did not yet have a son and that Mustafa was the only other surviving male member of the dynasty: for a young sultan who might or might not be able to leave an heir, killing his only brother would have meant risking the extinction of the line. According to this explanation, the decision was not a change of principle but a necessity. Indeed, after his sons were born, Mustafa's life was in danger again; the report of the Venetian ambassador Contarini of 1612 writes that Ahmed several times set about having him killed and then gave it up (the account of a foreign observer, relayed by TDV). View 2 — The reaction of 1595. The same two articles count as a second factor the hatred that Mehmed III's killing of his nineteen brothers aroused among the people and among administrators. View 3 — The actors in the palace. TDV's article 'Kösem Sultan' writes that there is no definite information about Kösem's role in this process, but that she had a relative share in bringing the practice to an end, even if it was in order to protect her own sons. The same articles also mention the role of the Shaykh al-Islam Hocazâde Esad Efendi and the deputy grand vizier (kaymakam) Sofu Mehmed Pasha in Mustafa's elevation to the throne in 1617, the promptings of the chief black eunuch (kızlarağası) Mustafa Agha according to the historian of the period Peçuylu, and the report that Kösem belonged to this group. View 4 — A sultan's decision, or a practice that took hold later? Here TDV's own articles place the emphasis differently. The article 'Ahmed I' says that the principle of seniority (ekberiyet) was adopted from this sultan onwards. The article 'Şehzade', however, writes that the three-century tradition came to an end in 1617, when Mustafa came to the throne on Ahmed's death, and that life in the cage (kafes) began from that year. The article 'Mustafa I' also reports that the decision of 1617 was taken by going ‘outside the prevailing order of succession’, that it was criticised by contemporaries and that Osman II, in a letter to the King of England, maintained that succession passed from father to son. Kayapınar (Belleten, 2004), reviewing Vatin and Veinstein's work ‘Le Sérail ébranlé’ (2003), reports that the authors see the confinement of the princes to the cage and the choice of 1617 as a decision of the religious and political notables of the state, and that fratricide, though it very probably did not disappear altogether because of the reactions, became rarer. Hitzel (BCAI, 2005), reviewing the same book, states that the authors treat the practice as one that preserved order, although it was never a recognised rule. THE LIMIT OF THE WORD ‘ABOLISHED’: after 1603 too, brothers or close relatives were killed — Osman II had his brother Mehmed killed (1621), and Murad IV his brothers Bayezid, Süleyman and Kasım (1635, 1638); Mustafa IV ordered the killing of his cousin Selim III, and Mahmud II in turn had his brother Mustafa IV strangled (1808). In other words, what changed in 1603 was not that the provision was deleted from the law code, but that the open contest for the throne among princes sent out to sanjaks gave way to princes waiting under surveillance in the palace.", "4492b8b0"], "kisa": ["The sources put forward four separate explanations: the lack of an heir, public reaction, centres of power in the palace and a practice of succession that took hold later. The word ‘abolished’ is itself disputed: the practice continued at intervals until 1808.", "5cc8d789"], "not": ["This card does not choose any view. The four explanations do not exclude one another; most of the sources count more than one of them together. Further reading: Leslie P. Peirce, The Imperial Harem (Oxford University Press, 1993) · Baki Tezcan, The Second Ottoman Empire (Cambridge University Press, 2010) · Nicolas Vatin – Gilles Veinstein, Le Sérail ébranlé (Fayard, 2003). For the scene of the event, see the card ‘An illness, a storm’ in the Magazine section; for a comparison of the practice with other dynasties, see the Curiosities section.", "8cb0ce42"]},
"tartisma-akdeniz-vehran-onemi":{"baslik": ["Oran (Vehrân): A Port Taken and Lost for Two Centuries — Why Was It Indispensable to the Regency of Algiers?", "b7f260c9"], "metin": ["① Geography itself defended the city. Oran was founded in 290 (903) by members of the Maghrawa tribe; its name means 'two lions' or 'lions' den' in the Tuareg language. The old city was built on both banks of the Ra's al-Ayn valley, and the 375-meter Aïdour hill to its west protected the harbor from westerly winds — in the words of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, 'its geographical structure has given the city a position that is sheltered against attacks from outside and strategic'. At a point very close to both Morocco and Spain, it was situated so as to control the sea route in the western Mediterranean. ② It was already a base in the Middle Ages. It suffered destruction repeatedly in the struggle between the Fatimids and the Andalusian Umayyads (297/910, 342/954), and passed successively into the hands of the Banu Ifran, the Zirids, the Almoravids (473/1080, Yusuf ibn Tashfin) and the Almohads; the Almohad ruler had a fleet built here and set out from here on his campaigns to take all of the Maghreb and al-Andalus. Under the Abd al-Wadids (Zayyanids) the city became the port of Tlemcen, the great trading center of the interior; for this reason the Marinids, who besieged Tlemcen in the 14th century, landed troops on the coast of Oran and had a fortress built here. When Granada fell to the Spaniards in 1492, Muslims and Jews from al-Andalus took refuge here and enlarged the city further. ③ 1509: the Spanish landing. On 17 May 1509 the Kingdom of Castile occupied Oran, killed most of its people and took the rest captive; no Muslims or Jews remained in the city. The Spaniards enlarged the walls and fortresses and turned it into a garrison town. ④ Five Ottoman attempts, four of them unsuccessful. Oruç Reis, master of Algiers, blockaded the city in 1516; but because the local rulers were uneasy at the Ottoman presence and cooperated with Spain, Oruç Reis and his brother İshak Reis were killed in 1518. Barbaros Hayreddin Pasha's attempts at recovery were not enough to retake the city either. In 1556, under Salih Pasha, beylerbey (governor-general) of Algiers, Piyale Pasha's expedition came to nothing because of the beylerbey's death. In 1563 Hasan Pasha besieged the city again but was forced to withdraw when help arrived from Spain. In the period up to 1708 the Spaniards had to contend with famine and plague as well as Berber attacks. ⑤ 1708: the first definitive Ottoman conquest — and its loss in 1732. After a long siege, the forces of Mustafa bin Yusuf Bey, who rebuilt Mascara, the center of the Western Beylik of Algiers, and who was popularly known as 'Bouchelaghem' (the big-mustached), received the surrender of Oran in 1708 in the name of the Ottomans; the news aroused great joy in Istanbul, and the bey moved his seat to the city. During the twenty-four years of Ottoman rule maritime trade revived. But in 1732 the city was retaken by Spain; with Spanish rule trade died down again and the population declined. ⑥ The earthquake of 1790 and 12 September 1792: the definitive conquest. When the great earthquake of 1790 devastated the town, the people founded a new settlement on the eastern side of the valley. On 12 September 1792 the Ottoman governor in Mascara, Kuloğlu Mehmed bin Osman, succeeded in finally retaking Oran from the Spaniards; this second conquest too was greeted with great satisfaction in Istanbul, and the people gave Mehmed Bey the title 'Mehmed (Muhammad) el-Kebir' (the Great). In total Spain held the city for 199 (1509-1708) + 60 (1732-1792) = 259 years; the 24-year Ottoman period in between falls outside this continuity. ⑦ Administrative and political importance: it became the capital of a beylik. Mehmed Bey made Oran the new center of the Western Beylik (one of the three sanjaks of the Regency of Algiers, the others being the Eastern/Constantine and Titteri/Médéa) — the TDV article on Algeria (Cezayir) records this as 'the West (center Mascara, Oran after 1792)'; moving the seat of the beylik from Mascara to Oran made the city no longer merely a port but an administrative capital. Mehmed Bey, who had the city rebuilt, settled the Muslim and Jewish population of the surrounding area here, restored to their original use the mosques that had been converted into churches, and built the mosques of Mustafa Pasha and of Mehmed Bey (Jami al-Basha), as well as a palace. ⑧ Economic importance: the gateway to Tlemcen, then the center of European trade. In the Ottoman period the city returned to its position as 'the port of Tlemcen', and maritime trade revived. After the French occupation (4 January 1831) it changed identity: while the Muslim population migrated to the interior, tens of thousands of French and other Europeans settled in the city, and Oran became the most important center of trade with Europe in Algeria. Today, too, its port competes with that of the capital, Algiers; according to 2009 data the population of the city is 852,000, and that of the province of the same name is 1,453,158.", "29bbef91"], "kisa": ["The city changed hands three times from 1509 to 1792 — behind Spain's persistence, amounting to 259 years in total, and the five separate campaigns the Ottomans undertook to win it back lay the same reason: the western gateway of the Maghreb.", "2f34ebac"], "bag": ["Its importance: because of its position, Oran was a gateway that was both easy to defend and valuable to seize — a port close to Morocco and Spain at the same time, controlling the sea route of the western Mediterranean and the trade of Tlemcen. That is why Spain held it at great expense for two and a half centuries, and the Ottomans tried to retake it in five separate campaigns. The definitive conquest of 1792 raised the city from a mere port to the capital of a beylik; only then was the coastline of the Regency of Algiers completed from end to end — and thirty-eight years later it became one of the first landing points of the French.", "aa3d7b1c"]},
"tartisma-bolge0073-1821-osmanli-ordusu-nerede":{"baslik": ["Where was the Ottoman army while the rebels were taking the towns one by one? The four simultaneous fronts of 1821–1822", "088eff3b"], "metin": ["① The measurable part of the question: how many fronts were there at the same time? Nauplion (Anabolu) fell on 12 December 1822. In that month the military situation of the Ottoman State was as follows. ② The eastern front — Erzurum under siege. According to the TDV article on Iran, using as a pretext the search by Ottoman officials of the caravan that included the shah's wife, who was going on pilgrimage, the Crown Prince Abbas Mirza crossed the border with a large military force in the autumn of 1821, seized the provinces of Kars and Bayazıt and laid siege to Erzurum. ③ The Iraqi front — Baghdad was occupied. According to the same source, Iranian troops simultaneously launched a large-scale attack on Northern Iraq and the province of Baghdad; Sulaymaniyah and Baghdad were occupied and Kirkuk was besieged. What ended the war was not an Ottoman victory: Ottoman resistance and an epidemic that engulfed Azerbaijan forced the Iranians to withdraw; the peace treaty concluded at Erzurum in 1823 stipulated that the Iranian forces would withdraw within sixty days. That is, in the two most critical years of the Greek revolt, the eastern half of the empire was a war zone. ④ The Albanian front — and the detail here is absolutely crucial. Mahmud II dismissed Ali Pasha of Tepelena, the governor of Ioannina, who was behaving semi-independently (March 1820), and sent forces against him. The commander entrusted with this task was Hurşid Ahmed Pasha — and, as the TDV records, Hurşid Pasha was at the same time the man who had been appointed governor of the Morea. That is, when the revolt broke out, the man who should have been governing the Morea was besieging a fortress at Ioannina. Ali Pasha's resistance at Ioannina lasted nearly two years, and in the words of the TDV, during this resistance the Greek revolt became generally widespread. Ali Pasha was killed on 14 February 1822, and his head was brought to Istanbul on 24 February. ⑤ The force he faced was no small one. Ali Pasha had an army of 30,000–40,000 men, 7,000 of them standing troops, and a small navy; the population under his rule is estimated at 1.5–2 million, and his annual revenue amounted to 4–5 million kuruş (TDV). This was not a hunt for bandits but a civil war. The TDV article on Greece states the outcome plainly: the rebellion of Ali Pasha, suppressed in 1822, made the Greek uprising easier and contributed to its success. ⑥ The army itself — it was still the Janissary corps. The Janissary corps stood until 1826, and according to the TDV article on the Janissaries, the series of wars that began in 1768 \"had laid bare that the Janissary army no longer had any military value\". The corps had become an obstacle standing in the way of renewal. The Nizam-i Cedid army founded by Selim III had been disbanded in 1807, and as the TDV records, such implacable enmity had developed between the two systems that even in times of need it had never been possible to use the two forces together. In 1821 the Ottoman State did not have an army trained on the European model. ⑦ And what about Arabia? The Wahhabi front was closed by this date, so it is not included in the list: Diriyah had been besieged on 6 April 1818 and was captured on 9 September 1818 by Egyptian forces under the command of İbrahim Pasha (TDV). But that campaign had been carried out not by the Ottoman army but by the Egyptian army — and this is the clue that leads to the real answer to the question. ⑧ The solution again came from Egypt. The island of Crete was given to Mehmed Ali Pasha so that he would suppress the revolts there, and Egyptian forces landed on Crete in June 1822. As the revolt continued in the Morea, in 1824 the sultan asked Mehmed Ali for help; the Egyptian fleet under the command of İbrahim Pasha landed in the Morea in 1825, made Modon its headquarters and harshly suppressed the revolt (TDV, Greece). The state had become dependent on the army of its own province. ⑨ The short answer: was it weak? According to the sources, the issue was not sheer strength but simultaneity and STRUCTURE. In 1821–1822 the Ottoman State was fighting a state in the east, fighting one of its own governors in the west, grappling with a revolt in the south, and doing all this with a corps which, by its own admission, no longer had any military value. The piling up of these three explains why the towns fell \"one by one\". And the fact that the sultan dared to destroy this corps in 1826, precisely at a time when the external threat was at its height, although it seemed ill-timed, is, in the words of the TDV, an indication of how well he followed developments.", "22e241d0"], "kisa": ["When Nauplion fell, the Ottoman State was occupied in four places at once: Erzurum was under Iranian siege, Baghdad had been occupied, the governor of the Morea was besieging a rebel pasha at Ioannina, and the army itself was still the Janissary corps that was to be abolished in 1826.", "b892b5e7"], "bag": ["Significance: The loss of Nauplion was not merely the loss of a fortress; it was the result of a year in which the commander who should have been governing the Morea was tied down at Ioannina, and the eastern army at Erzurum and Baghdad. Shortly afterwards the town was to become the first capital of the new Greek state.", "30043a3c"]},
"tartisma-bolge0073-cezayir-ordusu-1830":{"baslik": ["Did Algiers have no army? Two separate forces and two separate collapses in 1830", "21831f43"], "metin": ["① The first question: what military force of its own did the Regency of Algiers have? It had one, and for a long time it was formidable. Algiers was part of the system of the western garrison regencies (Garp Ocakları) in North Africa, and this garrison corps (ocak) formed a military aristocracy. The core of the military class consisted of Janissaries sent from Istanbul, headed by an agha. According to estimates, Turkish military strength was around 15-20,000 in Algiers, 6-8,000 in Tunis and 6,000 in Tripoli; in 1634 there were some 20,000 Janissaries in Algiers alone. Those enrolled in the corps received pay and were subject only to their own leaders, and any soldier with ability could rise to the highest office. ② The second pillar: the fleet. One of the most important strengths of the western regencies was their powerful fleets, and it was Algiers that possessed the largest. The seamen lived on the coast, did not mix with the Janissaries and pursued their profession as a separate corps. This naval power was taken so seriously that France, although it had a trade treaty with the Ottoman state, had been obliged to conclude a separate treaty with the dey of Algiers at Marseilles on 21 March 1629; seven years later England and the Netherlands did the same. ③ The third pillar: the tribes — and this pillar was weak from the outset. The corps' territory was extensive, and it was difficult to maintain order away from the coast. The Turks solved this by exempting certain privileged tribes, called the Makhzan, from tax and using them to keep the tax-paying tribes in obedience; they also took advantage of rivalries among the tribes. Since it was hard to collect taxes from nomads, military units were sent out every spring and autumn to collect them — these expeditions were called the \"mahalle\" or \"mahalle-i mansure\". In other words, merely to collect taxes in its own interior, the corps had to mount a campaign twice every year. ④ So why could it not hold out? The decline did not begin in 1830. Toward the eighteenth century, as the French and British navies grew stronger and brought the Mediterranean under their control, the maritime activity of the Algerians was crippled; as Algiers lost its sources of income and its importance, its population began to fall. In the second half of the century the fleet shrank and the number of Janissaries fell from around 20,000 to as low as 5,000. As revenue fell, taxes were raised, and as taxes rose, revolts broke out. The administration too was unstable: of the twenty-eight deys who came to power after 1671, half were killed by the populace and the soldiery. ⑤ And the measure of the force that faced it. On 16 June 1827 France declared war on Algiers and blockaded its coasts; the blockade lasted three years. On 14 June 1830 it sent a great fleet and a fresh force of 37,000 men under the command of General Bourmont. That is, a corps reduced to 5,000 men was confronted by an entirely fresh army seven times its size. The city of Algiers was occupied on 5 July 1830. ⑥ The second question: why did Istanbul not help? On this point the source has no hesitation. When in August 1827 France sent a note to the Sublime Porte asking for permission to punish the dey, the Porte, preoccupied with the Greek revolt, sent Tahir Pasha as mediator, also at Britain's wish; the French seized the pasha's ship and took him to Toulon. The verdict of the TDV is this: the Sublime Porte thought that Algiers was strong enough to fight the French on its own and did not wish to become actively involved in the war — in fact, it had no means of sending forces to Algiers either. ⑦ The calendar of that impossibility. In the months when Algiers fell, the Ottoman state had neither a fleet nor a settled army, and this is not an interpretation but the sequence of dates: the Janissary corps had been abolished on 15 June 1826, and the Asakir-i Mansure-i Muhammediyye founded in its place was only four years old. On 20 October 1827 the combined British, French and Russian fleet burned the Ottoman fleet at Navarino, and France landed troops in the Morea. The following year the Ottoman-Russian war began, and it ended on 14 September 1829 with the Treaty of Adrianople (Edirne). The independence of Greece was accepted in February 1830. When the French army landed at Sidi Ferruch (14 June 1830), it had been three years since the empire lost its fleet and four since it disbanded its army, and it had just emerged defeated from a war. ⑧ Resistance did not end; there was simply no centre. After Hüseyin Pasha, the dey, surrendered and was sent into exile on 9 August 1830, the tribes of the west gathered around Emir Abd al-Qadir and proclaimed him sultan in 1832; in the east Ahmed Bey, the emir of Constantine — himself a kuloğlu (son of a Turkish soldier and a local woman) — carried on the struggle until 1837. It took the French until 1847 to seize the whole country. In 1830 the unmarried Janissaries were sent by ship to Anatolia, the islands, Izmir and Crete. The Ottoman government contented itself with protesting against the occupation, and in 1847, recognising the French occupation, it declared that its rights over Algeria had ended. ⑨ The short answer. Algiers had an army, but it was not the army of half a century earlier: a naval power that drew its income from corsairing, whose number of soldiers fell to a quarter once that income was cut off. As for the Ottoman state, it truly had no power to prevent this, and the source says so openly. The two collapses took place separately; 1830 is only the moment at which both fell in the same year.", "e8ff764a"], "kisa": ["The question is really two questions, and their answers differ. The corps had an army, but within half a century it had shrunk to a quarter; Istanbul, for its part, had no strength at all to send help — and the source says so in its own words.", "4bd6e489"], "bag": ["Significance: 9 August 1830 is the day on which a colour three hundred years old was erased from the map. To understand why that colour could not be defended, one must look not at Algiers but at the Morea, Navarino and Edirne at the same moment — the western end of the empire broke away in the four years when the centre was at its most defenceless.", "24ddf1da"]},
"tartisma-bolge0073-hurmuz-bogazi-el-degistirme":{"baslik": ["Whose was the Strait of Hormuz? Six changes of hands in five hundred years", "07d47cc0"], "metin": ["① First the city moved (1300). Hormuz, which gave the strait its name, was originally a port on the mainland. During the Mongol raids, the ruler Qutb al-Din Tahamtan moved the population to the island of Jarun north of the strait, and this place was called New Hormuz. The ruins of Old Hormuz are today 80 km from Minab. The new port soon became the keystone of the maritime trade route between the Persian Gulf and India, and kept this position until the 17th century (TDV). Ibn Battuta describes it as a large, beautiful and busy trading city; the Russian traveller Afanasy Nikitin, who saw the island in 1472, says that people and goods from every part of the world were to be found there. ② Portugal (1507–1622). Afonso de Albuquerque besieged the island in 1507 but turned back without taking it. When he besieged it again seven years later, taking advantage also of the fact that Shah Ismail, the sultan's overlord, was in those years engaged in a struggle with the Ottoman State, he seized the island and made the sultan, who was a vassal of Iran, subject to Portugal (1514). The Portuguese built a strong fortress surrounded by a moat at a commanding spot on the island and brought the whole strait, the entrance to the Gulf and the Iranian coast opposite under their control; they stopped ships bound for the Hejaz and tormented would-be pilgrims (TDV). But their influence was limited: they could not have much effect on the transit trade going from the coast into the interior of Iran, and had to content themselves with the trade of the island's port. ③ The Ottoman attempts — and neither succeeded. A fleet under the command of Piri Reis besieged the island in 1552 but could not take it. Two years later Seydi Ali Reis, while taking the Ottoman fleet at Basra to Suez, encountered Portuguese ships near Hormuz and defeated them. Another campaign was planned in 1570 but could not be carried out (TDV). The Ottoman State established itself in the Gulf by way of Basra and al-Ahsa, but it never got its hands on the key to the strait. ④ Safavid Iran (1622) — and the port was moved across the water. Hormuz was recovered in the time of Shah Abbas I, 108 years after the Portuguese occupation (23 April 1622); the operation was carried out with the support of the fleet of the English East India Company. By the shah's order the port installations on the island were demolished, Gombroon on the opposite shore was built up with their stones, and all commercial activity was shifted to this new town, named Bandar Abbas after the shah; the military installations were left untouched (TDV). From this date Hormuz declined rapidly and turned into today's small fishing town. The centre of gravity of the strait passed from the island to the mainland and never returned. ⑤ Ya'rubi Oman (from the 1620s). The expulsion of the Portuguese from Muscat and its environs took place in the time of Imam Nasir b. Murshid of the Omani Ya'rubi dynasty (1620); for some time longer the Portuguese continued to collect taxes from Hormuz, Julfar and Qalhat. In 1633 Nasir b. Murshid captured Julfar (present-day Ras al-Khaimah) (TDV, Ras al-Khaimah). The naval power of the Ya'rubis extended as far as East Africa: the colonies of Zanzibar and Mombasa attained autonomous administration in this period. ⑥ The Qawasim (18th century). The Qawasim tribe, which had played a major role in freeing the region from the Portuguese, settled on the Julfar coast. When the Ya'rubis went into decline, the tribal sheikh Rahma al-Matari began to act freely and declared his independence at Ras al-Khaimah, which he had founded beside old Julfar. Matari, who built up a strong navy and land army at Ras al-Khaimah and Sharjah, consolidated his control over the traffic of the Strait of Hormuz after Nader Shah was killed (1747) and Iran lost its influence in the Gulf; in the words of the TDV, this situation continued for about a century. At the beginning of the second half of the 18th century, some of the people of Hormuz, troubled by the Qawasim, migrated to Sharjah. ⑦ Busaidi Oman (after 1741) and the leasing of the island. After the death of Sayf b. Sultan II (1743), the period of the Al Bu Said dynasty began with the seizure of power by Imam Ahmad b. Said. In 1762 Ras al-Khaimah, the centre of the Qawasim, agreed by a treaty to submit to Imam Ahmad, and the two sides tried together to counter the Iranian threat (TDV, Oman). Omani interest in Hormuz began with Imam Ahmad; in 1793 his son Sultan b. Ahmad, by fighting, seized a 150-kilometre strip of the Iranian coast, including the islands of Qeshm and Hormuz. Under the treaty made with Iran, the island was leased to the Sultanate of Muscat and Oman from 1798 (TDV, Hormuz). ⑧ Britain (1798–1820) — not the one who held the lock, but the one who decided about the lock. Britain opened an agency in Muscat (18 January 1798), and Omani–British relations began with this agreement. From the first years of the 19th century it signed non-aggression treaties with the Arab emirates, which it brought to consent by using force through the East India Company; in 1819, in an attack, it seized Ras al-Khaimah, held it under occupation for a short time and destroyed the ships in the harbour. Thereupon Emir Sultan b. Saqr, who had withdrawn to Sharjah, was also obliged to sign a non-aggression treaty (1820, TDV Ras al-Khaimah). Both shores of the strait were now within the British maritime order. ⑨ Iran takes it back (1868). In 1855 the lease of Hormuz was taken up again and its term limited to twenty years. In 1868 Shah Nasir al-Din, who had been looking for an opportunity to break the treaty, withdrew the powers and privileges granted by earlier shahs concerning Bandar Abbas and Hormuz; Iranian rule was re-established on the island in place of the Sultanate of Oman (TDV). ⑩ And the last Ottoman claim. At the beginning of the 20th century the Ottoman State was asserting that Hormuz was among its own territories and was preventing Iran's attempt to build a quarantine station there (TDV). This was the very last Ottoman claim over a strait that it had never been able to seize in four hundred years. ⑪ A summary of five hundred years. The Strait of Hormuz changed hands not through the holding of a small island or port on its shore, but according to whose ships the trade passing over that water depended on. Portugal held it with a fortress; Iran took it by pulling down the fortress and moving the port to the opposite shore; Oman and the Qawasim held it with their fleets; and Britain bound them all by treaty without governing any of them.", "a0f318a2"], "kisa": ["The key to the strait never stayed in the same hands for long: the Kingdom of Hormuz, Portugal, Safavid Iran, Ya'rubi and Busaidi Oman, the Qawasim and finally Britain. Each change of hands came together with a change in the direction of a trade route.", "613f8e60"], "bag": ["Significance: This strait, the only outlet of the Persian Gulf, is the narrowest passage in the atlas where the colours of five different states appear in turn. The Ottoman State entered the Gulf from Basra but could never hold the strait; this is also the geographical logic of today's disputes over Hormuz.", "fbd322af"]},
"tartisma-bolge0073-misir-osmanli-modernlesme":{"baslik": ["What did Egypt do that the Ottomans could not? The modernisation of a province and that of an empire", "3ded2070"], "metin": ["① First let us correct the wrong question. It is not that the Ottoman State \"could not\" modernise, but that it was able to do so only late. According to the TDV article on Mahmud II, the abolition of the Janissary corps (1826) opened the way for the reforms of the final period of the sultan's reign, which was to last thirteen years, and in this new period the old order and its institutions were granted no right to survive whatsoever. The real question is this: why was Mehmed Ali of Kavala able to do this fifteen years earlier? ② The elimination of the rival caste: 1811 and 1826. Mehmed Ali continued the struggle against the Mamluk beys, whom he saw as the greatest obstacle to his rule; in 1808–1810 he neutralised the beys in Upper Egypt, and on 1 March 1811, at a great banquet he gave in honour of his son Tosun, he had all but a few of the Mamluk beys killed (TDV). Mahmud II was able to do the same only on 15 June 1826, and before that he had to spend years in preparation: he made careful appointments to the leading posts of the army, created a new drilled unit under the name of the Eşkinci corps (29 May 1826), and by announcing that these soldiers would begin drill in European-style uniforms on 11 June, he all but drove the Janissaries to revolt; in the words of the TDV, there is no doubt that the last Janissary revolt was not accidental and that it was the sultan's handiwork. ③ The composition of the resistance was different. The TDV describes the first period of Mahmud II as \"the domination of the anti-reform front, which saw its interest in the preservation of the traditional order, was represented chiefly by the military and learned (ilmiye) classes, and was supported by the ayan in Anatolia and Rumelia\". In other words, facing the sultan was a three-legged alliance: the Janissary corps, the ulema and the provincial notables. Nor was the Janissary body simply an army: according to a study based on the Surety Registers of 1791–1793, about 40 per cent of the 1,110 owners of businesses and shops in Istanbul had Janissary connections; the funds of the ortas (Janissary companies) lent money at interest to small tradesmen, and the transport and construction sectors were largely controlled by Janissaries (TDV, Janissaries). To disband an army meant at the same time to dismantle the credit and labour market of the capital. ④ Mehmed Ali also broke the ulema early. The pasha, who increased his control over land through the centralised economic system applied in Egypt, largely broke the power of the ulema, whom he saw as a danger to his rule, by subjecting endowment (waqf) lands to taxation (TDV). What is interesting is that Mahmud II used the same instrument in 1826: an attempt was made to finance the new army through the rational use of endowment wealth, and for this purpose the Ministry of Imperial Endowments was established and all endowment wealth was gathered under its administration. The same remedy, fifteen years apart. ⑤ Scale: a province and an empire. By 1821 Mehmed Ali had registered and taxed all the cultivable land of Lower and Upper Egypt; he abolished the tax-farming (iltizam) system and nationalised the endowment lands, and almost all the land of Egypt became state property (TDV). The Nile valley is a country stretching along a single river, which it is possible to survey and control. To carry out the same operation in an empire stretching from Rumelia to Baghdad, with a separate dynasty of ayan in each of its regions, was an entirely different task. The TDV sums this up as follows: although the case of Mehmed Ali remains the single exception where he failed, Mahmud II succeeded in making the weight of central authority felt over the Ottoman lands to a degree that had not been possible in earlier periods. ⑥ Finance: this is where the real difference lies. Mehmed Ali's system earned the state money — but by taking it from the people. Through the monopoly system, crops were bought very cheaply, and together with heavy taxes this order was \"enriching the administration while impoverishing the people\"; during the pasha's rule the cultivable land doubled, the variety of cotton was improved, and Egypt became one of the world's leading cotton producers (TDV). On the Ottoman side the picture is the reverse: according to the TDV, weakness in the financial sphere was the weakest side of the reign of Mahmud II. The war indemnity paid to Russia was 400 million kuruş — far above the state's total revenues for one year. As a result of devaluation carried out ten times, the silver content of the kuruş fell by 80 per cent. So one side was finding the money for reform, while the other in the same years was losing the value of its money. ⑦ Two programmes side by side. Egypt: in 1809 students began to be sent to Europe; in 1810 the foundation of the navy was laid at Bulaq; in 1816 a school of geometry; in 1820 the first military school in Aswan and the first printing press in Bulaq; in 1827 a medical school; in 1828 the newspaper Vekāyi-i Mısır (Egyptian Events); in 1834 an engineering school; in 1837 the Ministry of Education (TDV). The Ottomans: the Asâkir-i Mansûre-i Muhammediyye (Victorious Soldiers of Muhammad) army in 1826, the Ministry of Imperial Endowments in 1826, Takvîm-i Vekāyi‘ in 1831, the Mekteb-i Maârif-i Adliyye school, the Military Academy in 1835, the Redif (reserve) organisation, the Supreme Council of Judicial Ordinances (Meclis-i Vâlâ-yı Ahkâm-ı Adliyye), quarantine (TDV). The list is almost the same; the dates are shifted by ten to fifteen years. ⑧ And the proof came on the battlefield. This is the TDV's clearest sentence: the fact that the regular Egyptian forces, trained by modern methods, brought under control in a short time a rebellion which Ottoman forces lacking any discipline and training had been unable to suppress for years had a significant psychological effect, especially on public opinion in Istanbul; the success of the trained Egyptian forces was perceived as a development that restored the reputation of Selim III and acknowledged that he had been right about reform. In other words, Egypt's success was not an obstacle to Ottoman reform but its trigger. ⑨ Mehmed Ali was not an Egyptian nationalist. The TDV is clear on this point: the pasha's cultural outlook was an Ottoman one, the language he spoke was Turkish, and his view of the world was a typically Ottoman view; to regard him as the forerunner of Egyptian nationalism is a mistaken approach. The two modernisations were not two rival civilisational projects but applications of the same tradition on two different scales. ⑩ The short answer. It was not a single thing that held the Ottomans back: ① the economic and social roots of the rival military caste in the capital, ② the alliance of the Janissary corps, the ulema and the ayan, ③ the impossibility of controlling land and taxation on the scale of an empire, ④ public finances that collapsed under war indemnity and currency depreciation, ⑤ and the fact of fighting on four separate fronts in the same years. What Egypt had was a single valley, a single ruler, and a rival whose elimination had been completed in 1811.", "93eb986f"], "kisa": ["The sources do not give a single cause. Mehmed Ali eliminated his rival military caste in 1811, whereas Mahmud II was able to do the same only in 1826 — and the moment he did so, he launched a reform programme very similar to Egypt's. The fifteen years in between are the difference in scale and resistance.", "72be9f4e"], "bag": ["Significance: Here lies the root of the Egyptian crisis stretching from Kütahya to Nizip. When an empire that disbanded its army in 1826 came face to face with a province that had done so in 1811, the fifteen-year gap came back in the form of the defeats at Konya in 1832 and Nizip in 1839.", "a05f091f"]},
"tartisma-bolge0073-timar-dunyadaki-benzerleri":{"baslik": ["Was the timar peculiar to the Ottomans? Six different answers to the same question", "21b16865"], "metin": ["① The source's answer: no, it was not peculiar to them — and the reason is no coincidence. The article on the timar in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam frames the matter as a structural problem: in a state where the cash economy and the central treasury have not developed sufficiently and where the army consists largely of cavalry, a system of assigning revenues inevitably emerges. The cavalryman's needs could be met only in a rural setting, and the tax levied on the produce of the soil could be turned into cash only in the local market. The outcome the source records is that this system prevailed in ancient Iran, in the Byzantine Empire, in Western Europe, in the Islamic states and in the Turco-Mongol states. So the question is not \"who invented it\" but \"how did everyone solve the same problem\". ② Byzantium — the pronoia. The resemblance is not superficial but direct. In the words of the TDV, the Turkish word \"timar\" (care) is the exact equivalent of the Greek word pronoia. That the number of auxiliary troops a holder of a revenue grant had to bring to the battlefield was proportional to the size of the grant is likewise a practice found both in the Ottoman timar and earlier in the Byzantine pronoia. During their conquest of the Balkans the Ottomans let many pronoia holders carry on in their own pronoias as Ottoman timar holders; being a Muslim was not a precondition for being a sipahi (timar-holding cavalryman), and in some sanjaks the proportion of Christian timar holders reached 35 per cent of all sipahis. This shows that the two systems did not merely resemble each other, but that one could be directly converted into the other. ③ The Islamic world — the iqta. Certain basic features of the timar are seen in Iran under Seljuk rule and in Egypt under Mamluk rule; there too the force the holder of an iqta had to bring was proportional to the size of the iqta. Since the Ottomans came into direct contact with the Mamluks, they very probably knew the iqta system. In Seljuk Anatolia there existed appanage-type timars and military assignments resembling the Ottoman timar. ④ Safavid Iran — the tiyul. In the Iranian lands the word tiyul replaced iqta from the fifteenth century onward; it is first seen in texts of the Karakoyunlu period. In the Safavid era tiyul meant the granting to a person of the right to collect taxes in a particular area, and tiyuls took the place of the iqta system. Tiyuls were granted from state (miri) lands, dynastic estates and private properties, and were regulated by the Divan-i A‘la; their holders were emirs, tribal chiefs and army commanders. The point the source stresses is this: although the term was new, the system was a continuation of the tradition of meeting military expenses with land because of a shortage of cash. ⑤ The soyurghal — and here the real distinction appears. The TDV defines the soyurghal in a single sentence: a kind of land-based grant that carried tax immunity and could pass to heirs. Minorsky defines the tiyul as \"authority to collect taxes for a certain period\" and distinguishes it from the hereditary soyurghal. According to Ortaylı, the soyurghal was a privilege lavishly granted to the old ruling class; a state with a weak bureaucracy preferred to ally with the old provincial elite rather than destroy it and create a vacuum of authority. In other words, two separate models stood side by side in the same region: the one that was time-limited and revocable (tiyul), and the one that was permanent and passed on by inheritance (soyurghal). ⑥ India — the jagir. In the Turkish states of India the iqta was mostly known as the \"jagir\". The jagir method, applied from the time of the Delhi sultanates onward, was systematised under the Mughals: lands, towns and villages belonging to the state were given to statesmen and commanders according to their office and rank, in proportion to the salary assessed for them; the official who was given land was obliged to maintain a certain number of soldiers, to join campaigns with them when required and to keep order in his area. This is almost identical to the scheme of obligations of the Ottoman timar. ⑦ Western Europe — and the one sharp difference. Murakami, who has studied the soyurghal, says that for revenue grants of this kind the terms \"fief and lehen\" are used in Western Europe and the term \"iqta\" in the eastern Islamic world. But the TDV article on the timar states plainly the fundamental difference between the Ottoman system and Western feudal practice: the timar did not pass to heirs by inheritance. The law recognised for the son of a deceased sipahi a share on the scale of an initial timar; but the revenue grant itself did not become family property: it reverted to the state and was redistributed. The second difference concerns the reaya (tax-paying subjects): the Ottoman regime was in principle opposed to any personal bond being formed between the military class and the farmer; the sipahi could not force the peasant to work at his own will, nor could he collect more tax than the law code (kanunname) specified; most of the forced labour services inherited from pre-Ottoman times had been converted into obligations subject to a fee. ⑧ A correction: nor did the timar exist throughout the empire. The typical Ottoman province was one where the timar was in force, but the provinces whose revenues were not distributed as timars were salyaneli (provinces on an annual-remittance basis); the list the source gives is this: Egypt, Baghdad, Yemen, Habesh, Basra, al-Ahsa, Algiers, Tripoli and Tunis. So the system was neither peculiar to the Ottomans nor spread across the whole Ottoman realm. ⑨ Scale. According to the budget of 1527-1528, 46 per cent of Rumelia's total revenue of 198.2 million akçe and 56 per cent of Anatolia's 129.6 million akçe were allotted to has, zeamet and timar holdings; there were 17,288 and 16,468 revenue grants in the two provinces respectively. The sipahi class was the basic unit of the Ottoman military system until, during the Austrian wars of 1593-1606, the need for musket-bearing troops made the employment of salaried soldiers unavoidable. ⑩ The end. Attempts at reform (the regulations of 1777 and 1791) produced no result. In 1827 some 5,200 timar-holding sipahis from fifty-three sanjaks in Rumelia and Anatolia were turned, together with their timar revenues, into cavalry of the Asakir-i Mansure (the new army). However, the timar revenues did not cover their livelihood; some had their timars taken away and were put on monthly pay, while those who wished to retire kept their timars untouched in return for the substitute fee for an armed retainer (cebelü bedeli). A four-hundred-year-old backbone thus gave way to a salaried army and cash taxation.", "7f2fd520"], "kisa": ["According to the source, the timar was not an Ottoman invention but the inevitable solution of every state with an undeveloped cash economy and an army based on cavalry. The real question is not whether the system existed but in which detail it diverged — and the divergence turns on a single point: does the revenue grant pass by inheritance?", "9f9ee9f0"], "bag": ["Significance: On the map the timar is not a colour, but it is how the colour was held. The opening of a region to the timar meant sipahis, registers and permanent administration there; remaining salyaneli meant a province far from the centre, held with salaried garrisons. Part of the difference between Algiers breaking away easily in 1830 and Rumelia not breaking away for centuries lies here.", "0728ecb6"]},
"tartisma-celali-sebepleri":{"baslik": ["What gave rise to the Celalis? — there is no single cause, there are eight explanations", "695d7717"], "metin": ["① Safavid incitement and sect: The first wave (Şahkulu, Nur Ali, Sheikh Celal, Kalender Shah) arose from Turkmen groups nourished by the propaganda of Shah Ismail; this explanation is strong for the early sixteenth century and weak for the later great revolts.\n② Population pressure: In the sixteenth century the population of Anatolia grew rapidly, while the cultivated land did not increase at the same pace. Young men who abandoned their farms had to seek a place in a bey's household, in a fortress or in a madrasa; those who could find none were left adrift (the TDV bases this view on M. A. Cook).\n③ Money and prices: In the financial crisis at the end of the century, debased akçes flooded the market, prices rose, and the sale of grain from the coasts to Europe despite the ban heightened the fear of famine.\n④ Oppression by administrators: Provincial posts were obtained for money, and administrators appointed for short terms collected illegal levies (salgun) from the people. According to a couplet of the age, with offices openly on sale it was impossible for Celalis not to appear. The justice decrees (adaletname) issued from 1565 onward were a response to these complaints.\n⑤ The breakdown of the timar order and the 'victims of office': Almost all the great chiefs were officials of the state who had been dismissed or wronged; those whose revenue grants had been taken away because they had fled a campaign or arrived late also joined them.\n⑥ Turkmen reaction against centralising administration: The TDV says that the Celali movement of the sixteenth century can also be read as the resistance of freely living Turkmen communities to the centre's effort to control them.\n⑦ The spread of the musket: After Prince Bayezid's musket-bearing units, firearms multiplied among the people; contemporary documents refer to the musket outright as a 'cause of disorder', and the state forbade the people to carry muskets.\n⑧ The Haçova deserters: Some sources link the revolts to Cigalazade Sinan Pasha's cancellation in 1596 of the revenue grants of those who had fled the battle. The TDV article on the Celali revolts finds this explanation inadequate: the soldiers who lost their grants swelled Karayazıcı's ranks, but the cause must be sought in the masterless levends, the wronged sipahis, the unemployed suhtes and the unprotected peasantry.", "f352ac3b"], "kisa": ["Sect, population, money, oppression, the timar, the musket… The sources bring different causes to the fore; the TDV article openly rejects the most widespread 'single cause' explanation.", "ccffaaef"], "not": ["According to the TDV, apart from a few exceptions, the Celali chiefs did not aim to wrest Anatolia away from the Ottomans; most sought to obtain a sanjak or a beylerbeylik (governorate-general).", "13184f9b"], "bag": ["The explanations do not exclude one another: ①-⑥ describe the ground of the movement, ⑦ and ⑧ the factors that accelerated it. The real debate is which of them weighed most and how this changed from one period to another.", "a0d2d1f6"]},
"tartisma-dunya-hint-okyanusu-portekiz":{"baslik": ["From the Red Sea to the Indian Ocean: why could the Ottomans not drive the Portuguese from Indian waters?", "6b56575d"], "metin": ["① The route changed. Vasco da Gama's rounding of southern Africa and reaching India in 1497 shook the monopoly on trade and pilgrimage routes that the Red Sea had carried for centuries. Using this new route, the Portuguese built forts at the key points of the Indian Ocean: in 1509 they forced the governor of Surat in Gujarat into the treaty they wanted, on 10 August 1511 they took and plundered Malacca, and in 1513 they landed on Dahlak at the entrance to the Red Sea. Malacca, having fallen into Portuguese hands, never regained its former commercial supremacy; Muslim merchants moved to the port of Aceh in Sumatra.\n\n② An inheritance was taken over. When the Ottomans took Egypt in 1517, they also inherited the struggle the Mamluks had begun against the Portuguese. On 18 April 1517 Selman Reis defended Jeddah against a Portuguese fleet of thirty ships; in the report he presented to İbrahim Pasha in 1525, he listed one by one the Portuguese forts at Hormuz, Diu, Goa, Calicut, Cochin, Ceylon and Sumatra and proposed that they be recovered. By taking Aden in 1538, Hadım Süleyman Pasha secured the southern mouth of the Red Sea.\n\n③ Three expeditions on the ocean, three retreats. That same year Hadım Süleyman's fleet crossed to India and besieged the Portuguese fort of Diu; the outer forts fell and the Portuguese withdrew into the inner citadel, but when the support expected from Gujarat did not come, the Ottoman troops withdrew on the night of 6 November 1538, leaving behind some of their heavy guns. In 1552 Piri Reis sailed from Suez and took Muscat, but could not capture the fortress of Hormuz; fearing a surprise attack by a superior Portuguese fleet, he left his main fleet at Basra and returned, and at the end of 1553 he was executed in Egypt. In 1554, while trying to take the ships at Basra to Suez, Seydi Ali Reis twice clashed with a Portuguese fleet beyond Hormuz, was driven by a storm to the coast of Gujarat, lost his ships, and could return to Istanbul only overland, by way of India, Central Asia and Iran.\n\n④ Why? Three causes stand out in the account of the sources. The sea: TDV says that Hadım Süleyman's sailors were 'accustomed to the conditions of the Mediterranean'; they struggled against the monsoon winds. The Portuguese fleet facing Seydi Ali Reis consisted entirely of galleons, whereas the Ottomans put out onto the ocean in oared galleys (kadırga and baştarda). BASES: the Portuguese held permanent, fortified bases at Goa, Diu, Hormuz and Malacca; the Ottoman footholds opening onto the ocean were Suez, Yemen and Basra, and they had no place on the Indian coast to resupply and winter. Allies: the sultans of Gujarat swung back and forth between the two sides — in 1535 Bahadur Shah gave the Portuguese permission to build a fort at Diu, and in 1538 the expected help did not come. In 1567 a defense treaty was made with the Sultanate of Aceh; most of the mission and soldiers sent there settled and mingled with the local people, and Malacca remained Portuguese.\n\n⑤ The outcome — a loss or a boundary? The Ottomans could not win the ocean, but neither did they lose the Red Sea: the Portuguese could not establish themselves at Jeddah or Aden, and the pilgrimage route and Egypt remained secure. As TDV notes, in this new period the Red Sea's character as a trade route weakened, while its character as a pilgrimage route continued. Portugal's loss of Malacca was the work not of the Ottomans but of another European power: in January 1641 the city passed into the hands of the Dutch.", "a26c9b81"], "kisa": ["The Ottomans held the Red Sea and Basra and protected the pilgrimage route; but in no expedition could they take the Portuguese forts at Diu, Hormuz, Goa and Malacca. There was no single cause: a navy built for the Mediterranean, the lack of a base on the Indian coast and unreliable allies.", "56d5bea0"], "bag": ["Significance: the fall of Malacca (1511) is a turning point showing that Asian trade was shifting from the Red Sea to the Cape of Good Hope. The Ottomans stopped this shift in the Red Sea but could not stop it on the ocean — and the cause of that boundary was not a single defeat but the sum of shortcomings in type of fleet, bases and allies.", "38f8dcbc"]},
"tartisma-isyanciya-makam":{"baslik": ["Calming revolt with office — why did the Ottomans make rebels pashas?", "1d3ceb43"], "metin": ["Examples (only what the source relates):\n\n• Karayazıcı Abdülhalim (1600) — when hard pressed at Urfa, he asked for quarter; in return for the promise of the sanjak of Amasya or Antep, he handed over Hüseyin Pasha, who was with him. The promise was not kept and forces marched against him, but under pressure from certain circles in Istanbul he was later given the governorship of the sanjak of Çorum. Troops were sent against him again on the grounds that he was oppressing the people.\n\n• Deli Hasan (1603) — the Celali chief who took Kütahya and marched on Afyonkarahisar was made beylerbey of Bosnia when he asked for pardon. In the source's words, the matter was settled 'amicably'.\n\n• Tavil Ahmed (1605) — following the example of Deli Hasan, he was offered the beylerbeylik of Shahrizor; he did not accept it and besieged Harput.\n\n• Canbolatoğlu Ali Pasha (1606-1607) — to keep him from growing stronger, a diploma appointing him governor of Aleppo was sent to him, but his revolt did not stop. After his defeat he took refuge with the sultan and was pardoned, and received the beylerbeylik of Temeşvar (and then of Belgrade); a short while later he was killed.\n\n• Kalenderoğlu Mehmed and Muslu Çavuş (1607-1608) — Kuyucu Murad Pasha gave one of them the sanjak of Ankara and the other that of İçel. This was not a pardon but a military ruse: to keep them occupied and prevent them from joining forces.\n\n• Gürcü Abdünnebi and Katırcıoğlu Mehmed (1649) — on condition that they disband, one was offered the office of agha of the Turkmens and the other a sanjak, but no agreement was reached. Katırcıoğlu, who asked for pardon after his defeat, was given the sanjak of Beyşehir and later the beylerbeylik of Karaman; he fought against Venice in Crete and fell as a martyr before Candia on 17 December 1668.\n\n• Pazvandoğlu Osman (1799) — the ayan (provincial notable) who defied the state at Vidin received, after the failure of the army sent against him and after the French landing in Egypt, first the rank of kapıcıbaşı (chief gatekeeper) and then, on 21 June 1799, that of vizier. When he did not submit, his vizierate was revoked in November 1800 and restored on conditions in August 1802.", "d36053aa"], "kisa": ["Deli Hasan became beylerbey of Bosnia, Katırcıoğlu beylerbey of Karaman, Pazvandoğlu a vizier. Was the state helpless, or was it acting with calculation?", "25863845"], "not": ["What the examples have in common is that the state was fighting on two fronts at the same time (Austria and Iran; France in 1799). The case of Kalenderoğlu and Muslu Çavuş must be kept separate: there the office was not a compromise but part of the plan of suppression.", "1a050a86"], "bag": ["The TDV article on the Celali revolts weighs both sides of this method together. The gain: pardoned chiefs were mostly given posts on the Rumelian frontier; besieged Anatolian cities were saved, and the forces and supplies to be allotted to the fronts were freed. The price: the state was reluctant to grant horsetail standards and sanjak banners, because very few of those pardoned remained obedient and most carried on their old habits in their new posts; moreover, the chiefs' men were entered in the registers of the standing army (kapıkulu) contrary to the law, and this hastened the deterioration of those corps.", "8185ab6f"]},
"tartisma-kadinlar-saltanati":{"oneri": ["The term was invented in 1916 and was a criticism from the outset; today's scholarship reads the same phenomenon the other way round.", "62fac1d6"], "ad": ["The “Sultanate of Women” — a period or an accusation?", "ba2da7fd"], "metin": ["The name for the period between the end of the 16th century and the middle of the 17th century, in which valide sultans and haseki sultans were markedly influential in state affairs, was given by the book “Kadınlar Saltanatı” (The Sultanate of Women), published by the historian Ahmed Refik Altınay in 1916. The term was born within the historiography of the time of its publication, which read the empire along a line of “stagnation and decline”, and it named the period as one of interference by women and the harem in the government of the state, that is, within a negative framework. TDV's 'Valide Sultan' article also refers by this name to the span between the last quarter of the 16th century and the middle of the 17th century, when valide sultans interfered directly in state affairs, and writes that the period closed in 1656 when Turhan Sultan transferred power to Köprülü Mehmed Pasha. Later academic studies began to question this naming itself; one of the most frequently cited works in this field is Leslie Peirce's book “The Imperial Harem: Women and Sovereignty in the Ottoman Empire” (Oxford University Press, 1993). The question debated in this literature today is not only “did women really rule” but also “why, and in whose eyes, did this period receive this name”. The debate over the phenomenon itself, that is, over the extent to which women ruled, is the subject not of this card but of the “Sultanate of Women” card in the 'Curiosities' section. This distinction is important today too: although in popular usage the term passes as a neutral name for a period, at its origin lies a critical point of view of the early 20th century.", "99c617bb"], "kisa": ["The term was invented in 1916 and was a criticism from the outset; today's scholarship reads the same phenomenon the other way round.", "62fac1d6"], "bag": ["This card tells only the history of the term. For three views on whether the period really was a form of government, see the “Sultanate of Women” card in the 'Curiosities' section.", "29f8f7d5"]},
"tartisma-karadeniz-azak-1696-rus-ordusu-don-kazaklari":{"ad": ["The second Azov campaign (1696): by what route did the Russian army reach Azov, and were the Don Cossacks on the Russian side?", "eb146ecc"], "metin": ["The previous year. The first campaign, in 1695, failed, and the army withdrew on 2 October. Russia thereupon decided to build a fleet: in November 1695 the order for a new campaign was issued, and ships were built at Voronezh, Kozlov, Dobroye and Sokolsk.\n\nTHE MARCH. In 1696 the army marched from Moscow to Voronezh on 8 March and assembled there at the end of March (about 75,000 men, commanded by Shein). Gordon's five regiments boarded river boats on 23 April and went down from Voronezh to Azov; the galley flotilla left Voronezh in May, reached Cherkassk, the centre of the Don Cossacks, on 15 May and came out into the Sea of Azov on 27 May. In other words, the army came to Azov not overland but mainly along the river Don itself. In June, Ukrainian Cossack regiments of about 15,000 men also joined the siege.\n\nTHE DON Cossacks. The Don Cossack Host had been bound to Russia since 1671. The Host is named among the groups that took part in the campaigns of 1695-1696; according to the sources, in May 1696 Cossack boats attacked Ottoman ships off Azov. Since Cherkassk lies about 30 km upstream of Azov, it is not a mistake that the Cossacks appear just beyond Azov: this land belonged not to the Ottomans but to the Don Host, which was bound to Russia.\n\nTHE DAY of surrender. The Russian source puts the surrender of the fortress on 18 July (Old Style), and the Ukrainian encyclopaedia puts the entry into the fortress on 19 July; the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, however, gives the date of its capture as 6 August 1696. The date of 19 July in the chronology is based on the day of entry into the fortress.", "fc8362af"], "kisa": ["In the spring of 1696 the Russian army marched from Moscow to Voronezh and from there went down the Don to Azov in river craft. The Don Cossack Host had been bound to Russia since 1671 and fought on the Russian side in the siege.", "4a37e6f3"]},
"tartisma-karadeniz-cerkezler":{"oneri": ["Before the Russo-Caucasian wars the Circassians lived in an area of more than 100,000 km² stretching from the Sea of Azov to the Caucasus Mountains…", "996b0347"], "ad": ["The Circassians: the emptying of a homeland from the Kuban to the Black Sea coast", "76596807"], "metin": ["According to the article on the Circassians in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, before the Russo-Caucasian wars (1763-1864) began, the Circassians lived in an area of more than 100,000 square kilometres, extending from the Black Sea coast in the west to the Sunzha river in the east, and from the Sea of Azov in the north to the Caucasus Mountains in the south. This area amounted to about a quarter of the Caucasus. The Circassians were divided into two groups: the Western Circassians (the Shapsug, Abzakh, Natukhai, Adamey and other tribes) and the Eastern Circassians (the Kabardians and the Besleney).\n\nIn 1861-1864 the Russians occupied the lands of the Abzakh, Shapsug and Ubykh. The people were told that unless, within one month, they either settled in the places that would be shown to them along the Kuban or emigrated to Ottoman lands, they would be deported to the interior of Russia as prisoners of war. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, many researchers regard this expulsion as the first act of ethnic cleansing in modern history. The number of those expelled and those who emigrated to Ottoman lands between 1858 and 1878 is given as between 1.2 and 1.5 million.\n\nThe result is plainly visible in the census of 1897. Speakers of Circassian made up only 2 per cent of the population in the Kuban Oblast and 3.4 per cent in the Black Sea (Chernomorskaya) Governorate. According to Bilyi, after the Treaty of Adrianople (Edirne) of 1829 the left bank of the Kuban was settled with Cossacks of the Black Sea, Azov and Line Cossack hosts.", "4f8f9dd4"], "kisa": ["Before the Russo-Caucasian wars the Circassians lived in an area of more than 100,000 km² stretching from the Sea of Azov to the Caucasus Mountains. In the census of 1897 the share of Circassian speakers in the Kuban Oblast was 2 per cent.", "7205cadc"], "not": ["When reading the map: the atlas shows the Circassian coast as loosely attached to the Crimea from 1475 onward. This was a legal tie; according to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, until the 18th century the Circassians were legally subject to the Crimea but free in practice. In terms of population, the coast and the mountains remained the Circassian homeland until the expulsion.", "2e9bb1de"]},
"tartisma-karadeniz-etnik-genel":{"oneri": ["In 1500 the steppe was home to Turko-Tatar nomads, the coastal towns to Greek, Armenian and Jewish merchants,…", "7d5a9fde"], "ad": ["Whose homeland was the land north of the Black Sea? Four hundred years of population change on the steppe", "8018313f"], "metin": ["The steppe north of the Black Sea was the homeland not of a single people but of a layered population. Within the Crimean peninsula and on the steppe to its north lived the Crimean Tatars and the Nogai hordes loosely bound to the Crimean khan. The population of the coastal towns, however, was mixed: according to TDV's article on the Crimea, in the tahrir registers of 1542 the population of Caffa was about 16,000 and consisted mostly of Christians. In the towns the Greek, Armenian and Jewish (mostly Karaite) communities together outnumbered the Muslim population.\n\nIn the 16th century two Cossack communities appeared on the northern and eastern edge of the steppe. The Zaporozhian Cossacks lived below the Dnieper rapids, and the Don Cossacks along the lower course of the Don. The mountainous region between the Kuban and the Black Sea coast was the homeland of the Circassians (Adyghe). From the 17th century onwards the Kalmyks led a nomadic life on the steppe between the Don and the Volga.\n\nThis picture changed radically in the last quarter of the 18th century. As a result of the annexation of the Crimea (1783), the dispersal of the Nogai hordes, the migrations of the Crimean Tatars and Circassians to Ottoman lands and the Russian state's opening of the south to planned settlement, in the census of 1897 the majority of the population in all the provinces of this region spoke Ukrainian or Russian.", "591db57b"], "kisa": ["In 1500 the steppe was home to Turko-Tatar nomads, the coastal towns to Greek, Armenian and Jewish merchants, and the Dnieper and Don river lands to the Cossacks. By 1900, Ukrainians and Russians made up the majority of the population in the same lands.", "a3766d9b"], "not": ["When reading the map: what the atlas colours is political ownership, not population. That a region appears in the colour of the Crimean Khanate or of Russia does not tell us who lived there. The cards below separate the two.", "8413e16d"]},
"tartisma-karadeniz-kazaklar":{"oneri": ["'Cossack' was not the name of a nation but of a social estate.", "34c62b32"], "ad": ["Were the Cossacks a people? The origins of the Zaporozhian, Don and Kuban Cossacks", "b47911b8"], "metin": ["The Cossacks were armed communities who settled on the steppe frontier and lived under leaders of their own choosing. According to Brekhunenko's article in the Entsyklopediia istorii Ukrainy, the Don Cossack community was formed at the turn of the 15th and 16th centuries from Muscovite-Ryazan frontier people who came to the Middle and Lower Don, together with Ukrainian, Azov and Horde Cossacks; the society took shape in the middle of the 16th century. According to the same source, people of Muscovite-Ryazan origin predominated on the Middle Don, and those from the Ukrainian lands on the Lower Don. The dividing line ran through the town of Razdory. The Don Cossacks numbered 1,888 in 1613, about 30 thousand at the beginning of the 18th century and 200 thousand at the end of that century.\n\nFrom the end of the 17th century the Don Cossacks spread along left-bank tributaries such as the Medveditsa and the Khoper. On the lower reaches of the right-bank tributaries, however, they were able to settle firmly only in the 1720s. By a decree of 1746 their boundary with the Zaporozhian lands was fixed at the river Kalmius.\n\nThe Zaporozhian Sich on the lower Dnieper was dissolved in 1775. According to the Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, some of the Cossacks crossed into Ottoman lands and founded the Danubian Sich, but most stayed. From these the Russian government organized the Black Sea Cossack Host in 1788. In 1792 Catherine II gave this host about 30,000 km² of land between the Kuban and Yeia rivers; by 1795, 20,000 Cossacks and a number of Russians were living there. According to Bilyi's article on the Kuban, from the end of the 18th century the great majority of the population of the steppe part of the Kuban consisted of Ukrainians. The host was fed by mass migrations from the former Hetmanate and Sloboda Ukraine: 41 thousand people in 1809-11, more than 48 thousand in 1821-25 and about 16 thousand in 1848-50. In 1860 the Kuban Cossack Host was formed from the Black Sea Cossacks and the six western units of the Line Host. According to the IEU, this host comprised 400,000 people in 1860 and 1,392,000 people, families included, in 1912.", "129a40be"], "kisa": ["'Cossack' was not the name of a nation but of a social estate. The Zaporozhian Cossacks were largely Ukrainian, the Don Cossacks were of mixed origin, and the Kuban Cossacks were the descendants of the Zaporozhians.", "bbeda6db"], "not": ["Because the census of 1897 asked about mother tongue, it has no 'Cossack' column. In the Don Host Oblast speakers of Russian made up 66.8 per cent and speakers of Ukrainian 28.1 per cent; in the Kuban Oblast the figures were 47.4 per cent for Ukrainian and 42.6 per cent for Russian. Bilyi, for his part, writes that in 1897 Ukrainians made up 53 per cent in the Kuban and 80 per cent in Chornomoriia, the old homeland of the Black Sea Cossacks. The two sets of proportions were calculated differently; both are given here with their sources.", "d330dff8"]},
"tartisma-karadeniz-kirim-tatar-nogay":{"oneri": ["In the 1770s about nine-tenths of the population of the Crimea were Crimean Tatars.", "fdcee668"], "ad": ["Crimean Tatars and Nogais: from majority to minority on the peninsula", "508e3c93"], "metin": ["The Crimean Tatars were settled in the mountainous south of the peninsula, on its plains and in its towns; in the northern steppe, together with the Nogais, they led a semi-nomadic life. The Nogais were subject to the Crimean khan, but in the words of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam this tie was \"loose\". At the beginning of the 18th century the Yedisan, Jamboyluk, Budjak and Kuban Nogais were under the rule of the Crimean Khanate. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, some of these hordes had moved from the Kuban basin to the north of the Black Sea under Kalmyk pressure.\n\nAccording to the estimates given by the Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, about 400,000 Crimean Tatars lived in the Crimea in 1770, and they made up some 90 per cent of the population. In 1795 their number fell to 137,000 (87.6 per cent); in the census of 1897 they numbered 194,300 and made up 35.6 per cent of the population. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the migrations that continued without interruption for 150 years after the annexation came in mass waves in 1812, 1828-1829, 1860-1861, 1874, 1890 and 1902. In 1860 alone nearly 200,000 Crimeans migrated to Ottoman lands, and from that date on the Crimean Tatars were a minority on the peninsula.\n\nThe fate of the Nogais was harsher still. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, in 1783 forces commanded by General Suvorov and Leontyev inflicted a crushing defeat and a massacre on the Nogais. According to the Entsyklopediia istorii Ukrainy, the Nogai horde that nomadized in the Kuban was partly destroyed and partly deported to the east of the Caucasus governorship. At the end of the 1850s the Nogais of the Kuban, Stavropol and Taurida migrated en masse to Ottoman lands and were settled in places such as Çukurova, Ankara, Konya, Kırşehir and Sivas. In the census of 1897 speakers of Nogai made up only 2.3 per cent of the population of Stavropol Governorate.", "6ff54bfc"], "kisa": ["In the 1770s about nine-tenths of the population of the Crimea were Crimean Tatars. By the census of 1897 this share had fallen to one-third. Waves of migration spanning more than a century explain the difference.", "e060e89f"], "not": ["Treat the figures with care: the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam writes that it is 'estimated' that at least 1,800,000 Crimean Tatars migrated to the Ottoman lands between 1783 and 1922. This total is disputed and cannot be compared directly with the Russian censuses. The figures for 1770 and 1795 given here are estimates provided by the IEU, not census data.", "c9a7ea8f"]},
"tartisma-karadeniz-ruslar-ukraynalilar":{"oneri": ["In 1897 the most widely spoken language in every one of the governorates north of the Black Sea was either Ukrainian or Russian.", "87e9be13"], "ad": ["The population of 'New Russia': Ukrainians, Russians and imported colonists", "c068097f"], "metin": ["The Russian state did not merely conquer the south; it also opened it to settlement. According to the Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine, Empress Elizabeth established New Serbia in 1752 in the northwest of the Zaporozhian lands. The 4,008 Cossack and peasant households there were removed, and in their place Serbian, and later Bulgarian, Wallachian and Greek military colonists were brought from the Habsburg lands. According to the same source, the Orthodox Greeks of the Crimea were resettled on the Azov coast, around present-day Mariupol. Some of them spoke Greek and some Turkish or Crimean Tatar, and they gave their villages the names of their settlements in the Crimea. Armenians were settled on the lower Don, in Nakhichevan near Rostov. At the beginning of the 19th century the government invited Germans, Bulgarians and other colonists to the empty steppes of Southern Ukraine and the Crimea.\n\nAccording to the mother-tongue tables of the 1897 census (as a percentage of each governorate total):\n- Taurida (1,447,790): Ukrainian 42.2 · Russian 27.9 · Tatar 13.6 · German 5.4 · Yiddish 3.8 · Bulgarian 2.8 · Greek 1.2\n- Kherson (2,733,612): Ukrainian 53.5 · Russian 21.0 · Yiddish 11.8 · Moldavian-Romanian 5.4 · German 4.5\n- Yekaterinoslav (2,113,674): Ukrainian 68.9 · Russian 17.3 · Yiddish 4.7 · German 3.8 · Greek 2.3\n- Don Host Oblast (2,564,238): Russian 66.8 · Ukrainian 28.1 · German 1.4 · Kalmyk 1.3 · Armenian 1.1\n- Kuban Oblast (1,918,881): Ukrainian 47.4 · Russian 42.6 · Circassian 2.0 · Karachay 1.4\n- Stavropol (873,301): Russian 55.2 · Ukrainian 36.6 · Nogai 2.3 · Turkmen 1.7 · Kalmyk 1.2\n\nThe general pattern is clear: Ukrainian predominated in the old Zaporozhian and Crimean steppe in the west, and Russian in the Don and Stavropol in the east. The Kuban was divided between the two. The towns, however, were far more mixed: according to the IEU, in Mariupol in 1897 speakers of Russian made up 63 per cent, of Yiddish 15 per cent, of Ukrainian 10 per cent, of Greek 5 per cent and of Turkish 3 per cent.", "fce67984"], "kisa": ["In 1897 the most widely spoken language in every one of the governorates north of the Black Sea was either Ukrainian or Russian. Alongside their speakers, however, there were also German, Jewish, Greek, Bulgarian, Serbian, Moldavian and Armenian communities; most had come at the invitation of the state.", "e9678ce5"], "not": ["The census of 1897 asked not about nationality but about mother tongue; the official name of the 'Ukrainian' column is 'Little Russian' and that of the 'Russian' column 'Great Russian'. For this reason the IEU writes that the official figures for the Crimea are unreliable: according to the same census, those born in the Ukrainian governorates were twice as numerous as speakers of Ukrainian. The IEU's estimate is that there were 180-200 thousand Ukrainians (most of them in the north) and 180-200 thousand Russians (most of them in the towns) on the peninsula.", "538f447b"]},
"tartisma-karadeniz-ukraynalilar-kimdir":{"ad": ["Who are the Ukrainians? The people of the \"borderland\"", "febfd27e"], "metin": ["The meaning of the name. According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the word \"Ukraine\" means \"march, border region\". The history of the country is the shared history of all the states and communities that have lived there; nor would it run counter to the historical evidence to say that its people were born of a mixture of these.\n\nROOTS: The area between the Middle Dnieper, the Pripet, the Carpathians and the Vistula is regarded as the original homeland of the early Slavs. The early history of Ukraine is linked to the Principality of Kyiv, which was destroyed by the Mongol-Tatar invasion in 1240. Earlier, the same steppe had been ruled by the Scythians, Sarmatians, Goths and Huns, followed by the Khazars, Pechenegs, Uzes and Cumans.\n\nDIVERGENCE: After Kyiv broke up, the eastern regions passed to the Golden Horde, while the western principalities passed first to Lithuania (Galicia-Volhynia 1323) and then to Poland (Galicia 1352). After the Union of Lublin (1569), the Polish nobility's imposition of Catholicism on the Orthodox people provoked a reaction: with the founding of the Kyiv Academy and the restoration of the Orthodox church hierarchy (1620), \"the idea of Ukrainianness began to take root.\"\n\nTHE COSSACKS and the Ukrainians: When Poland could not protect the region from Crimean raids, the people set up their own armed organization. The Cossacks, who grew stronger from the 16th century onward under their elected hetmans, made their centre the Sich (Zaporozhian Sich), founded in 1552 at the Dnieper rapids (Porohy). The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam refers to the Cossacks as the force representing Ukraine's national reaction; that is, the Zaporozhian Cossacks were a military and social section of the Ukrainians, not a separate nation. The Don Cossacks, on the other hand, are a separate community (see the card \"Were the Cossacks a people?\").\n\nNAMING: The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam records that the Ukrainians, who belong to the Slavic ethnic group, are also known by the name \"Little Russians\" and that their official language is Ukrainian; in the 2001 census they made up 77.8% of the country's population.", "643e17c8"], "kisa": ["The Ukrainians are an East Slavic people; their name comes from the word Ukraine, meaning \"march, border region\". Their identity was shaped by the legacy of Kyiv, Orthodox resistance under Lithuanian-Polish rule and the Cossack tradition.", "acb78b39"]},
"tartisma-kasrisirin-tarih-yazimi":{"oneri": ["1639 is generally regarded as the date of birth of one of the world's oldest borders.", "0c1b9b6c"], "ad": ["Founding document or founding myth? Two views of Qasr-e Shirin", "ff81c0c1"], "metin": ["The widespread account is this: in 1639 the frontier between the Ottomans and Safavid Iran was drawn, and from that day to this it has stood almost unchanged. In Turkish historiography this account is read together with Murad IV's victories at Yerevan and Baghdad: the sultan recovered Iraq, forced Iran to make peace and permanently secured the eastern frontier. The Ottoman side's information rests largely on the records in Kâtib Çelebi's Fezleke, Naîmâ's History and Feridun Bey's Münşeât. From the Iranian point of view, the same treaty marks the close of a period in which the reign of Shah Safi lost Baghdad as well as Kandahar; the TDV's article on the Safavids mentions it as a treaty that both determined the frontier to a large extent and began a long peace between the two countries. The historian Sabri Ateş objects to this picture. In his view, the idea of a '1639 border' is a founding myth firmly embedded in the historiographies and nationalisms of the countries that share this border. Yet there exist copies of the treaty that differ from one another; it took nearly four centuries for an ill-defined frontier zone to become a clearly defined and controlled line, and only through the work of the mixed commissions between 1843 and 1914 did the line become an internationally recognised border. The two views are not entirely opposed: 1639 really is the document to which all later treaties referred; the debate is about how definite a line that document drew.", "e2b609d1"], "kisa": ["1639 is generally regarded as the date of birth of one of the world's oldest borders. Some historians think this account is too neat.", "3bce2102"], "not": ["Read with care: 'taking a treaty as the basis' and applying every detail of its text exactly are not the same thing. The Kerden and Erzurum negotiations rested on 1639, but which copy the parties read, and how, became a matter of dispute.", "a30010da"]},
"tartisma-kirimrus-carlik-sovyet":{"oneri": ["For a long time Russian historiography portrayed the Crimean Khanate as the continuation of the Golden Horde, a raiding and plundering state…", "39f2a361"], "ad": ["From the 'Tatar yoke' to the 'nest of plunder': the Crimea in Tsarist and Soviet historiography", "285a3f9c"], "metin": ["In Russian historiography Mongol-Tatar rule is referred to as the 'Tatar yoke', and this period is held to have ended on the banks of the Ugra river in 1480. The Crimean khans, however, regarded themselves as the heirs of precisely that Golden Horde. For this reason, in the Russian account the Khanate was easily seen as the 'remnant of the yoke'. As Brian Glyn Williams points out, both Western and Soviet sources for a long time presented the Crimean Tatars as the 'Mongol invaders' of the 13th century. The Tsarist period: according to Johannes Remy, Russia legitimised the annexation of 1783 by a claim to the superiority of European culture and by leaning on the peninsula's past before the Tatars; it was even asserted that the Russians were descendants of the Scythians who had lived in the Crimea in antiquity. The cities were given names of Greek origin: Simferopol, Yevpatoria, Sevastopol, Feodosia. The Soviet period: the account grew harsher still. The Soviet historian P. N. Nadinskii, in his history of the Crimea published in Simferopol in 1951, wrote that the Crimea had been reduced under the wings of the Turkish sultan to a 'nest of plunder', and that the Tatars engaged in peaceful production only little and reluctantly; according to Williams, this thesis indirectly rejected the idea that the Crimean Tatars had a legitimate past of statehood. It must also be remembered that this book was written in the years when the Crimean Tatars had been driven from their homeland: as Remy reports, after the war the Soviet government deported all the Crimean Tatars, about 194,000 people, to Uzbekistan on a charge of collaboration; at least one in five died in the first eighteen months of exile, and Tatar place names were replaced by Russian ones. The Encyclopedia of Ukraine also notes that after the war Soviet researchers concerned themselves only with the Volga-Ural (Kazan) Tatars and ignored the Crimean Tatars. The same pressure was also felt in Soviet Ukrainian historiography: Ocakli shows that the historian Holobuc'kyj, who was obliged to follow the Marxist and Russocentric line, interpreted the events of the 1650s as the khan's fear that Ukraine's 'reunification' with Russia would put an end to the Tatar plundering raids.", "97fd94dc"], "kisa": ["For a long time Russian historiography portrayed the Crimean Khanate as the continuation of the Golden Horde, a raiding and plundering state. This account was used to explain not only the past but also the annexation of 1783 and the deportation of 1944.", "bb340b37"], "not": ["In present-day Russian memory the Crimea is remembered above all for the two great sieges of Sevastopol, that is, for Russian heroism; Remy's title puts these two faces together: heroism and ethnic cleansing.", "a32592b6"]}
});
